(window.COURSE_MODULES = window.COURSE_MODULES || []).push({"id":"S2","code":"Section 2","title":"Computer Networks and Web Technologies","overview":"<p><b>Section 2: Computer Networks and Web Technologies</b> is about how computers and phones connect and talk to each other, from the earbuds paired with your phone to the undersea fibre cables that link Belize to the rest of the world. You will learn the types of networks (LAN, MAN, WAN, PAN, WLAN, client–server and peer-to-peer, Internet, intranet and extranet), the hardware that makes them work (network cards, switches, routers, modems, access points), the media that carry the data (twisted pair, coaxial, fibre-optic and wireless), mobile technologies such as 4G/5G, Bluetooth, NFC and GPS, and the technologies behind the Web: URLs, domain names, IP addresses, DNS, HTML, HTTP and HTTPS, FTP, search engines, e-mail, VoIP and the cloud.</p>\n<p>This matters in real life every single day: when you submit work on Google Classroom or Teams, tap your phone to pay on Albert Street, stream Carnival live or call family overseas on WhatsApp, you are using everything in this section. It matters in the exam too. Networking and web questions appear regularly in <b>Paper 01</b> (multiple choice) and in the structured questions of <b>Paper 02</b>, where command words such as <i>distinguish</i>, <i>explain</i> and <i>evaluate</i> demand precise definitions and scenario-based reasoning. The web concepts here (URLs, hyperlinks, web servers, uploading) also prepare you for the <b>web page component of your SBA</b> in Section 4, where you plan, build, link and publish pages.</p>\n<p>Work through the four units in order: <b>Types of networks</b> → <b>Network components and transmission media</b> → <b>Mobile communication technologies</b> → <b>The Web and web technologies</b>. Use the level dial on every screen: start at Foundation if a topic is new, move to Core for exam standard, and push to Challenge when you are aiming for Grade One. Finish each unit with its structured question, then try the labs (ipconfig and ping in the Windows Lab, designing a network, comparing mobile plans, publishing a website), the 30-question Section Test and the Paper 02 practice question.</p>","objectives":["By the end you will be able to define a computer network and distinguish among LAN, MAN, WAN, PAN and WLAN (SO 2.1).","Compare client–server and peer-to-peer networks and choose one for a given scenario (SO 2.1).","Distinguish among the Internet, an intranet and an extranet, and describe mobile networks and network topologies (SO 2.1).","Explain the functions of a NIC, hub, switch, router, modem and wireless access point, with repeaters and bridges as enrichment (SO 2.2).","Compare transmission media (twisted pair, coaxial, fibre-optic, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, infrared, microwave, satellite), explain bandwidth and distinguish simplex, half-duplex and full-duplex (SO 2.2).","Assess the importance of mobile communication technologies (2G–5G, mobile data, hotspots, Bluetooth, NFC, GPS) for business, education, emergencies and telemedicine, including issues of coverage, cost and security (SO 2.3).","Explain the relationships among key web concepts: Internet vs WWW, web page, website, web server, browser, URL, domain name, IP address, DNS, HTML, HTTP/HTTPS, hyperlink and ISP (SO 2.4).","Describe uploading, downloading, FTP, search engines, e-mail, blogs, wikis, social media, VoIP and cloud computing, with their advantages and disadvantages (SO 2.4)."],"test":{"title":"Section 2 Test","pass":70,"items":[{"q":"A hotel group links the reservation systems of its hotels in Jamaica, Antigua and Barbados so all front desks share one booking database. What type of network is this?","options":["WAN","PAN","MAN","LAN"],"answer":0,"why":"Key: A. The hotels are on different islands, so the network covers a wide area. A LAN covers one site, a MAN one city, and a PAN the space around one person."},{"q":"Jada's smartwatch sends her step count to her phone, which plays music through her Bluetooth earbuds while she jogs along the Roseau boardwalk. These devices form a","options":["MAN","PAN","WAN","LAN"],"answer":1,"why":"Key: B. Devices around one person, linked by Bluetooth within a few metres, form a Personal Area Network. The other options cover much larger areas."},{"q":"At a secondary school, students can log in at any of the 90 computers and find their own saved documents, and the technician backs up all work every night from one machine. Which network model is in use?","options":["PAN","Extranet","Peer-to-peer","Client–server"],"answer":3,"why":"Key: D. Roaming log-ins and central backup depend on a central server. Peer-to-peer has no server; PAN and extranet describe area and access, not this model."},{"q":"A resort in English Harbour lets approved travel agents in the USA log in to its booking system to reserve rooms for their clients. The agents are using the resort's","options":["extranet","Internet","intranet","LAN"],"answer":0,"why":"Key: A. Selected outsiders with controlled log-ins use an extranet. An intranet is for the resort's own staff only; the public Internet requires no special access; a LAN is defined by area."},{"q":"In a computer lab arranged in a star topology, the central switch suddenly fails. What is the MOST likely result?","options":["Only one computer loses its connection","Data is rerouted through the backbone cable","All computers in the lab lose network access","The printer becomes the new switch"],"answer":2,"why":"Key: C. Every device in a star connects through the central switch, so its failure stops the whole network. One computer failing would affect only that computer; rerouting is a mesh feature; a printer cannot act as a switch."},{"q":"A school replaces cables in its library with Wi-Fi so students can use laptops anywhere. Which is a disadvantage of this WLAN compared with the old cabled LAN?","options":["New devices are easier to add","No cables need to be laid","Signals can be intercepted, so strong encryption is needed","Students can move around the library"],"answer":2,"why":"Key: C. Radio signals travel through the air and can be intercepted, so WPA2/WPA3 encryption is needed. The other three options are advantages of a WLAN."},{"q":"An emergency operations centre wants its network links to keep working even if one connection is cut by a falling tree during a hurricane. Which topology BEST meets this need?","options":["Mesh","Ring","Bus","Star"],"answer":0,"why":"Key: A. Mesh provides several routes, so data can avoid a broken link. A bus fails if the backbone breaks, a star if the centre fails, and a single ring if one link breaks."},{"q":"A three-person tour desk at the cruise pier shares one printer and a few files between laptops. They have no IT budget and little technical skill. Which is the MAIN advantage of a peer-to-peer network for them?","options":["Cheap and simple to set up without a dedicated server","Central backup on a server","Scales easily to hundreds of users","Strong central security"],"answer":0,"why":"Key: A. Peer-to-peer uses existing computers and needs no server or specialist. Central security, central backup and scalability are client–server advantages."},{"q":"Ms Hodge is on a call while a bus carries her from San Fernando to Port of Spain. Her phone moves from one cell tower's area to the next without the call dropping. This process is called","options":["roaming","tethering","a handover","pairing"],"answer":2,"why":"Key: C. Passing a call between cells is a handover. Roaming means using another operator's network; tethering shares data; pairing links Bluetooth devices."},{"q":"A lab of 30 PCs connected through an old hub slows down whenever students save work at the same time. The technician replaces the hub with a switch. Why does this help?","options":["A switch sends data only to the port of the intended device","A switch boosts the Wi-Fi signal","A switch connects the lab directly to the Internet","A switch converts digital signals to analogue"],"answer":0,"why":"Key: A. A switch uses MAC addresses to deliver data only where it is needed, reducing collisions. Converting signals is a modem's job, connecting to the Internet is a router's, and boosting is a repeater's."},{"q":"Which device connects the LAN in a Frederick Street jewellery store to its ISP's network and forwards data packets between the two networks?","options":["Network interface card","Switch","Hub","Router"],"answer":3,"why":"Key: D. Routers connect different networks using IP addresses. Switches and hubs connect devices within one LAN; a NIC connects one device."},{"q":"A family in a rural area still connects over a telephone line. Which device converts the computer's digital signals into analogue signals for the line, and back again?","options":["Modem","Switch","Router","Wireless access point"],"answer":0,"why":"Key: A. Modem stands for modulator–demodulator. A router links networks, a switch links LAN devices and a WAP lets wireless devices join a LAN."},{"q":"A school wants students' tablets in every classroom to connect to its existing cabled network. Which device should be installed in each block?","options":["Bridge","Wireless access point","Hub","Modem"],"answer":1,"why":"Key: B. A wireless access point connects wireless devices to a wired LAN. A modem converts signals for an ISP line, a bridge joins LAN segments, and a hub is a wired connector."},{"q":"Kittitian Water &amp; Light company needs a high-bandwidth link to a power station 2 km away, past high-voltage equipment and in an area with frequent lightning. Which medium is MOST suitable?","options":["Bluetooth","Fibre-optic cable","Infrared","Unshielded twisted pair"],"answer":1,"why":"Key: B. Fibre carries light, so it is immune to electrical interference and lightning, and it handles long distances at high bandwidth. UTP is limited to about 100 m and affected by interference; infrared and Bluetooth have tiny ranges."},{"q":"A cruise ship sailing between Haiti and Barbados wants to offer Internet access to passengers while far from land. Which transmission medium makes this possible?","options":["Twisted pair","Coaxial cable","Infrared","Satellite"],"answer":3,"why":"Key: D. Only satellite covers ships in the open ocean. Cables cannot reach a moving ship, and infrared works only over a few metres."},{"q":"Andre downloads a 500 MB video for his SBA on a 25 Mbps connection. Ignoring overheads, how long will the download take?","options":["40 seconds","160 seconds","125 seconds","20 seconds"],"answer":1,"why":"Key: B. 500 MB × 8 = 4,000 Mb; 4,000 ÷ 25 = 160 s. 20 s comes from forgetting to multiply by 8; the other values come from arithmetic slips."},{"q":"Security officers at Carnival Village use two-way radios. Only one person can speak at a time and each says 'Over' when finished. Which transmission mode is this?","options":["Simplex","Broadcast-only","Half-duplex","Full-duplex"],"answer":2,"why":"Key: C. Both directions, but one at a time, is half-duplex. Simplex is one-way only; full-duplex allows both at once, as in a phone call."},{"q":"Rohan's TV remote stops working whenever his little brother sits between the remote and the TV. Which medium does the remote MOST likely use?","options":["Satellite","Infrared","Bluetooth","Wi-Fi"],"answer":1,"why":"Key: B. Infrared needs a clear line of sight and is blocked by people and objects. Bluetooth and Wi-Fi radio pass around and through obstacles; satellites are not used by remotes."},{"q":"An engineer in Paramaribo wants to control a port crane remotely in real time, with almost no delay between her command and the crane's movement. Which feature of 5G is MOST important here?","options":["Very low latency","Infrared support","Longer battery life of 2G phones","SMS text messaging"],"answer":0,"why":"Key: A. Low latency is what allows real-time remote control. SMS arrived with 2G; infrared and 2G batteries are irrelevant."},{"q":"At a store on Market Street, Tamara holds her phone about 2 cm from the card terminal and the payment is approved after she scans her fingerprint. Which technology sent the payment details?","options":["Infrared","NFC","GPS","Bluetooth"],"answer":1,"why":"Key: B. Tap-to-pay over a few centimetres with no pairing is Near Field Communication. GPS gives location, Bluetooth needs pairing over about 10 m, and infrared is used by remotes."},{"q":"Kemar is hiking where there is no mobile signal, yet his phone still shows his exact position on a map he downloaded earlier. Why is this possible?","options":["NFC tags along the trail send his position","The phone guesses from his last Wi-Fi network","The GPS receiver uses satellite signals and does not need mobile data","The phone uses Bluetooth from nearby hikers"],"answer":2,"why":"Key: C. GPS is receive-only from satellites; the downloaded map provides the background. Bluetooth and NFC do not provide location in this way, and guessing would not be exact."},{"q":"Dante's laptop has no Internet connection, but his phone has a strong 4G signal. He turns on a setting so the laptop can use the phone's data over Wi-Fi. This is called","options":["a personal hotspot (tethering)","pairing","a handover","roaming"],"answer":0,"why":"Key: A. Sharing a phone's mobile data with other devices is tethering via a personal hotspot. Roaming uses another operator's network; pairing is Bluetooth; handover is moving between cells."},{"q":"At Douglas–Charles Airport, Rhea sees two networks: the airport's official one and 'Free_Airport_WiFi2' with no password. What is the MAIN risk of logging in to her bank on the second network?","options":["Bank apps cannot work on any Wi-Fi","Her battery will drain instantly","It may be a fake (evil twin) hotspot used to capture her data","It will use up her mobile data bundle"],"answer":2,"why":"Key: C. An open hotspot imitating the real network can be used to intercept log-ins. Battery and data use are not the main risk, and bank apps do work on Wi-Fi."},{"q":"Mr Lake, who lives in Dutch Quarter, sends his daily blood-pressure readings from a home monitor through his phone to a heart specialist overseas and has monthly video consultations. This is an example of","options":["telemedicine","e-commerce","cloud gaming","roaming"],"answer":0,"why":"Key: A. Health care delivered at a distance using telecommunications is telemedicine. The other options are unrelated uses."},{"q":"Kayla says that a WhatsApp video call to her cousin in Antigua and reading the CXC website are both uses of the World Wide Web. Which statement is correct?","options":["Only the video call uses the Internet","Neither uses the Internet","Both are part of the World Wide Web","Both use the Internet, but only the website is part of the World Wide Web"],"answer":3,"why":"Key: D. The Web is linked pages accessed with HTTP; the call is a VoIP service that uses the Internet but not the Web. The other options misunderstand the Internet–Web relationship."},{"q":"Keisha is booking a snorkelling trip on the site https://www.tobagotours.co.tt/trips/reef.html and wants to know which country's domain it uses. Which part of the URL is the top-level domain?","options":["https",".tt","/trips/","reef.html"],"answer":1,"why":"Key: B. The TLD is the last part of the domain name: .tt (Trinidad and Tobago). https is the protocol, /trips/ the path and reef.html the file name."},{"q":"Ashley types www.coralreefcafe.gd into her browser. Before the page can load, which system converts that name into the web server's IP address?","options":["DNS","VoIP","HTML","FTP"],"answer":0,"why":"Key: A. The Domain Name System translates domain names into IP addresses. HTML writes pages, FTP transfers files and VoIP carries calls."},{"q":"Ms Hodge is about to pay school fees online. The address starts with https:// and shows a padlock. What does the HTTPS protocol guarantee?","options":["The site contains no errors","The page will load faster","Data sent between her browser and the server is encrypted","The organisation running the site is honest"],"answer":2,"why":"Key: C. HTTPS encrypts data in transit. It does not prove a site is honest (scammers can use HTTPS), error-free or faster."},{"q":"A web designer finishes a website for a soursop shop on her laptop. To make it available to the world, she logs in to the hosting server and transfers the files. What is she doing, and with which protocol?","options":["Downloading, using HTTP","Uploading, using DNS","Downloading, using VoIP","Uploading, using FTP/SFTP"],"answer":3,"why":"Key: D. Sending files from her laptop to the server is uploading, and FTP/SFTP is the file transfer protocol. DNS translates names, VoIP carries calls, and downloading is the opposite direction."},{"q":"A small business in Holetown stores all its records in cloud storage. During a two-day Internet outage after a storm, what problem is it MOST likely to face?","options":["Its records are permanently deleted","Staff cannot access the records until the connection returns","Its computers stop turning on","The cloud provider loses all data on the island"],"answer":1,"why":"Key: B. Cloud data is safe off-site but needs an Internet connection to reach it. An outage does not delete cloud data, stop computers starting or affect data held overseas."}]},"paper2":{"type":"write","title":"Paper 02 practice: Seaside Medical Centre","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Structured question: networks, mobile and web technologies (15 marks)","stimulus":"<p><b>Seaside Medical Centre</b> in Santiago de los Caballeros has 20 computers, a server holding patient records and a shared printer. It is opening a second clinic in San Pedro de Macorís and wants both sites to share the same records. Doctors visit elderly patients at home using tablets on 4G/5G. The centre's new patient portal at <code>https://portal.seasidemedical.do/appointments/book.html</code> lets patients book appointments; only staff can see the internal staff-policy pages.</p>","prompt":"<p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Distinguish</b> between a LAN and a WAN, and <b>state</b> which network is needed to link the two clinics. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> the function of a router and of a switch in the Santiago de los Caballeros clinic. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> why the centre should use a client–server network rather than peer-to-peer. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Identify</b> TWO mobile technologies that help doctors on home visits, and <b>explain</b> how each helps. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Identify</b> the protocol and the top-level domain in the portal's URL, and <b>explain</b> why the protocol is important for patients. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>What term describes the staff-only policy pages? <b>Justify</b> your answer. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":120,"max":480,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> A LAN covers ... whereas a WAN ... The clinics need a ...<br><b>(b)</b> A router ... A switch ...<br><b>(c)</b> Client–server is better because ...<br><b>(d)</b> First, ... helps doctors by ... Second, ...<br><b>(e)</b> The protocol is ... and the TLD is ... This matters because ...<br><b>(f)</b> They are an ... because ...</p>","checklist":["LAN vs WAN compared on the same point; WAN (or MAN) chosen to link the clinics.","Router = connects networks; switch = connects devices in the LAN, forwarding to the right one.","Client–server linked to confidential records, central security and backup.","Two named mobile technologies (e.g. 4G/5G data, GPS, Bluetooth) each with how it helps.","https and .do identified; encryption of patient data explained.","Intranet named and justified by staff-only access."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"LAN vs WAN","marks":3,"look":["\\bLAN\\b|local area","\\bWAN\\b|wide area|\\bMAN\\b","whereas|while|but|compared|however","small|one (building|site)|large|countr|island|distance"],"min":3,"strong":"Clear distinction and correct choice.","weak":"Compare area in one sentence and state that a WAN (or MAN, since both clinics are on one island) links them."},{"criterion":"Router and switch","marks":2,"look":["router.{0,80}(network|internet|forward|route|ip)","switch.{0,80}(device|computer|destination|intended|mac|lan)"],"min":2,"strong":"Both functions correct.","weak":"Router connects networks; switch connects devices in the LAN and sends data to the right one."},{"criterion":"Client–server","marks":2,"look":["server","secur|backup|back up|central|confidential|access"],"min":2,"strong":"Reasons linked to records.","weak":"Central server gives secure, controlled access and central backup of confidential patient records."},{"criterion":"Mobile technologies","marks":4,"look":["4g|5g|mobile data|cellular","gps|bluetooth|nfc|hotspot","record|access|navigat|find|locat|upload|monitor|video","because|so that|which|allows|lets|helps"],"min":3,"strong":"Two technologies with explanations.","weak":"E.g. 4G/5G data lets doctors access records and video-call specialists; GPS lets them find patients' homes."},{"criterion":"URL and HTTPS","marks":2,"look":["https","\\.do\\b","encrypt|secure|privacy|intercept"],"min":2,"strong":"Parts and importance.","weak":"https and .do; HTTPS encrypts patients' personal and medical data in transit."},{"criterion":"Intranet","marks":2,"look":["intranet","staff|employee|member|private|only"],"min":2,"strong":"Named and justified.","weak":"An intranet: private pages only staff (members of the organisation) can access."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> A LAN covers a small area such as one building and is usually owned by one organisation, whereas a WAN covers a large geographical area, linking networks that are far apart. The two clinics are at different sites, so their LANs must be joined into a wider network: a WAN (or, since both are on one island, a MAN), for example through a secure VPN link over the Internet.</p><p><b>(b)</b> The router connects the clinic's LAN to other networks, such as the Internet and the San Pedro de Macorís clinic, forwarding data packets between them. The switch connects the 20 computers, server and printer within the LAN, sending data only to the device it is addressed to.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Patient records are confidential, so a central server lets the centre control exactly who can access them, keep audit logs and back them up every night in one place. Peer-to-peer would leave records spread across PCs with weak security and no central backup.</p><p><b>(d)</b> First, 4G/5G mobile data lets the doctor's tablet reach the patient-records server securely from the patient's home, so the doctor can check history and update notes immediately. Second, GPS lets the doctor navigate quickly to patients' homes, even in unfamiliar areas, so less time is wasted travelling.</p><p><b>(e)</b> The protocol is HTTPS and the top-level domain is .do. HTTPS encrypts the data travelling between the patient's browser and the server, so personal and medical details cannot be read if intercepted.</p><p><b>(f)</b> An intranet, because the pages are private to the organisation and can only be accessed by its own staff after logging in, not by patients or the public.</p>","key":"<p>(a) 1 LAN description; 1 WAN description in contrast; 1 WAN/MAN chosen for the link. (b) 1 router function; 1 switch function. (c) 1 central server; 1 security/backup reason linked to records. (d) For each of two technologies: 1 named, 1 explained. (e) 1 https and .do both correct; 1 encryption/privacy explanation. (f) 1 intranet; 1 justification (staff/members only).</p>","teacherTip":"Accept MAN in (a) if justified by both sites being on one island. In (d), do not credit 'Wi-Fi' for home visits unless the student explains hotspot/tethering; GPS, Bluetooth links to medical devices and mobile data are the strongest answers.","easy":{"title":"Paper 02 practice: Seaside Medical Centre (Foundation)","genre":"Structured question: networks, mobile and web technologies (9 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Is the network inside the Santiago de los Caballeros clinic a LAN or a WAN? <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li><b>State</b> what a router does. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li><b>State</b> ONE reason the centre should use a server. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> TWO mobile technologies doctors can use on home visits. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>From the URL, <b>write down</b> the protocol and the top-level domain. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Why should the portal use HTTPS? <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>Are the staff-only pages an intranet or an extranet? <i>(1 mark)</i></li></ol>","frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> It is a ...<br><b>(b)</b> A router connects ...<br><b>(c)</b> A server ...<br><b>(d)</b> ... and ...<br><b>(e)</b> Protocol: ... TLD: ...<br><b>(f)</b> HTTPS ...<br><b>(g)</b> ...</p>","min":30,"max":240,"checklist":["LAN.","Router connects networks.","One server reason.","Two mobile technologies.","https and .do.","HTTPS encrypts.","Intranet."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"LAN","marks":1,"look":["\\bLAN\\b|local"],"min":1,"strong":"Correct.","weak":"LAN."},{"criterion":"Router","marks":1,"look":["network|internet"],"min":1,"strong":"Correct.","weak":"Connects the clinic's network to other networks/the Internet."},{"criterion":"Server reason","marks":1,"look":["secur|backup|back up|central|record"],"min":1,"strong":"Correct.","weak":"Keeps records secure and backed up in one place."},{"criterion":"Mobile technologies","marks":2,"look":["4g|5g|mobile data|cellular|gps","gps|bluetooth|nfc|hotspot|4g|5g"],"min":2,"strong":"Two named.","weak":"E.g. 4G/5G and GPS."},{"criterion":"URL parts","marks":2,"look":["https","\\.do\\b"],"min":2,"strong":"Both correct.","weak":"https and .do."},{"criterion":"HTTPS reason","marks":1,"look":["encrypt|secure|safe|privacy"],"min":1,"strong":"Correct.","weak":"It encrypts patients' data."},{"criterion":"Intranet","marks":1,"look":["intranet"],"min":1,"strong":"Correct.","weak":"Intranet."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> A LAN.</p><p><b>(b)</b> A router connects the clinic's network to other networks such as the Internet.</p><p><b>(c)</b> A server keeps patient records secure and backed up in one place.</p><p><b>(d)</b> 4G/5G mobile data and GPS.</p><p><b>(e)</b> Protocol: https. TLD: .do.</p><p><b>(f)</b> HTTPS encrypts patients' personal data so nobody can read it if it is intercepted.</p><p><b>(g)</b> An intranet.</p>","key":"<p>(a) 1 LAN. (b) 1 connects the clinic's network to other networks such as the Internet. (c) 1 keeps patient records secure and/or backed up in one central place. (d) 1 mark each for two mobile technologies (4G/5G mobile data, GPS, Bluetooth, phone hotspot). (e) 1 https; 1 .do. (f) 1 it encrypts patients' data so it cannot be read if intercepted. (g) 1 intranet.</p>","teacherTip":"In (d), do not credit 'Wi-Fi' for home visits unless the student explains a phone hotspot; 4G/5G mobile data and GPS are the strongest answers, and two marks need two different technologies. In (e), the top-level domain is .do, not seasidemedical.do. In (g), the staff-only pages are an intranet; watch for the 'Internet' misspelling."},"up":{"title":"Paper 02 practice: Seaside Medical Centre (Challenge)","genre":"Structured question: networks, mobile and web technologies with evaluation (20 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Distinguish</b> between a LAN and a WAN, and <b>recommend</b> how the two clinics should be linked securely. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> the functions of a router, a switch and a wireless access point in the Santiago de los Caballeros clinic. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Recommend</b> a transmission medium for linking the server room to a new X-ray building 300 m away, and <b>justify</b> it. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Assess</b> the use of mobile technology for home visits, considering benefits, coverage and security. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>A patient types the portal's domain into a browser. <b>Explain</b> how DNS and HTTPS work together to load the page securely. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>The centre plans to move patient records to the cloud. <b>Evaluate</b> this plan for a clinic in a hurricane zone, and <b>recommend</b> ONE safeguard. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":220,"max":700,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> ... whereas ... I recommend ... because ...<br><b>(c)</b> I recommend ... because twisted pair ...<br><b>(d)</b> Mobile technology helps ... However ... Overall ...<br><b>(e)</b> First DNS ... then HTTPS ...<br><b>(f)</b> On one hand ... On the other hand ... I recommend ...</p>","checklist":["LAN/WAN distinction with a secure link (e.g. VPN/leased line).","Three device functions.","Fibre (or justified wireless bridge) for 300 m with reasons.","Balanced assessment of mobile tech: benefits, coverage AND security, with a judgement.","DNS → IP → encrypted HTTPS connection explained in order.","Cloud evaluated (off-site resilience vs Internet dependence/privacy) with a safeguard."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"LAN vs WAN and secure link","marks":3,"look":["\\bLAN\\b|local area","\\bWAN\\b|wide area|\\bMAN\\b","vpn|encrypt|leased|secure"],"min":3,"strong":"Distinction and secure recommendation.","weak":"Compare LAN/WAN and recommend a VPN or leased link."},{"criterion":"Device functions","marks":3,"look":["router.{0,80}(network|internet|forward|route)","switch.{0,80}(device|destination|intended|mac)","access point.{0,80}(wireless|wi-?fi|tablet)|wap.{0,60}(wireless|wi-?fi)"],"min":3,"strong":"All three functions.","weak":"Router connects networks; switch forwards within LAN; WAP connects wireless devices."},{"criterion":"Medium for 300 m","marks":3,"look":["fib(re|er)","100 ?m|distance|far|300","bandwidth|interference|image|x-?ray|large|secur"],"min":3,"strong":"Justified choice.","weak":"Fibre: beyond twisted pair's 100 m limit; high bandwidth for large X-ray images; immune to interference."},{"criterion":"Assess mobile tech","marks":4,"look":["record|access|video|gps|navigat|monitor","coverage|signal|dead spot","secur|lost|stolen|encrypt|lock|wipe","overall|on balance|therefore|conclu"],"min":4,"strong":"Balanced judgement.","weak":"Benefits, coverage issues and security risks with mitigations, then a conclusion."},{"criterion":"DNS and HTTPS","marks":3,"look":["\\bDNS\\b","ip","encrypt|tls|https|secure"],"min":3,"strong":"Sequence explained.","weak":"DNS returns the server's IP; browser opens an encrypted HTTPS connection; page delivered securely."},{"criterion":"Cloud evaluation","marks":4,"look":["off.?site|backup|hurricane|survive|resilien","internet|connection|outage|privacy|confidential|third","recommend|safeguard|should","because|so that|which"],"min":3,"strong":"Evaluated with a safeguard.","weak":"Weigh off-site resilience against Internet dependence and confidentiality; recommend a backup link, encryption or local copies."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> A LAN covers one site and is owned by one organisation, whereas a WAN links networks over a large area using leased or public links. The clinics should join their LANs through a secure, encrypted VPN over the Internet (or a leased line), so patient records cross the link safely.</p><p><b>(b)</b> The router connects the clinic LAN to the Internet and the San Pedro de Macorís clinic, forwarding packets using IP addresses. The switch connects computers, server and printer, forwarding data only to the intended device. The wireless access point lets doctors' tablets and other Wi-Fi devices join the LAN.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Fibre-optic cable: 300 m is three times the ~100 m limit of twisted pair; fibre carries large X-ray images at very high bandwidth and is immune to electrical interference from medical equipment and lightning.</p><p><b>(d)</b> Mobile data lets doctors view and update records at the patient's home and video-call specialists, and GPS speeds travel, improving care for elderly patients. However, hilly areas may have dead spots, so the app should store notes offline and sync later; and a lost tablet could expose confidential records, so tablets need encryption, screen locks and remote wipe. Overall, mobile technology is highly valuable for home visits provided these measures are in place.</p><p><b>(e)</b> The browser asks a DNS server to translate portal.seasidemedical.do into the server's IP address. It then contacts that IP address and sets up an HTTPS connection, which uses the site's certificate and TLS to encrypt everything exchanged, so the patient's booking details are protected in transit.</p><p><b>(f)</b> Storing records in the cloud means they are kept off-site, so a hurricane that floods the Santiago de los Caballeros server room would not destroy them, and both clinics could reach them. On the other hand, staff cannot access records without an Internet connection, and confidential data is held by a third party. I recommend a backup Internet link (mobile or satellite) so records stay reachable during outages, with the records encrypted and the provider meeting data-protection requirements.</p>","key":"<p>(a) 1 LAN covers one site and is owned by one organisation; 1 WAN links networks over a large area using leased or public links, given as a contrast; 1 a secure link recommended with a reason (encrypted VPN over the Internet, or a leased line; accept MAN if justified). (b) 1 mark each: router connects the clinic LAN to the Internet/San Pedro de Macorís clinic and forwards packets by IP address; switch connects the computers, server and printer and forwards data only to the intended device; wireless access point lets tablets and other Wi-Fi devices join the LAN. (c) 1 fibre-optic cable (or a justified wireless bridge); 2 justifications from: beyond twisted pair's 100 m limit; high bandwidth for large X-ray images; immune to electrical interference and lightning; hard to tap. (d) 1 benefits explained (records at the patient's home, video calls, GPS navigation); 1 coverage problem with a mitigation (offline notes that sync later); 1 security risk with a mitigation (encryption, screen lock, remote wipe); 1 overall judgement. (e) 1 DNS translates portal.seasidemedical.do into the server's IP address; 1 the browser connects to that IP and sets up HTTPS using the site's certificate/TLS; 1 everything exchanged is encrypted, protecting patients' details in transit. (f) 1 benefit: records off-site survive a hurricane and both clinics can reach them; 1 drawback: no access without the Internet, or confidential data held by a third party; 1 safeguard recommended; 1 safeguard justified (how it reduces the drawback).</p>","teacherTip":"In (a), accept a MAN for the two-clinic link if the student justifies it, but the third mark needs the security idea (VPN, encryption or a leased line). In (c), reject Cat6 for 300 m unless switches or repeaters along the run are described. Cap one-sided answers: (d) needs benefits, coverage AND security before the judgement, and (f) needs a drawback as well as the hurricane benefit before the safeguard is credited."}},"assignments":[{"id":"S2-A1","level":1,"bloom":"Understand","title":"Network detective poster","brief":"<p>Your school's IT club wants a poster for the computer lab showing how a network gets data from a student's computer to the Internet. Investigate the lab (with your teacher's permission) or your home network and make a labelled poster.</p>","tasks":["Identify at least FIVE network components you can see or that must exist (e.g. NIC, cable, switch, router, wireless access point, modem/ONT).","Draw a clear diagram showing how they connect from one computer to the Internet.","Under each component, write its function in ONE sentence beginning with a verb (e.g. 'Connects…', 'Forwards…').","Label the type of network (LAN/WLAN) and one transmission medium used.","Add one safety or security tip for the network (e.g. strong Wi-Fi password)."],"deliverable":"One A3 poster (paper or digital, e.g. Canva/Publisher), plus a photo or PDF uploaded to Google Classroom/Teams.","rubric":[{"criterion":"Components identified and correctly labelled","marks":5,"descriptor":"At least five correct components, each correctly named."},{"criterion":"Functions accurate","marks":5,"descriptor":"Each function is correct, concise and starts with a verb."},{"criterion":"Diagram shows the data path","marks":4,"descriptor":"Logical order from device to Internet with clear connections."},{"criterion":"Network type, medium and tip","marks":3,"descriptor":"Correct network type, a medium and a sensible security tip."},{"criterion":"Design and clarity","marks":3,"descriptor":"Readable fonts, consistent colours, uncluttered layout."}],"time":40,"sim":"pcbuild"},{"id":"S2-A2","level":1,"bloom":"Remember","title":"Flashcards and quiz duel (pairs)","brief":"<p>With a partner, build a revision set for Section 2 that the whole class can use before the Paper 01 exam.</p>","tasks":["Choose 20 terms from the Section 2 glossary, covering all four units.","Make a flashcard for each: term on the front; definition and a Caribbean example on the back.","Write 10 multiple-choice questions, each with a short scenario, four options and the correct answer.","Swap quizzes with another pair, answer theirs and mark them using their key.","Write three sentences on which terms your pair found hardest and why."],"deliverable":"20 flashcards (paper or digital, e.g. Quizlet) and a 10-item quiz with answer key, submitted as one file.","rubric":[{"criterion":"Flashcard accuracy","marks":6,"descriptor":"All definitions correct and in your own words."},{"criterion":"Caribbean examples","marks":4,"descriptor":"Relevant local examples on most cards."},{"criterion":"Quiz quality","marks":6,"descriptor":"Each item has a scenario, one clearly correct answer and plausible distractors."},{"criterion":"Reflection and teamwork","marks":4,"descriptor":"Honest reflection; both partners contributed."}],"time":45,"sim":"racer"},{"id":"S2-A3","level":2,"bloom":"Apply","title":"Network proposal for West Bay Auto Parts","brief":"<p><b>West Bay Auto Parts</b> has a shop with 8 computers and a warehouse next door where staff use handheld scanners. It wants to share a stock database, a printer and the Internet, and to let its supplier in Puerto Rico check stock levels. Write a proposal for the owner.</p>","tasks":["State and justify the type of network (by area) and the network model (client–server or peer-to-peer).","Draw a network diagram showing all components and the topology.","List each component (NIC, switch, router, modem/ONT, WAP) with its function and why it is needed here.","Recommend transmission media for the shop, the warehouse and the link to the ISP, with reasons.","Explain how the supplier will be given access (intranet/extranet) and one way to keep it secure.","Include a simple table of estimated costs using illustrative prices you research online."],"deliverable":"A word-processed proposal of 500–800 words with a labelled diagram and cost table (PDF or DOCX).","rubric":[{"criterion":"Network type and model justified","marks":4,"descriptor":"Correct choices with reasons tied to the scenario."},{"criterion":"Diagram","marks":4,"descriptor":"Accurate, labelled, shows topology and all devices."},{"criterion":"Components and functions","marks":6,"descriptor":"Every component correctly explained and justified."},{"criterion":"Transmission media","marks":4,"descriptor":"Suitable media for each link with reasons."},{"criterion":"Extranet and security","marks":4,"descriptor":"Correct term and a realistic security measure."},{"criterion":"Cost table and presentation","marks":3,"descriptor":"Clear table; professional formatting; sources noted."}],"time":90,"sim":"winsim"},{"id":"S2-A4","level":2,"bloom":"Analyse","title":"Mobile life survey (group research)","brief":"<p>How do people in your community really use mobile technology? In groups of 3–4, survey people (classmates, family, a local business owner) and analyse the results.</p>","tasks":["Design a 10-question survey covering devices owned, mobile data vs Wi-Fi use, NFC/contactless payment, GPS apps, hotspot use, coverage problems and security habits.","Collect at least 20 responses (no names or personal data).","Enter the data into a spreadsheet and create at least TWO suitable charts.","Analyse: identify three patterns and compare different age groups or users.","Interview one local business owner about how mobile technology affects their business.","Present findings as an infographic with a short conclusion on the importance of mobile technology locally."],"deliverable":"An infographic (1 page) plus the spreadsheet file, submitted by one group member; each member submits a 100-word individual reflection.","rubric":[{"criterion":"Survey design and ethics","marks":4,"descriptor":"Clear, unbiased questions; no personal data collected."},{"criterion":"Data handling and charts","marks":5,"descriptor":"Accurate spreadsheet; appropriate, labelled charts."},{"criterion":"Analysis","marks":6,"descriptor":"Three valid patterns, supported by the data."},{"criterion":"Business interview","marks":3,"descriptor":"Relevant insights linked to SO 2.3."},{"criterion":"Infographic and conclusion","marks":4,"descriptor":"Clear design and a reasoned judgement."},{"criterion":"Individual reflection","marks":3,"descriptor":"Specific contribution and learning described."}],"time":120},{"id":"S2-A5","level":3,"bloom":"Evaluate","title":"Podcast: Is 5G worth it for a small island?","brief":"<p>A youth radio show wants a 5-minute podcast episode debating whether a small Caribbean island should invest heavily in 5G now. In groups of 3, plan and script the episode.</p>","tasks":["Research 5G's features (speed, latency, device density) and how it differs from 4G.","Prepare arguments FOR (business, tourism, telemedicine, smart sensors for flooding) and AGAINST (cost, small population, coverage on hilly terrain, security, digital divide).","Write a script with a host and two guests holding opposing views, including at least two but realistic local scenarios.","End with the host's balanced conclusion and a recommendation with justification.","Optional: record the episode (audio only) and edit it."],"deliverable":"A podcast script of 700–900 words (or a recorded MP3 under 6 minutes plus the script) and a source list.","rubric":[{"criterion":"Technical accuracy","marks":5,"descriptor":"5G and 4G features described correctly; no invented statistics about real companies."},{"criterion":"Balance of arguments","marks":6,"descriptor":"Strong points on both sides, linked to Caribbean contexts."},{"criterion":"Evaluation and conclusion","marks":5,"descriptor":"Clear, justified judgement that weighs the arguments."},{"criterion":"Script/production quality","marks":4,"descriptor":"Engaging, well-structured, suitable for the audience."}],"time":120},{"id":"S2-A6","level":3,"bloom":"Create","title":"Build a mini-site: How the Web works","brief":"<p>Create a three-page website that explains to Form 3 students what happens when you type a web address and press Enter. Use the Web Page Builder or a web authoring tool.</p>","tasks":["Plan a site map and a simple wireframe for three pages: Home, 'From name to page' and 'Stay safe online'.","On 'From name to page', explain URLs (with a labelled example), DNS, IP addresses and HTTP/HTTPS in a step-by-step sequence.","On 'Stay safe online', explain the padlock, why it does not prove a site is honest, and how to read a domain from the right.","Include a navigation bar on every page, at least one image with alt text and the four hyperlink types: to another page, to a bookmark on the same page, to an e-mail address and to a downloadable file.","Publish the site using the FTP step in the Web Page Builder and record the URL.","Ask two classmates to test it and write a short evaluation (accuracy, navigation, load time, display on a phone)."],"deliverable":"The published mini-site (URL or exported folder), the site map/wireframe, and a 200-word evaluation.","rubric":[{"criterion":"Planning (site map, wireframe)","marks":3,"descriptor":"Clear plan matching the finished site."},{"criterion":"Accuracy of explanations","marks":6,"descriptor":"URL, DNS, IP, HTTP/HTTPS explained correctly and simply."},{"criterion":"Hyperlinks and navigation","marks":5,"descriptor":"All four hyperlink types work; consistent navigation."},{"criterion":"Design and accessibility","marks":3,"descriptor":"Readable, consistent, alt text on images."},{"criterion":"Publishing","marks":2,"descriptor":"Site uploaded successfully."},{"criterion":"Evaluation","marks":3,"descriptor":"Specific feedback used to suggest improvements."}],"time":150,"sim":"websim"}],"examTips":["Know the full names AND purposes of abbreviations: Wi-Fi, HTML, WWW, HTTP/HTTPS, FTP, TCP, DNS, ISP, URL, DSL, WiMAX, VoIP, IRC. A full name without its purpose usually earns only half the marks.","Define a network with BOTH halves: devices LINKED (cable or wireless) AND able to communicate/share resources. One half earns only one mark.","Classify networks by area (LAN/MAN/WAN/PAN) but intranet/extranet by WHO can access. Several buildings on one campus is still a LAN.","For devices, write the FUNCTION with a verb: a router 'connects networks and forwards packets', a switch 'sends data only to the intended device' (a hub sends to ALL ports). 'Gives Wi-Fi' is not a router's function.","Choosing media: long distance, high bandwidth, lightning or interference → fibre-optic. Ships/remote areas/after disasters → satellite. Always justify with the scenario.","In bandwidth calculations, multiply MB by 8 to get megabits before dividing by Mbps, and show your working for method marks.","Never write that the Internet and the World Wide Web are the same: the Web is one service (linked pages over HTTP) that runs on the Internet.","HTTPS means the connection is encrypted; it does NOT prove a site is genuine. In URL questions, the TLD is the very last part of the domain (.ky in www.carnival.org.ky)."],"units":[{"id":"S2U1","title":"Types of networks","objectives":["Define a computer network and state its advantages and disadvantages (SO 2.1)","Distinguish among LAN, MAN, WAN, PAN and WLAN by geographical area and use (SO 2.1)","Compare client–server and peer-to-peer networks and choose one for a scenario (SO 2.1)","Distinguish among the Internet, an intranet and an extranet (SO 2.1)","Describe mobile (cellular) networks as wireless WANs (SO 2.1)","Describe star, bus, ring and mesh topologies and compare their reliability (enrichment)"],"videos":[{"title":"LAN, MAN, WAN and PAN explained","q":"LAN MAN WAN PAN types of networks explained"},{"title":"Client-server vs peer-to-peer networks","q":"client server vs peer to peer network explained GCSE"},{"title":"Internet, intranet and extranet","q":"internet intranet extranet difference explained"},{"title":"Network topologies: star, bus, ring, mesh","q":"network topologies star bus ring mesh explained"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"What is a computer network?","html":"<p>A <b>computer network</b> is a collection of computers, servers, mainframes, network devices, peripherals or other devices <b>connected to one another</b> (by cable or wirelessly) to allow the <b>sharing of data and other resources</b>. Put simply: two or more devices linked so that they can <b>communicate</b> and <b>share resources</b>. Examiners ask for this definition often, so learn it well.</p>\n<p>Networks exist for <b>data communication</b>: the process of collecting and distributing data (text, voice, graphics and video) electronically from one point to another. The best-known network of all is the <b>Internet</b>, which connects millions of people all over the world.</p>\n<p>This section covers <b>Specific Objective 2.1</b>: distinguishing among the different types of networks. Before we sort them into types, let's see why anyone bothers to network computers at all.</p>\n<figure class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 420 210\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A small school network: computers, a printer and a server connect to a switch, and a router links the network to the Internet\">\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"170\" y=\"95\" width=\"80\" height=\"26\" rx=\"5\" stroke=\"#F5C542\"/>\n<rect x=\"300\" y=\"20\" width=\"80\" height=\"26\" rx=\"5\" stroke=\"#F5C542\"/>\n<ellipse cx=\"340\" cy=\"150\" rx=\"62\" ry=\"30\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\"/>\n<rect x=\"20\" y=\"20\" width=\"56\" height=\"36\" rx=\"4\"/><rect x=\"20\" y=\"85\" width=\"56\" height=\"36\" rx=\"4\"/><rect x=\"20\" y=\"150\" width=\"56\" height=\"36\" rx=\"4\"/>\n<rect x=\"170\" y=\"165\" width=\"80\" height=\"30\" rx=\"4\"/><rect x=\"170\" y=\"20\" width=\"80\" height=\"34\" rx=\"4\"/>\n<line x1=\"76\" y1=\"38\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"104\"/><line x1=\"76\" y1=\"103\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"108\"/><line x1=\"76\" y1=\"168\" x2=\"170\" y2=\"114\"/>\n<line x1=\"210\" y1=\"54\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"95\"/><line x1=\"210\" y1=\"121\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"165\"/>\n<line x1=\"250\" y1=\"104\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"40\"/><line x1=\"340\" y1=\"46\" x2=\"340\" y2=\"120\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/>\n</g>\n<g font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"48\" y=\"42\">PC 1</text><text x=\"48\" y=\"107\">PC 2</text><text x=\"48\" y=\"172\">Laptop</text>\n<text x=\"210\" y=\"112\">Switch</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"37\">Router</text>\n<text x=\"210\" y=\"41\">File server</text><text x=\"210\" y=\"184\">Shared printer</text>\n<text x=\"340\" y=\"148\">The Internet</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"162\" font-size=\"9\">(via the ISP)</text>\n</g></svg><figcaption>A small LAN like a school computer lab: devices share a printer and a file server through a switch, and one router shares the Internet connection.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Why build a network?</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Share hardware</b>: thirty students in the Calabash Point Secondary lab can print to one laser printer instead of thirty printers.</li>\n<li><b>Share files and software</b>: your teacher saves a worksheet on the file server and everyone opens it.</li>\n<li><b>Share one Internet connection</b> through a single router.</li>\n<li><b>Communicate</b>: e-mail, chat, video calls, Teams and WhatsApp all travel over networks.</li>\n<li><b>Central management and backup</b>: the technician backs up the server once instead of visiting every PC.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>The downside</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Viruses and other malware can <b>spread</b> from one machine to all the others.</li>\n<li>If the server or main switch fails, <b>everyone</b> is affected.</li>\n<li>Networks cost money to set up and need skilled people to <b>manage and secure</b> them.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> \"State TWO advantages of networking the computers in a school\" is a classic 2-mark question. Give two <i>different</i> advantages (for example sharing a printer and sharing files), not the same one twice.</div>","plain":"<p>A network is just computers that are connected so they can \"talk\" and share. Think of your class WhatsApp group: instead of everyone walking to each other's house, you all connect once and share messages, photos and voice notes. A school network lets everyone share one printer, one set of files and one Internet line.</p>","deeper":"<p>Examiners like the word <b>resources</b>. A resource is anything worth sharing: hardware (printers, scanners, storage), software (licensed programs), data (files, databases) and the Internet connection itself. Strong answers also explain the <b>trade-off</b>: sharing makes things cheaper and easier to manage, but it also creates a <b>single point of failure</b> (if the server dies, nobody can open their files) and a <b>security risk</b> (one infected USB stick can spread malware across the lab). In Section 3 you will meet countermeasures such as firewalls and antivirus that reduce that risk.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"LAN, MAN and WAN: size matters","html":"<p>The main way to classify networks is by the <b>geographical area</b> they cover.</p>\n<table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Area covered</th><th>Caribbean example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>LAN</b> (Local Area Network)</td><td>A limited area such as a home, school, laboratory, university campus or office building</td><td>The computer lab at Frangipani Hill Academy; the network in a Front Street jewellery store</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>MAN</b> (Metropolitan Area Network)</td><td>A high-speed network linking LANs across a town or city (larger than a LAN, smaller than a WAN); often run by one organisation and carrying most of that organisation's traffic in the area</td><td>A network linking the Sint Maarten Water &amp; Light offices in Philipsburg, Cole Bay and Simpson Bay</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>WAN</b> (Wide Area Network)</td><td>A relatively large area such as a country or several countries; usually two or more LANs joined together</td><td>The Island Bank connecting branches in Sint Maarten, Curaçao, Aruba and Trinidad</td></tr>\n</table>\n<figure class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 420 220\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Nested boxes showing PAN inside LAN inside MAN inside WAN, from a few metres to the whole world\">\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"6\" y=\"6\" width=\"408\" height=\"208\" rx=\"14\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\"/>\n<rect x=\"30\" y=\"40\" width=\"300\" height=\"164\" rx=\"12\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<rect x=\"52\" y=\"74\" width=\"196\" height=\"120\" rx=\"10\" stroke=\"#F5C542\"/>\n<rect x=\"72\" y=\"112\" width=\"96\" height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/>\n</g>\n<g font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"28\">WAN: countries and continents (the Internet is the largest)</text>\n<text x=\"42\" y=\"60\">MAN: a town or city, e.g. Philipsburg to Cole Bay</text>\n<text x=\"62\" y=\"94\">LAN: one building or campus</text>\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"130\">PAN: ~10 m</text>\n</g>\n<g font-size=\"9\" fill=\"currentColor\"><text x=\"80\" y=\"146\">phone + earbuds</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"160\">+ smartwatch</text>\n<text x=\"178\" y=\"130\">school lab,</text><text x=\"178\" y=\"143\">office, home</text>\n<text x=\"256\" y=\"110\">linked</text><text x=\"256\" y=\"122\">offices across</text><text x=\"256\" y=\"134\">one town</text>\n<text x=\"340\" y=\"80\">bank branches</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"92\">on several</text><text x=\"340\" y=\"104\">islands</text></g>\n</svg><figcaption>Networks are classified by the geographical area they cover: PAN &lt; LAN &lt; MAN &lt; WAN.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Key facts</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A LAN is usually <b>owned and managed by one organisation</b> and is fast, because the cables are short.</li>\n<li>A WAN often uses links <b>leased from telecommunications companies</b>: undersea fibre cables, microwave and satellite links.</li>\n<li>A WAN is often a set of <b>LANs joined together</b>. The bank's branch LANs are linked into one WAN.</li>\n<li>WAN links run through <b>public networks</b> such as the telephone system, through <b>leased lines</b> rented from a telecom company, or through <b>radio</b>, <b>optical fibre</b> and <b>satellite</b> links.</li>\n<li>The <b>Internet</b> is the largest WAN in the world: a network of networks.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> When you are asked to <b>distinguish</b> between a LAN and a WAN, compare them on the <i>same</i> point: \"A LAN covers a small area such as one building, <b>whereas</b> a WAN covers a large area such as several countries.\"</div>","plain":"<p>Picture three sizes of network. A <b>LAN</b> is one building, like your school. A <b>MAN</b> is one town, like offices spread around Philipsburg. A <b>WAN</b> is huge, like a bank linking branches on different islands. The Internet is the biggest WAN of all.</p>","deeper":"<p>Size is the textbook test, but real examiners sometimes give clues instead of distances. <b>Ownership</b>: a LAN is almost always owned by one organisation, while WANs rent links from telecom providers. <b>Speed and errors</b>: short LAN cables give high speeds and few errors; long WAN links are slower and suffer more delay (latency). <b>Trap</b>: a university with several buildings on one campus is still a LAN (a campus network), even though it covers more than one building. A network covering one whole island like Sint Maarten (about 34 km² on the Dutch side) would normally be called a MAN, not a WAN.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"LAN, MAN or WAN?","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Each card describes a real-world style network in the Caribbean. Decide how big it is.</p>","main":"Which type of network is described?","bins":["LAN","MAN","WAN"],"items":[{"t":"A hotel chain links its resorts in Bonaire, Antigua and Barbados to one booking system.","bin":2,"fb":"Several islands: a WAN."},{"t":"The ten computers and printer in the Coralstone College library.","bin":0,"fb":"One building: a LAN."},{"t":"A family connects two laptops, a smart TV and a printer at home in Rincon.","bin":0,"fb":"One home: a LAN."},{"t":"A city government links its town hall, library and fire stations across Kingston.","bin":1,"fb":"Buildings spread across one city: a MAN."},{"t":"A shipping company links its offices in Port of Spain, Kingston and Miami.","bin":2,"fb":"Countries apart: a WAN."},{"t":"The network on one university campus with five buildings close together.","bin":0,"fb":"A campus of nearby buildings is still a LAN."},{"t":"The Internet.","bin":2,"fb":"The Internet is the world's largest WAN."},{"t":"The police connect stations across one city so officers can share records.","bin":1,"fb":"A city-sized network is a MAN."}],"win":"Sorted! You judged each network by the area it covers.","tutor":[{"h":"Ask: how far apart?","p":"<p>One building or campus = LAN. One town or city = MAN. Different countries or islands = WAN.</p>"},{"h":"Watch for the campus trap","p":"<p>Several buildings <i>close together</i> on one site still make a LAN.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"LAN, MAN or WAN? (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Is the network small (LAN) or very big (WAN)?</p>","bins":["LAN","WAN"],"main":"Small and local, or wide?","items":[{"t":"Two laptops and a printer at home.","bin":0,"fb":"One home: LAN."},{"t":"Computers in one school lab.","bin":0,"fb":"One room: LAN."},{"t":"The Internet.","bin":1,"fb":"The biggest WAN of all."},{"t":"A bank linking branches in five countries.","bin":1,"fb":"Five countries: WAN."}]},"up":{"title":"LAN, MAN or WAN? (Challenge)","intro":"<p>Trickier cards: think about area, ownership and links.</p>","items":[{"t":"A cruise line connects the ships' onboard networks to head office in Florida by satellite.","bin":2,"fb":"Satellite links across oceans: a WAN."},{"t":"A college links three campuses spread across one city using fibre leased from a telecom provider.","bin":1,"fb":"City-wide campuses: a MAN."},{"t":"A medical centre's network across its three-storey building and car-park kiosk.","bin":0,"fb":"One site: a LAN."},{"t":"An airport's network inside the terminal at Flamingo Airport.","bin":0,"fb":"One terminal building: a LAN."},{"t":"Regional exam body offices in Barbados and Jamaica sharing one database.","bin":2,"fb":"Islands apart: a WAN."},{"t":"Traffic-light controllers and CCTV cameras linked across one capital city.","bin":1,"fb":"City-wide: a MAN."},{"t":"Two LANs in different countries joined by an undersea cable.","bin":2,"fb":"LANs joined across countries form a WAN."},{"t":"A network that covers the whole of a small town such as Tera Kora.","bin":1,"fb":"A town: a MAN."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"PAN and WLAN: personal and wireless","html":"<p>Two more network types are in the syllabus.</p>\n<h4>PAN: Personal Area Network</h4>\n<p>A <b>PAN</b> connects devices <b>around one person</b>, usually within about 10 metres. It is mostly wireless, using <b>Bluetooth</b>, but can use a USB cable.</p>\n<ul><li>Your phone linked to your earbuds and smartwatch.</li><li>A laptop linked to a wireless mouse and a Bluetooth speaker at a beach lime in Marsh Harbour.</li><li>A game console linked to wireless controllers.</li></ul>\n<h4>WLAN: Wireless Local Area Network</h4>\n<p>A <b>WLAN</b> is a LAN in which devices connect <b>using radio waves (Wi-Fi)</b> instead of cables. A <b>wireless access point</b> (often built into the router) sends and receives the signals.</p>\n<ul><li>The Wi-Fi in a café on Bay Street or at the LPIA departure lounge.</li><li>The school's Wi-Fi that lets tablets connect to Google Classroom.</li></ul>\n<table><tr><th>WLAN advantages</th><th>WLAN disadvantages</th></tr>\n<tr><td>No cables to install: cheaper and neater, good for old buildings</td><td>Usually slower and less reliable than a good wired connection</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Users can move around (mobility)</td><td>Signals weaken through thick concrete walls (hurricane-proof buildings!) and with distance</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Easy to add new devices and visitors</td><td>Less secure: signals travel through the air, so strong encryption (WPA2/WPA3) and a good password are essential</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Supports phones and tablets that have no network port</td><td>Interference from other networks and devices</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> A WLAN is still a <b>LAN</b> (it covers a small area). The \"W\" only tells you the links are wireless. Don't confuse WLAN with WAN.</div>","plain":"<p>A <b>PAN</b> is the tiny network around <i>you</i>: phone, earbuds, smartwatch, all within arm's reach, usually by Bluetooth. A <b>WLAN</b> is a normal local network, but with Wi-Fi instead of cables, like the Wi-Fi at school or in a café.</p>","deeper":"<p>Think about <b>who it belongs to</b> and <b>how far it reaches</b>. A PAN is centred on one person and reaches a few metres; a WLAN serves many users in a building and reaches tens of metres from each access point. Large buildings use <b>several access points</b> so that users can roam without losing the connection (the LPIA terminal could not work with just one). Exam trap: a hotspot you create on your phone for a friend's laptop is effectively a tiny WLAN, but Bluetooth earbuds paired with that same phone form a PAN. You will meet Bluetooth, Wi-Fi and hotspots in detail in Units 2 and 3.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"The poolside Wi-Fi","bloom":"Apply","q":"<p>The Sunset Reef Hotel in Salem wants guests to use their phones and tablets anywhere around the pool and lobby. Running cables across the pool deck is impossible, and guests' devices have no network ports. Which type of network BEST suits this need?</p>","options":[{"t":"A wired LAN with cables to every sun lounger","fb":"The scenario says cables are impossible and guests' devices have no ports."},{"t":"A PAN using Bluetooth for each guest","fb":"A PAN serves one person within a few metres and would not connect guests to the hotel's Internet service."},{"t":"A WLAN using wireless access points around the pool and lobby","fb":"Correct. A WLAN gives local coverage by radio, so mobile devices can move freely without cables.","ok":true},{"t":"A WAN linking the hotel to other islands","fb":"A WAN covers large distances. The need here is local coverage around one hotel."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Read the constraints","p":"<p>No cables, mobile devices, one building area. Which network type matches <i>all</i> three?</p>"},{"h":"W for wireless","p":"<p>WLAN = a LAN whose links are wireless (Wi-Fi). WAN = wide area. The letters matter.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"The poolside Wi-Fi (Foundation)","q":"<p>A hotel in Salem wants guests to connect their phones to the Internet by Wi-Fi around the pool, with no cables. Which type of network is this?</p>","options":[{"t":"PAN (Personal Area Network)","fb":"A PAN is just the devices around one person."},{"t":"WLAN (Wireless Local Area Network)","fb":"Yes: local, and wireless.","ok":true},{"t":"WAN (Wide Area Network)","fb":"A WAN covers countries, not one hotel."}]},"up":{"title":"The poolside Wi-Fi (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"<p>The Sunset Reef Hotel installs a WLAN for guests. The manager also wants the front-desk computers, which handle card payments and guests' passport details, to join the same open Wi-Fi network \"to save money\". Which is the BEST evaluation of this plan?</p>","options":[{"t":"Sensible: Wi-Fi signals cannot be picked up outside the hotel, so the data is safe.","fb":"Radio signals leak through walls and windows; that is exactly why wireless needs encryption."},{"t":"Risky: the front desk should be on a separate, secured network (wired or a separate encrypted WLAN), because sensitive data on an open guest network could be intercepted.","fb":"Correct. Wireless is convenient, but guest networks are open to strangers; payment and passport data need separation and strong security.","ok":true},{"t":"Sensible: a WLAN is always faster than a wired LAN, so the front desk will work better.","fb":"Wireless is usually slower and less reliable than good cabling, not faster."},{"t":"Risky: front-desk computers cannot connect to any wireless network.","fb":"Most computers can use Wi-Fi with a wireless NIC; the issue is security, not ability."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"Client–server or peer-to-peer?","html":"<p>Networks can also be classified by <b>how the computers relate to each other</b>.</p>\n<figure class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 420 190\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Left: client-server network with a central server; right: peer-to-peer network where every computer links to the others\">\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"80\" y=\"70\" width=\"60\" height=\"40\" rx=\"5\" stroke=\"#F5C542\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<rect x=\"20\" y=\"15\" width=\"44\" height=\"28\" rx=\"4\"/><rect x=\"156\" y=\"15\" width=\"44\" height=\"28\" rx=\"4\"/>\n<rect x=\"20\" y=\"135\" width=\"44\" height=\"28\" rx=\"4\"/><rect x=\"156\" y=\"135\" width=\"44\" height=\"28\" rx=\"4\"/>\n<line x1=\"64\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"85\" y2=\"70\"/><line x1=\"156\" y1=\"35\" x2=\"135\" y2=\"70\"/><line x1=\"64\" y1=\"145\" x2=\"85\" y2=\"110\"/><line x1=\"156\" y1=\"145\" x2=\"135\" y2=\"110\"/>\n<rect x=\"250\" y=\"20\" width=\"44\" height=\"28\" rx=\"4\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\"/><rect x=\"356\" y=\"20\" width=\"44\" height=\"28\" rx=\"4\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\"/>\n<rect x=\"250\" y=\"130\" width=\"44\" height=\"28\" rx=\"4\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\"/><rect x=\"356\" y=\"130\" width=\"44\" height=\"28\" rx=\"4\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\"/>\n<line x1=\"294\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"356\" y2=\"34\"/><line x1=\"294\" y1=\"144\" x2=\"356\" y2=\"144\"/><line x1=\"272\" y1=\"48\" x2=\"272\" y2=\"130\"/><line x1=\"378\" y1=\"48\" x2=\"378\" y2=\"130\"/><line x1=\"294\" y1=\"48\" x2=\"356\" y2=\"130\"/><line x1=\"356\" y1=\"48\" x2=\"294\" y2=\"130\"/>\n</g>\n<g font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"110\" y=\"88\">SERVER</text><text x=\"110\" y=\"101\" font-size=\"9\">files, log-ins</text>\n<text x=\"42\" y=\"33\">client</text><text x=\"178\" y=\"33\">client</text><text x=\"42\" y=\"153\">client</text><text x=\"178\" y=\"153\">client</text>\n<text x=\"272\" y=\"38\">peer</text><text x=\"378\" y=\"38\">peer</text><text x=\"272\" y=\"148\">peer</text><text x=\"378\" y=\"148\">peer</text>\n<text x=\"110\" y=\"184\" font-weight=\"700\">Client–server</text><text x=\"325\" y=\"184\" font-weight=\"700\">Peer-to-peer</text>\n</g></svg><figcaption>In client–server, a central server provides services to clients. In peer-to-peer, every computer is equal and shares with the others directly.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Client–server network</h4>\n<p>One or more powerful computers called <b>servers</b> provide services, and the other computers, called <b>clients</b>, request them. Typical servers are:</p>\n<ul><li><b>File server</b>: stores users' files centrally.</li><li><b>Print server</b>: manages the queue for shared printers.</li><li><b>Authentication (log-in) server</b>: checks usernames and passwords.</li><li><b>Web and e-mail servers</b>: host websites and mailboxes.</li></ul>\n<p>When you log in at any computer in the Sea-Almond lab and your own documents appear, that is client–server at work: your files live on the server, not on the PC in front of you.</p>\n<h4>Peer-to-peer (P2P) network</h4>\n<p>All computers are <b>equal (peers)</b>. There is no dedicated server; each computer can share its own files and printer with the others, and each user manages their own machine.</p>\n<ul><li>A small family business on the Waterkant with three laptops sharing one printer.</li><li>Two friends sending a file directly from phone to phone (for example with AirDrop or Nearby Share).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> Define each one by its <b>key feature</b>: client–server has a <b>central server</b> that manages resources and security; peer-to-peer has <b>no central server</b> and every computer has equal status.</div>","plain":"<p>In <b>client–server</b>, there is a \"boss\" computer (the server) that keeps the files and checks who can log in; the other computers (clients) ask it for things. In <b>peer-to-peer</b>, there is no boss: every computer is equal and shares directly, like friends passing notes to each other in class.</p>","deeper":"<p>\"Server\" describes a <b>role</b>, not just a box. A server is any computer (or program) that provides a service, so a small office PC that shares a printer is acting as a server for that moment. That is why exam answers should focus on the <b>dedicated central server</b> as the distinguishing feature. Large organisations often run many servers in a <b>data centre</b>, or rent them in the <b>cloud</b> (Unit 4). Famous P2P <i>applications</i> such as BitTorrent run over the Internet, which itself is mostly client–server; and P2P file sharing is often linked to software piracy and copyright infringement, which you will meet in Section 3.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Comparing client–server and P2P","html":"<p>Neither type is \"best\". The right choice depends on <b>size, budget, security needs and skills</b>.</p>\n<table><tr><th></th><th>Client–server</th><th>Peer-to-peer</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Cost</b></td><td>Higher: server hardware, server software, a network administrator</td><td>Low: uses the computers you already have</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Security</b></td><td>Strong and central: one place sets passwords and access rights</td><td>Weaker: each user decides what to share</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Backup</b></td><td>Central: back up the server and everyone's work is safe</td><td>Each computer must be backed up separately</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Size</b></td><td>Scales to hundreds or thousands of users</td><td>Best for small networks (about 10 computers or fewer)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>If one machine fails</b></td><td>If the <b>server</b> fails, everyone loses access</td><td>Only that computer's shared files are lost; others carry on</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Skills needed</b></td><td>Specialist staff to set up and manage</td><td>Easy to set up</td></tr></table>\n<h4>Choosing</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Mahoe Grove High School</b> with hundreds of students: client–server, so students can log in anywhere and the technician can control access and back up centrally.</li>\n<li><b>A three-person tour desk at the cruise pier</b>: peer-to-peer is cheap and simple, and security needs are modest.</li>\n<li><b>A bank</b>: definitely client–server, because customer data must be centrally secured and audited.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> In \"justify your choice\" questions, link the feature to the scenario: \"Client–server is best for the clinic <b>because</b> patient records must be kept secure and backed up centrally.\"</div>","plain":"<p>Client–server costs more but is safer and better for big groups, because one central server controls passwords and backups. Peer-to-peer is cheap and easy, but only sensible for a handful of computers, like a tiny shop or a home.</p>","deeper":"<p>A strong Grade One answer weighs <b>both</b> sides. For example: \"Although client–server costs more and the server is a single point of failure, the school should choose it, because with 600 users central security and backup outweigh the cost. The single-point-of-failure risk can be reduced with a backup server and a UPS.\" Notice the three moves: <b>acknowledge</b> the drawback, <b>decide</b>, and <b>mitigate</b>. That structure works for any Evaluate or Justify question across the syllabus.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Which model fits?","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>For each organisation, choose the network model that suits it best.</p>","items":[{"text":"A busy clinic in Port-au-Prince must keep patient records secure and backed up every night.","options":["Peer-to-peer","Client–server"],"answer":1,"fb":"Sensitive data and central backup point to client–server."},{"text":"Three friends running a small T-shirt printing business from home share one printer.","options":["Client–server","Peer-to-peer"],"answer":1,"fb":"Small, cheap and simple: peer-to-peer."},{"text":"A school with 700 students who must be able to log in at any computer and find their own files.","options":["Peer-to-peer","Client–server"],"answer":1,"fb":"Log in anywhere = files on a central server."},{"text":"A grandmother sends photos from her tablet straight to her grandson's phone without any server.","options":["Client–server","Peer-to-peer"],"answer":1,"fb":"Direct device-to-device sharing is peer-to-peer."},{"text":"A bank with an IT department that controls exactly which staff can see which accounts.","options":["Peer-to-peer","Client–server"],"answer":1,"fb":"Central access control: client–server."},{"text":"A fishing co-op's office with two computers and no IT budget.","options":["Peer-to-peer","Client–server"],"answer":0,"fb":"Very small, very low budget: peer-to-peer."}],"win":"Great choices. Size, security and budget drive the decision.","tutor":[{"h":"Three questions","p":"<p>How many users? How sensitive is the data? Is there money and skill to manage a server?</p>"},{"h":"Rule of thumb","p":"<p>Small and simple: peer-to-peer. Large or sensitive: client–server.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which model fits? (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Choose client–server or peer-to-peer.</p>","items":[{"text":"A large school with 700 students.","options":["Peer-to-peer","Client–server"],"answer":1,"fb":"Large: client–server."},{"text":"Two laptops at home sharing a printer.","options":["Peer-to-peer","Client–server"],"answer":0,"fb":"Tiny: peer-to-peer."},{"text":"A bank protecting customer accounts.","options":["Client–server","Peer-to-peer"],"answer":0,"fb":"Sensitive data: client–server."}]},"up":{"title":"Which model fits? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Now choose the BEST reason as well as the model.</p>","items":[{"text":"A 6-person accounting firm handles clients' tax records. Which is the strongest choice?","options":["Client–server, because confidential records need central security and backup even in a small office","Peer-to-peer, because it is cheapest","Peer-to-peer, because small offices never need servers"],"answer":0,"fb":"Sensitivity of data can outweigh small size."},{"text":"Why does a server failure hurt a client–server network more than one PC failing in a P2P network?","options":["Servers are slower than PCs","The server holds shared resources everyone depends on: a single point of failure","P2P networks have no files"],"answer":1,"fb":"Central = single point of failure."},{"text":"A school wants client–server but fears server failure during exams. Best mitigation?","options":["Add a backup server, a UPS and regular off-site backups","Turn the server off during exams","Switch to peer-to-peer"],"answer":0,"fb":"Mitigate the risk instead of abandoning the benefits."},{"text":"Which statement about P2P is TRUE?","options":["P2P is more secure than client–server","Every computer can act as both client and server","P2P always needs a dedicated server"],"answer":1,"fb":"Peers both request and provide."},{"text":"A start-up expects to grow from 4 to 60 staff in two years. Best advice?","options":["Start simple if needed, but plan to move to client–server as it grows","Never network the computers","P2P now and forever"],"answer":0,"fb":"P2P does not scale well; plan for growth."}]}},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Network vocabulary","bloom":"Remember","intro":"<p>Match each term to its meaning.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"LAN","r":"Network covering one building or campus"},{"l":"MAN","r":"Network covering a town or city"},{"l":"WAN","r":"Network covering countries or continents"},{"l":"PAN","r":"Network of devices around one person, about 10 m"},{"l":"WLAN","r":"Local network using Wi-Fi radio links"},{"l":"Server","r":"Computer that provides services to other computers"},{"l":"Client","r":"Computer that requests services from a server"}],"win":"Vocabulary locked in. These terms appear in almost every Paper 02 networking question.","tutor":[{"h":"Decode the letters","p":"<p>L = Local, M = Metropolitan, W = Wide, P = Personal, WL = Wireless Local. AN = Area Network.</p>"},{"h":"Who gives, who asks?","p":"<p>A server <i>serves</i> (provides); a client asks, like a customer in a shop.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Network vocabulary (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"LAN","r":"One building"},{"l":"WAN","r":"Many countries"},{"l":"PAN","r":"Around one person"},{"l":"Server","r":"Provides services"}]},"up":{"title":"Network vocabulary (Challenge)","bloom":"Understand","pairs":[{"l":"LAN","r":"Usually owned by one organisation, high speed, short cables"},{"l":"WAN","r":"Often uses leased links such as undersea cables or satellites"},{"l":"MAN","r":"Could link a city's fire stations and town hall"},{"l":"PAN","r":"Usually built with Bluetooth"},{"l":"WLAN","r":"Needs WPA2/WPA3 encryption because signals travel through the air"},{"l":"Peer-to-peer","r":"No dedicated server; each computer shares its own resources"},{"l":"Client–server","r":"Central log-in, security and backup"},{"l":"The Internet","r":"The largest WAN: a network of networks"}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"Internet, intranet and extranet","html":"<p>These three words look alike, so examiners love them. They differ in <b>who is allowed in</b>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <b>Internet</b> is the <b>global, public</b> network of interconnected networks. Anyone with a device and a connection from an Internet Service Provider (ISP) can use it.</li>\n<li>An <b>intranet</b> is a <b>private</b> network belonging to one organisation, <b>for its own staff only</b>. It uses the same technology as the Internet (web pages, browsers, e-mail) but is protected by log-ins and a firewall.</li>\n<li>An <b>extranet</b> is a part of an organisation's private network that is <b>opened to selected outsiders</b> such as suppliers, partners or customers, who log in with their own accounts.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 420 210\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"The Internet as a large public area; inside it a company's private intranet, with an extranet doorway that lets selected partners in\">\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"6\" y=\"6\" width=\"408\" height=\"198\" rx=\"16\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\"/>\n<rect x=\"40\" y=\"48\" width=\"200\" height=\"128\" rx=\"12\" stroke=\"#F5C542\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<rect x=\"240\" y=\"88\" width=\"80\" height=\"48\" rx=\"6\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 3\"/>\n<circle cx=\"370\" cy=\"112\" r=\"16\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<line x1=\"320\" y1=\"112\" x2=\"354\" y2=\"112\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n</g>\n<g font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"18\" y=\"26\" font-weight=\"700\">THE INTERNET: public, anyone can connect</text>\n<text x=\"54\" y=\"70\" font-weight=\"700\">INTRANET (private)</text>\n<text x=\"54\" y=\"88\">Island Bank staff only:</text><text x=\"54\" y=\"102\">HR forms, policies, staff news,</text><text x=\"54\" y=\"116\">internal systems</text>\n<text x=\"54\" y=\"150\">log-in needed, behind a firewall</text>\n<text x=\"248\" y=\"106\" font-weight=\"700\">EXTRANET</text><text x=\"248\" y=\"120\" font-size=\"9\">part of intranet</text><text x=\"248\" y=\"131\" font-size=\"9\">opened to partners</text>\n<text x=\"354\" y=\"146\" font-size=\"9\">supplier</text><text x=\"354\" y=\"158\" font-size=\"9\">or agent</text>\n</g></svg><figcaption>An intranet is private to the organisation. An extranet opens a controlled part of it to approved outsiders. Both usually run on Internet technology.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Island Bank example</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Internet</b>: anyone can visit the bank's public website to see branch opening hours.</li>\n<li><b>Intranet</b>: staff log in to read the policy manual, book leave and use internal systems.</li>\n<li><b>Extranet</b>: the security company that services the ATMs logs in to a portal to see fault reports and schedule repairs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Typical users: <b>banks</b> rely on intranets to share confidential information among staff securely; <b>call centres</b> that serve other companies' customers often use extranets, since the call centre needs some of the client company's information without access to its whole network. An extranet is often simply a <b>private, password-protected part of a website</b>.</p>\n<p>Your school's <b>Google Classroom or Teams</b> works like an extranet in one way: teachers and enrolled students (and sometimes parents) can log in, but the public cannot.</p>","plain":"<b>Internet</b> = open to everyone in the world. <b>Intranet</b> = private, for the organisation's own staff (like a staff-room door that needs a key card). <b>Extranet</b> = the private network with a side door opened for invited outsiders, like suppliers or partners.","deeper":"<p>A memory trick: <b>intra</b> means <i>within</i> (as in intramural sports, played within one school) and <b>extra</b> means <i>outside</i>. Exam trap: an intranet is <b>not</b> the same as a LAN. A LAN is defined by <i>area</i>; an intranet is defined by <i>access</i>. A bank's intranet can span a WAN across five islands, and staff may reach it from home using a <b>VPN</b> (Virtual Private Network), which creates an encrypted tunnel across the public Internet. So remote access does not turn an intranet into the Internet; it is still private.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Benefits and risks of intranets and extranets","html":"<p>Why would an organisation build its own private \"mini-Internet\"?</p>\n<h4>Intranet benefits</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>One place for information</b>: policies, forms, timetables and announcements are always up to date (no more photocopied notices).</li>\n<li><b>Faster internal communication</b> and collaboration between departments and branches.</li>\n<li><b>Security</b>: confidential information stays inside the organisation.</li>\n<li>Staff already know how to use a browser, so <b>little training</b> is needed.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Extranet benefits</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Partners can <b>serve themselves</b>: a supplier checks stock levels and sends invoices without phoning.</li>\n<li><b>Faster ordering and fewer errors</b> in supply chains. A supermarket in Punda could let its wholesaler see when stock is low and deliver automatically.</li>\n<li>Customers can track orders or view their accounts.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Advantages and disadvantages of an intranet (learn these)</h4>\n<table><tr><th>Advantages</th><th>Disadvantages</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Commercial or confidential data is kept secure within the organisation</td><td>Requires upgrades, and there can be software incompatibility problems</td></tr>\n<tr><td>E-mails stay private and may also be encrypted</td><td>Security features can be inadequate if not set up and maintained well</td></tr></table>\n<h4>Risks and costs</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Setting up and maintaining the servers and security costs money and expertise.</li>\n<li>An extranet opens a door to outsiders, so it needs <b>strong authentication</b>, careful <b>access rights</b> (partners see only what they need) and monitoring.</li>\n<li>If the content is not kept current, staff stop trusting it.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th></th><th>Internet</th><th>Intranet</th><th>Extranet</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Who can access?</td><td>Anyone</td><td>Members of the organisation only</td><td>Organisation plus approved outsiders</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Log-in needed?</td><td>Not for public sites</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Typical uses</td><td>Businesses advertise, sell products and accept bill payments; people communicate by e-mail, newsgroups and social media; research and entertainment</td><td>Distribute documents, share information, staff training, conferencing among workers</td><td>Share information, build closer relationships and improve efficiency with customers and suppliers</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Example</td><td>A news website</td><td>A bank's staff portal</td><td>A supplier portal; a call centre's client system</td></tr></table>","plain":"<p>An intranet is like a staff-only noticeboard and filing cabinet online: quick, private and always updated. An extranet lets trusted partners in through a locked side door, so they can check orders themselves. Both need passwords and protection, and cost money to run.</p>","deeper":"<p>Think about <b>least privilege</b>: an extranet should show each partner <i>only</i> the data they need. If the ATM maintenance company can see customer balances, the design has failed. This links directly to <b>access privileges</b> and <b>audit trails</b> in Section 3 (Countermeasures). Also notice that many organisations now host intranets in the <b>cloud</b> (for example Microsoft SharePoint or Google Sites restricted to the organisation's domain). The location of the server does not decide whether it is an intranet; <b>who is allowed access</b> does.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Internet, intranet or extranet?","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Read how each service is used and who can use it.</p>","main":"Where does this belong?","bins":["Internet","Intranet","Extranet"],"items":[{"t":"Hospital staff log in to read the latest infection-control policy.","bin":1,"fb":"Staff only: intranet."},{"t":"A wholesaler logs in to a supermarket's portal to see which shelves need restocking.","bin":2,"fb":"Approved outsider: extranet."},{"t":"Cruise-line employees book their annual leave on an internal web page.","bin":1,"fb":"Employees only: intranet."},{"t":"Anyone reads The Road Town Courier's sports page online.","bin":0,"fb":"Public: Internet."},{"t":"A school's exam board partner uploads marks to a secure portal provided by the school.","bin":2,"fb":"An outside partner with controlled access: extranet."},{"t":"Teachers at a school download the staff handbook from a private staff portal.","bin":1,"fb":"Staff only: intranet."},{"t":"Tourists check the ferry times to Virgin Gorda on a public website.","bin":0,"fb":"Open to anyone: Internet."},{"t":"A travel agent in Miami logs in to a resort's system to book rooms for clients.","bin":2,"fb":"Partner access: extranet."}],"win":"Excellent. You sorted each service by who is allowed in.","tutor":[{"h":"Who is using it?","p":"<p>Anyone = Internet. Only the organisation's own people = intranet. Outsiders with a log-in = extranet.</p>"},{"h":"Look for clues","p":"<p>Words like <i>staff</i>, <i>employees</i>, <i>internal</i> suggest intranet. <i>Supplier</i>, <i>partner</i>, <i>agent</i> suggest extranet.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Internet, intranet or extranet? (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Who can use it?</p>","items":[{"t":"A public website anyone can visit.","bin":0,"fb":"Internet."},{"t":"A staff-only website inside a company.","bin":1,"fb":"Intranet."},{"t":"A company website where suppliers log in.","bin":2,"fb":"Extranet."},{"t":"Watching videos on a public video site.","bin":0,"fb":"Internet."},{"t":"Employees checking their payslips on the company portal.","bin":1,"fb":"Intranet."}]},"up":{"title":"Internet, intranet or extranet? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Careful: some cards include a distractor such as distance or VPN.</p>","items":[{"t":"A bank manager at home uses a VPN to open the bank's internal policy site.","bin":1,"fb":"A VPN gives remote access, but it is still the private intranet."},{"t":"A hotel group's staff portal spans branches on four islands.","bin":1,"fb":"An intranet can span a WAN; access is what matters."},{"t":"Parents log in to a school portal to see their own child's grades.","bin":2,"fb":"Selected outsiders with limited access: extranet."},{"t":"A government ministry's public page listing passport fees.","bin":0,"fb":"Public information: Internet."},{"t":"A courier company's clients track their parcels after logging in with an account number.","bin":2,"fb":"Customers given controlled access: extranet."},{"t":"Engineers at an electricity company see live fault maps on an internal dashboard.","bin":1,"fb":"Internal staff system: intranet."},{"t":"A public wiki that anyone can edit.","bin":0,"fb":"Open to all: Internet."}]}},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"True about intranets?","bloom":"Understand","intro":"<p>Your classmate wrote these notes about intranets for revision. Some are wrong.</p>","q":"Tap EVERY statement that is correct.","lines":["1. An intranet is a private network for an organisation's own members.","2. An intranet can only exist inside one building.","3. An intranet uses the same technologies as the Internet, such as web pages and browsers.","4. Anyone on the Internet can browse an intranet without logging in.","5. An intranet can help staff find up-to-date policies and forms quickly.","6. A VPN can let staff reach the intranet securely from home."],"correct":[0,2,4,5],"win":"Spot on. Private, web technology, useful for staff, and reachable securely by VPN.","miss":"Check again: an intranet is defined by who may access it, not by its size, and it always needs a log-in.","tutor":[{"h":"Access, not area","p":"<p>An intranet can span many buildings or islands. What makes it an intranet is that only members can get in.</p>"},{"h":"Same tools","p":"<p>It looks like a website because it uses web technology, but it is private.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"True about intranets? (Foundation)","q":"<p>Tap ALL the correct statements (there are two).</p>","lines":["1. An intranet is private to one organisation.","2. Anyone in the world can see an intranet.","3. Staff use an intranet to share company information."],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Correct: private, for staff.","miss":"An intranet is not public."},"up":{"title":"True about intranets? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","q":"<p>Tap EVERY statement that is correct about intranets AND extranets.</p>","lines":["1. An extranet gives selected outsiders controlled access to part of a private network.","2. An intranet must be hosted on a server inside the organisation's own building.","3. Extranets need careful access rights so partners only see what they need.","4. A LAN and an intranet are the same thing.","5. Both intranets and extranets require authentication such as a username and password.","6. An extranet removes the need for any firewall.","7. An intranet can span several countries over a WAN."],"correct":[0,2,4,6],"win":"Excellent precision: access defines intranets and extranets, not location or size.","miss":"Watch the traps: hosting location, LAN vs intranet, and firewalls."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Mobile networks","html":"<p>Your phone is connected to a <b>mobile (cellular) network</b>, a type of <b>wireless WAN</b> run by a mobile network operator.</p>\n<h4>How it works</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The area is divided into <b>cells</b>. Each cell has a <b>base station</b> (cell tower with antennas) that communicates with phones by radio.</li>\n<li>Base stations are linked by cable, fibre or microwave to the operator's <b>core network</b>, which connects to the telephone system and the Internet.</li>\n<li>As you move, say on a bus from Philipsburg to Simpson Bay, your call is passed from one cell to the next without dropping. This is called a <b>handover</b> (or handoff).</li>\n<li>A <b>SIM card</b> (or eSIM) identifies you as a subscriber to the network.</li>\n<li>In a mobile network the <b>last link is wireless</b>: radio waves carry signals between your phone and the base station (base transceiver station). Each cell is served by at least one fixed transceiver, and neighbouring cells use <b>different sets of frequencies</b> to avoid interference.</li>\n<li>The mobile network is a <b>radio-based common carrier</b>: one shared radio network that carries voice, text and data for everyone who subscribes. The <b>radio technology</b> it uses (for example <b>GSM</b>, the standard used across most of the Caribbean and the world, or <b>CDMA</b>, used by some operators in the USA) decides which phones will work on it and where.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Mobile networks on islands</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Hills such as those around Cole Bay and Dutch Quarter can block signals, creating <b>dead spots</b>, so towers are placed on high ground.</li>\n<li>On a shared island like ours, a phone near the border can pick up a tower on the other side. Near Marigot you may suddenly be <b>roaming</b>, which can mean extra charges.</li>\n<li>After hurricanes such as Irma, damaged towers and power cuts can knock out coverage, so operators bring in backup generators and temporary towers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Generations of mobile technology (2G, 3G, 4G, 5G) and what you can do with them are covered in <b>Unit 3</b>. For now, remember: a mobile network is a <b>WAN</b> that uses <b>radio cells</b> to give wide-area wireless access.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> Do not mix up a <b>WLAN</b> (Wi-Fi in one building, run by its owner) with a <b>mobile network</b> (cells covering a whole country, run by a telecom operator).</div>","plain":"<p>Your phone talks to the nearest cell tower by radio. The country is split into many small areas called cells, each with its own tower. As you travel, your phone hops from tower to tower without dropping your call. All the towers together make a huge wireless WAN.</p>","deeper":"<p>Why <b>cells</b> instead of one giant transmitter? Radio frequencies are limited. By using many small cells, the same frequencies can be <b>reused</b> in cells that are far apart, so many more people can call at once. Busy places (the cruise pier on a six-ship day, Carnival Village at J'ouvert) may get extra small cells to handle the crowd. This is also why your battery drains faster in an area with weak signal: the phone boosts its transmit power to reach a distant tower. Link: GPS on your phone does not use the cell network at all; it uses satellites (Unit 3).</p>"},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"learn","title":"Network topologies (enrichment)","html":"<p>A <b>topology</b> is the <b>layout</b> or shape of the connections in a network. The syllabus treats this as enrichment, but it often helps you explain why a network fails or works well.</p>\n<figure class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 420 240\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Four network topologies: star with a central switch, bus with one backbone cable, ring where each device links to two neighbours, and mesh with many links\">\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"85\" y=\"40\" width=\"30\" height=\"20\" rx=\"3\" stroke=\"#F5C542\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<circle cx=\"40\" cy=\"25\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"160\" cy=\"25\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"40\" cy=\"80\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"160\" cy=\"80\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"98\" r=\"9\"/>\n<line x1=\"48\" y1=\"29\" x2=\"85\" y2=\"45\"/><line x1=\"152\" y1=\"29\" x2=\"115\" y2=\"45\"/><line x1=\"48\" y1=\"76\" x2=\"85\" y2=\"57\"/><line x1=\"152\" y1=\"76\" x2=\"115\" y2=\"57\"/><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"89\"/>\n<line x1=\"230\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"400\" y2=\"60\" stroke=\"#F5C542\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n<circle cx=\"255\" cy=\"30\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"315\" cy=\"30\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"375\" cy=\"30\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"92\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"345\" cy=\"92\" r=\"9\"/>\n<line x1=\"255\" y1=\"39\" x2=\"255\" y2=\"60\"/><line x1=\"315\" y1=\"39\" x2=\"315\" y2=\"60\"/><line x1=\"375\" y1=\"39\" x2=\"375\" y2=\"60\"/><line x1=\"285\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"285\" y2=\"83\"/><line x1=\"345\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"345\" y2=\"83\"/>\n<circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"175\" r=\"42\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"133\" r=\"9\" fill=\"currentColor\" fill-opacity=\".15\"/><circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"162\" r=\"9\" fill=\"currentColor\" fill-opacity=\".15\"/><circle cx=\"125\" cy=\"210\" r=\"9\" fill=\"currentColor\" fill-opacity=\".15\"/><circle cx=\"75\" cy=\"210\" r=\"9\" fill=\"currentColor\" fill-opacity=\".15\"/><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"162\" r=\"9\" fill=\"currentColor\" fill-opacity=\".15\"/>\n<circle cx=\"315\" cy=\"140\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"365\" cy=\"170\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"345\" cy=\"215\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"285\" cy=\"215\" r=\"9\"/><circle cx=\"265\" cy=\"170\" r=\"9\"/>\n<g stroke=\"#7FB2FF\"><line x1=\"315\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"365\" y2=\"170\"/><line x1=\"315\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"345\" y2=\"215\"/><line x1=\"315\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"285\" y2=\"215\"/><line x1=\"315\" y1=\"140\" x2=\"265\" y2=\"170\"/><line x1=\"365\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"345\" y2=\"215\"/><line x1=\"365\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"285\" y2=\"215\"/><line x1=\"365\" y1=\"170\" x2=\"265\" y2=\"170\"/><line x1=\"345\" y1=\"215\" x2=\"285\" y2=\"215\"/><line x1=\"345\" y1=\"215\" x2=\"265\" y2=\"170\"/><line x1=\"285\" y1=\"215\" x2=\"265\" y2=\"170\"/></g>\n</g>\n<g font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\">\n<text x=\"100\" y=\"54\" font-size=\"9\">switch</text><text x=\"100\" y=\"122\">STAR</text><text x=\"315\" y=\"118\">BUS (backbone cable)</text>\n<text x=\"100\" y=\"179\">RING</text><text x=\"420\" y=\"236\" text-anchor=\"end\">MESH (full mesh shown)</text>\n</g></svg><figcaption>Star, bus, ring and mesh: the topology is the layout of the links between devices.</figcaption></figure>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Star</b>: every device connects to a central <b>switch</b> (or hub). This is the layout of almost every modern LAN, including your school lab.</li>\n<li><b>Bus</b>: all devices attach to <b>one main cable</b> (the backbone), with terminators at each end. Common in older networks.</li>\n<li><b>Ring</b>: each device connects to <b>two neighbours</b>, forming a loop. Data travels around the ring from device to device.</li>\n<li><b>Mesh</b>: devices have <b>many</b> connections to each other, so there are several paths for data. In a <b>full mesh</b> every device links to every other. The Internet's backbone is a partial mesh.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Home \"mesh Wi-Fi\" systems use the same idea: several wireless units talk to each other so a signal can reach every room of a concrete house.</p>","plain":"<p>Topology just means the <b>shape</b> of a network. <b>Star</b>: everyone plugs into one box in the middle. <b>Bus</b>: everyone hangs off one long cable. <b>Ring</b>: everyone holds hands in a circle. <b>Mesh</b>: lots of connections, like a spider's web, so there are many routes.</p>","deeper":"<p>Topologies can be <b>physical</b> (how the cables actually run) or <b>logical</b> (how data actually flows). A network can look like a star physically while data flows logically like a ring. For a full mesh of <i>n</i> devices, the number of links is <b>n(n − 1) ÷ 2</b>. Five devices need 10 links; 20 devices need 190. That is why full mesh is used only where reliability really matters, such as linking a few critical routers, and why big networks combine topologies into a <b>hybrid</b> (for example, star LANs joined by a mesh WAN).</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Comparing topologies","html":"<p>The best way to compare topologies is to ask: <b>what happens when something breaks?</b></p>\n<table><tr><th>Topology</th><th>Advantages</th><th>Disadvantages</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Star</b></td><td>If one cable or PC fails, only that device is affected. Easy to add devices and find faults. Good performance with a switch.</td><td>If the central switch fails, the whole network goes down. Uses more cable than bus.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Bus</b></td><td>Cheap and simple; uses the least cable.</td><td>A break in the backbone stops the whole network. Slows down as more devices are added (data collisions). Hard to find faults.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Ring</b></td><td>Orderly data flow; performs well under heavy load because devices take turns.</td><td>One broken link or device can stop the whole ring (unless a dual ring is used). Adding devices disrupts the network.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Mesh</b></td><td>Very reliable: if one link fails, data takes another route. Handles lots of traffic.</td><td>Expensive and complex: many cables or connections to install and manage.</td></tr></table>\n<h4>Caribbean thinking</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A <b>hurricane shelter's</b> emergency communications should not depend on a single link, so a mesh-style design with several routes is attractive.</li>\n<li>A <b>school lab</b> uses a star: when one student's cable is damaged, the rest of the class keeps working, and the technician just protects the switch (and gives it a UPS).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> \"The central device fails\" = star problem. \"The main cable breaks\" = bus problem. \"One link in the loop breaks\" = ring problem. \"Expensive, many links\" = mesh.</div>","plain":"<p>Each shape has a weak spot. <b>Star</b>: the middle box. <b>Bus</b>: the one long cable. <b>Ring</b>: any break in the circle. <b>Mesh</b> has no single weak spot because there are many routes, but it costs the most.</p>","deeper":"<p>Island links show why <b>redundancy</b> matters. Several Caribbean territories have experienced Internet outages when a single undersea cable was cut. Networks that connect to <b>two or more cables or a satellite backup</b> behave like a mesh: traffic is rerouted and service continues. When an exam asks you to <b>recommend</b> a topology, weigh reliability against cost. Hospitals, airports such as Douglas-Charles Airport and banks justify the cost of redundant (mesh-like) links; a small shop does not.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Topology clues","bloom":"Understand","intro":"<p>Match each topology to the clue that describes it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Star","r":"Every device connects to a central switch"},{"l":"Bus","r":"Devices share one backbone cable with terminators at each end"},{"l":"Ring","r":"Each device links to exactly two neighbours in a loop"},{"l":"Mesh","r":"Many links give several possible routes for data"}],"win":"Nicely matched. Now you can picture every layout.","tutor":[{"h":"Picture the shape","p":"<p>Star = sun with rays. Bus = washing line. Ring = circle of friends. Mesh = spider's web.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Topology clues (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Star","r":"Central box in the middle"},{"l":"Bus","r":"One long main cable"},{"l":"Ring","r":"Devices in a circle"}]},"up":{"title":"Topology clues (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Match each topology to its main WEAKNESS or a fact about it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Star","r":"Fails completely if the central switch fails"},{"l":"Bus","r":"A break in the main cable stops everyone; collisions grow as devices are added"},{"l":"Ring","r":"One broken link can stop the loop unless a dual design is used"},{"l":"Full mesh","r":"Needs n(n − 1) ÷ 2 links: 10 links for 5 devices"},{"l":"Hybrid","r":"Combines topologies, e.g. star LANs joined by a mesh WAN"}]}},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Network the auto-parts shop","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>You are the IT consultant for <b>West Bay Auto Parts</b>. The owner, Mr Richardson, has 8 computers, one printer and a warehouse next door. He also wants his main supplier in Puerto Rico to check stock levels. Make the best decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Mr Richardson asks: \"What kind of network do I need for the shop and the warehouse next door?\"</p>","choices":[{"t":"A WAN, because there are two buildings.","to":"bad1","fb":"Two buildings next door is still a local area."},{"t":"A LAN, because both buildings are on one site close together.","to":"n2","fb":"Right. Neighbouring buildings on one site form a LAN."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>He has 8 PCs but plans to grow to 25 staff, and the stock and customer database must be backed up every night. Which model?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Peer-to-peer, because it is cheaper today.","to":"bad2","fb":"Cheap now, but it will not scale to 25 users and backup becomes messy."},{"t":"Client–server, with the database and backups on a central server.","to":"n3","fb":"Good. Growth plus central backup favours client–server."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The warehouse staff carry handheld barcode scanners and move around all day. How should they connect?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Add wireless access points so the warehouse has a secured WLAN (WPA3).","to":"n4","fb":"Yes. Mobile devices need wireless, with encryption."},{"t":"Run a long cable to each scanner.","to":"bad3","fb":"Staff move around; cables would trip them and limit movement."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Finally, the supplier in Puerto Rico wants to see stock levels so it can restock automatically. What do you set up?</p>","choices":[{"t":"An extranet portal: the supplier logs in and sees only the stock data it needs.","to":"good","fb":"Perfect. Controlled outsider access is an extranet."},{"t":"Put the whole stock database on the public website.","to":"bad4","fb":"Anyone, including competitors, could see it."}]},"good":{"text":"<b>Excellent consultancy!</b> A LAN with a star layout, a client–server model, a secure WLAN in the warehouse and an extranet for the supplier. Mr Richardson's stock will never run dry again.","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<b>Not quite.</b> A WAN is for large areas such as different islands. Buildings next to each other form a LAN.","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<b>Risky.</b> Peer-to-peer struggles beyond about 10 computers and each PC must be backed up separately. Client–server suits growth and central backup.","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<b>Impractical.</b> Moving staff need wireless connections. A WLAN with strong encryption is the answer.","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<b>Security failure.</b> Public pages are on the Internet for everyone. Partner access belongs in an extranet with log-ins and limited rights.","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Area first","p":"<p>Decide LAN/MAN/WAN from distance.</p>"},{"h":"Then model and access","p":"<p>Growth and backup suggest client–server. Outsiders with log-ins suggest an extranet.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Network the auto-parts shop (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Help <b>West Bay Auto Parts</b> choose a network. It has 8 computers in one shop.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>What type of network covers one shop?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A LAN","to":"n2","fb":"Yes, one building."},{"t":"A WAN","to":"bad1","fb":"A WAN covers countries."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The supplier wants to log in and check stock. What should the shop give the supplier?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Access to every file on the network","to":"bad2","fb":"Too much access is unsafe."},{"t":"Extranet access with a password","to":"good","fb":"Yes, controlled outsider access."}]},"good":{"text":"<b>Well done!</b> A LAN for the shop and an extranet for the supplier.","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<b>Too big.</b> One shop is a LAN.","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<b>Unsafe.</b> Outsiders should see only what they need, through an extranet.","end":"bad"}}},"up":{"title":"Network the auto-parts shop (Challenge)","intro":"<p>West Bay Auto Parts is expanding: a second branch in North Side and a supplier in Puerto Rico. Make the expert calls.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The two branches (West Bay and North Side) must share one stock database in real time. What do you build?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Connect the two branch LANs across a secure link (e.g. a VPN over the Internet), forming a MAN/WAN.","to":"n2","fb":"Correct. Joining LANs across distance needs a wider network and a secure tunnel."},{"t":"A single WLAN broadcast across the island from West Bay.","to":"bad1","fb":"Wi-Fi does not reach kilometres; you join LANs over wider links."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Where should the shared stock database live?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A separate copy on each branch PC, updated by USB stick weekly.","to":"bad2","fb":"Copies drift apart and stock figures conflict."},{"t":"On a central server (or cloud server) with nightly off-site backups, accessed by both branches.","to":"n3","fb":"Good: one source of truth, backed up off-site for hurricane season."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The link between branches occasionally fails during storms. How do you keep trading?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Add a backup Internet link (e.g. mobile 4G/5G) so traffic can reroute: redundancy, mesh-style.","to":"n4","fb":"Yes: a second path is the mesh principle."},{"t":"Accept closing whenever the link fails.","to":"bad3","fb":"Lost sales; redundancy is affordable."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>The supplier needs stock data. What access do you give?</p>","choices":[{"t":"An extranet account restricted to read-only stock levels, with MFA and logging.","to":"good","fb":"Least privilege plus monitoring."},{"t":"The owner's own administrator password.","to":"bad4","fb":"Sharing admin credentials destroys accountability and security."}]},"good":{"text":"<b>Grade One consultancy.</b> Joined LANs via a secure link, central backed-up server, redundant links and a least-privilege extranet.","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<b>Range problem.</b> Wi-Fi covers tens of metres, not kilometres.","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<b>Data chaos.</b> Separate copies go out of date; a central database avoids conflicts.","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<b>Avoidable loss.</b> A cheap backup link gives a second route.","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<b>Serious breach risk.</b> Never share admin passwords; give each partner its own limited account.","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"From tiny to worldwide","bloom":"Understand","intro":"<p>Arrange these networks from the SMALLEST area covered to the LARGEST.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Kayla's phone, earbuds and smartwatch","tag":"PAN"},{"t":"The computer lab at Frangipani Hill Academy","tag":"LAN"},{"t":"Offices linked across The Valley, Sandy Ground and Blowing Point","tag":"MAN"},{"t":"A bank linking branches on five Caribbean islands","tag":"WAN"},{"t":"The Internet: networks connected worldwide","tag":"WAN"}],"win":"Perfect order: personal, local, metropolitan, wide, worldwide.","tutor":[{"h":"Think in metres","p":"<p>PAN about 10 m, LAN up to a campus, MAN a city, WAN countries.</p>"},{"h":"The biggest of all","p":"<p>The Internet connects all the other networks.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"From tiny to worldwide (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put these from smallest to biggest.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Phone and earbuds (PAN)"},{"t":"School lab (LAN)"},{"t":"Bank branches in many countries (WAN)"}]},"up":{"title":"From tiny to worldwide (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Order these by area from smallest to largest. No labels this time: work out each network's type.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"A Bluetooth game controller paired with a console"},{"t":"Two laptops sharing files in a small office"},{"t":"A college campus network across four neighbouring buildings"},{"t":"Traffic cameras linked across one capital city"},{"t":"A regional airline's booking system linking six islands"},{"t":"The global Internet"}]}},{"type":"mcq","title":"When the server goes down","bloom":"Analyse","q":"<p>At 8:05 a.m. on a Monday, no student at a school in Oranjestad can log in or open their saved work on any of the 60 lab computers. The computers themselves start normally and the printer still prints a test page. The technician suspects one machine is to blame. Which machine is MOST likely to have failed, and what does this show?</p>","options":[{"t":"The printer, because printers control log-ins","fb":"Printers do not manage log-ins, and it still works."},{"t":"One student's PC, because in a peer-to-peer network one PC can stop all the others","fb":"One failed peer only affects its own shared files, not everyone's log-in."},{"t":"The central server, because in a client–server network log-ins and files depend on it: a single point of failure","fb":"Correct. Every client is affected at once, which points to the shared server.","ok":true},{"t":"The Internet, because log-ins always come from the Internet","fb":"School log-ins are handled by the school's own server on its LAN."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Everyone or one?","p":"<p>If everyone loses the same service at once, look for the shared, central part.</p>"},{"h":"What does a server do?","p":"<p>Authentication servers check log-ins; file servers store users' work.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"When the server goes down (Foundation)","q":"<p>In a school's client–server network, the server stops working. Students' PCs still turn on, but nobody can log in or open their files. Why?</p>","options":[{"t":"Every PC needs its own printer","fb":"Printers are not involved here."},{"t":"The server stores the files and checks log-ins for everyone","fb":"Yes: clients depend on the server.","ok":true},{"t":"PCs cannot turn on without a server","fb":"The PCs do turn on; they just cannot reach the server's services."}]},"up":{"title":"When the server goes down (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"<p>After the Monday server failure, the principal suggests replacing the school's client–server network with peer-to-peer so that \"one machine can never stop everything again.\" With 60 lab PCs and 700 students, which is the BEST response?</p>","options":[{"t":"Agree: peer-to-peer is more secure and scales better for 700 users.","fb":"Peer-to-peer is less secure and scales poorly."},{"t":"Disagree: servers never fail, so it must have been the printer.","fb":"Servers can and do fail; that is why redundancy exists."},{"t":"Keep client–server but add redundancy: a backup server, a UPS and regular backups, because central security and roaming log-ins are essential at this size.","fb":"Correct. Fix the single point of failure without losing central management.","ok":true},{"t":"Agree: in peer-to-peer every student can still log in on any machine.","fb":"Without a central server, roaming log-ins and central files disappear."}]}},{"type":"write","title":"Structured question: networking Breadfruit Valley Secondary","bloom":"Analyse","genre":"Structured question: types of networks (12 marks)","stimulus":"<p><b>Breadfruit Valley Secondary</b> in the Turks and Caicos Islands has two computer labs with 30 desktops each, a library with 10 laptops on Wi-Fi, and an office block. Students must log in at any computer and see their own files. The school wants an online portal where only staff can read policies and book rooms, and where parents can log in to see their own child's report.</p>","prompt":"<p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Define</b> the term <i>computer network</i>. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> the type of network (by area) that the school has, and give ONE reason. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> why a client–server network is more suitable than peer-to-peer for this school. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>The library laptops use a WLAN. <b>State</b> ONE advantage and ONE disadvantage of a WLAN compared with a cabled LAN. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Distinguish</b> between the staff part and the parents' part of the portal, using the correct terms. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":90,"max":380,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> A computer network is two or more ... linked so that ...<br><b>(b)</b> The school has a ... because ...<br><b>(c)</b> Client–server is more suitable because ... In addition ...<br><b>(d)</b> One advantage of a WLAN is ... One disadvantage is ...<br><b>(e)</b> The staff-only part is an ..., which ... whereas the parents' part is an ..., which ...</p>","checklist":["My definition mentions linking devices AND sharing resources or communicating.","I named LAN and linked it to the school being on one site.","I gave at least two client–server reasons tied to the scenario (log in anywhere, central security/backup, many users).","I gave one WLAN advantage and one disadvantage, clearly labelled.","I used the words intranet and extranet correctly and compared them with 'whereas'."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Definition of a network","marks":2,"look":["link|connect","share|communicat|resource"],"min":2,"strong":"Clear definition with both parts.","weak":"Say devices are linked (by cable or wireless) so they can share resources and communicate."},{"criterion":"Network type by area","marks":2,"look":["\\bLAN\\b|local area","one (site|building|campus)|small (area|geograph)|same (site|location)|close"],"min":2,"strong":"LAN with a reason about area.","weak":"Name LAN and say the school is on one site or a small area."},{"criterion":"Client–server justification","marks":3,"look":["server","log.?in|any computer|own files","secur|backup|back up|central|manage","many|large|60|700|students"],"min":3,"strong":"Well-linked reasons from the scenario.","weak":"Explain that a central server lets students log in anywhere, gives central security and backup, and copes with many users."},{"criterion":"WLAN advantage and disadvantage","marks":2,"look":["no cable|without cable|mobil|move|portab|easy to add","secur|slow|interfer|range|signal|reliab"],"min":2,"strong":"One advantage and one disadvantage given.","weak":"Give an advantage (mobility, no cables) and a disadvantage (less secure, interference, weaker signal)."},{"criterion":"Intranet vs extranet","marks":3,"look":["intranet","extranet","staff|employee|member","parent|outsider|outside|external"],"min":3,"strong":"Correct terms with a clear distinction.","weak":"Staff-only = intranet; parents (outsiders) with log-ins = extranet. Compare who can access each."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> A computer network is two or more computers or other devices linked together, by cable or wirelessly, so that they can communicate and share resources such as files, printers and an Internet connection.</p><p><b>(b)</b> The school has a LAN (Local Area Network), because all its computers are on one site in a small geographical area (the labs, library and office block on one campus).</p><p><b>(c)</b> Students must be able to log in at any computer and see their own files; this is only possible if the files and user accounts are stored on a central server. A server also lets the technician control security (passwords and access rights) and back up everyone's work in one place. With 70 computers and hundreds of students, peer-to-peer would be hard to manage, insecure and impossible to back up properly.</p><p><b>(d)</b> Advantage: laptops can be used anywhere in the library without cables, and new devices are easy to add. Disadvantage: the WLAN is less secure than cables because signals travel through the air and can be intercepted if not encrypted (it can also suffer interference and be slower).</p><p><b>(e)</b> The staff-only part is an <b>intranet</b>, a private network that only members of the school (staff) can access, for internal information such as policies and room bookings, whereas the parents' part is an <b>extranet</b>, which gives selected outsiders (parents) controlled access with their own log-ins to only the information they need (their own child's report).</p>","key":"<p>(a) 1 mark linked/connected devices; 1 mark share resources or communicate. (b) 1 LAN; 1 reason about small area/one site. (c) 1 mark each for up to three linked reasons: log in anywhere/central files; central security/access control; central backup; many users/scalability. (d) 1 advantage, 1 disadvantage. (e) 1 intranet identified for staff, 1 extranet identified for parents, 1 clear distinction about who has access (members vs selected outsiders).</p>","teacherTip":"Many students write 'WAN' because the school has several buildings; reinforce that one campus is a LAN. In (e), do not award the distinction mark for simply naming the terms: the answer must explain who can access each.","easy":{"title":"Structured question: networking Breadfruit Valley Secondary (Foundation)","genre":"Structured question: types of networks (7 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Define</b> the term <i>computer network</i>. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Is the school's network a LAN or a WAN? Give ONE reason. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> TWO reasons why the school should use a client–server network. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>What is the staff-only part of the portal called: intranet or extranet? <i>(1 mark)</i></li></ol>","frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> A computer network is two or more computers that are ... so that they can ...<br><b>(b)</b> It is a ... because the school ...<br><b>(c)</b> First, a server lets students ... Second, ...<br><b>(d)</b> The staff-only part is called an ...</p>","min":40,"max":220,"checklist":["I said devices are linked and share or communicate.","I chose LAN and said why.","I gave two different client–server reasons.","I named the intranet."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Definition","marks":2,"look":["link|connect","share|communicat|resource"],"min":2,"strong":"Both parts present.","weak":"Linked devices + sharing/communicating."},{"criterion":"LAN with reason","marks":2,"look":["\\bLAN\\b|local","one (site|building|campus)|small|close|same"],"min":2,"strong":"LAN and a reason.","weak":"LAN, because it is on one small site."},{"criterion":"Client–server reasons","marks":2,"look":["log.?in|own files|any computer","secur|backup|back up|central|many"],"min":2,"strong":"Two reasons.","weak":"Log in anywhere; central backup/security."},{"criterion":"Intranet","marks":1,"look":["intranet"],"min":1,"strong":"Correct term.","weak":"Staff-only = intranet."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Two or more computers linked together so they can share resources and communicate.</p><p><b>(b)</b> A LAN, because the school is on one site in a small area.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Students can log in at any computer and get their own files from the server; the technician can back up and secure all work centrally.</p><p><b>(d)</b> An intranet.</p>","key":"<p>(a) 1 mark devices linked/connected (by cable or wireless); 1 mark to share resources or communicate. (b) 1 LAN; 1 reason: the school is on one site/in a small area. (c) 1 mark each for two different reasons: students log in at any computer and reach their own files on the server; central security/access control; central backup. (d) 1 intranet.</p>","teacherTip":"Foundation students often choose WAN because the school has several buildings; remind them that one campus is a LAN. In (c), two marks need two different reasons: 'more secure' and 'safer' count as one point. In (d), credit only 'intranet'; watch for 'Internet', which changes the meaning."},"up":{"title":"Structured question: networking Breadfruit Valley Secondary (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Structured question: types of networks with evaluation (18 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Define</b> the term <i>computer network</i>. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Distinguish</b> between a LAN and a WAN, and state which the school has. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> why a client–server network is more suitable than peer-to-peer for this school. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Discuss</b> the use of a WLAN in the library, giving TWO advantages and TWO disadvantages. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Distinguish</b> between the staff part and the parents' part of the portal, using the correct terms. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>The school's server is a single point of failure. <b>Evaluate</b> this risk and <b>recommend</b> TWO measures, justifying each. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":150,"max":520,"frame":"<p><b>(b)</b> A LAN covers ... whereas a WAN ... The school has ...<br><b>(d)</b> On the one hand ... On the other hand ...<br><b>(f)</b> If the server fails ... This matters because ... I recommend ... because ...</p>","checklist":["I compared LAN and WAN on the same point using 'whereas'.","My client–server reasons are tied to the scenario.","I gave two advantages AND two disadvantages of WLAN.","I used intranet and extranet correctly.","I evaluated the server risk and justified two measures (e.g. backup server, UPS, off-site backup)."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Definition","marks":2,"look":["link|connect","share|communicat|resource"],"min":2,"strong":"Complete definition.","weak":"Linked devices + sharing/communicating."},{"criterion":"LAN vs WAN","marks":3,"look":["\\bLAN\\b|local area","\\bWAN\\b|wide area","whereas|while|but|however|compared","school (has|is|uses) a LAN|LAN.{0,40}school"],"min":3,"strong":"A true distinction plus the school's type.","weak":"Compare area/ownership in one sentence and say the school has a LAN."},{"criterion":"Client–server justification","marks":3,"look":["server","log.?in|any computer|own files","secur|backup|back up|central|manage"],"min":3,"strong":"Linked reasons.","weak":"Log in anywhere, central security, central backup."},{"criterion":"WLAN discussion","marks":4,"look":["no cable|without cable|mobil|move|portab","easy to add|flexib|cheaper|cost","secur|intercept|encrypt","slow|interfer|range|signal|reliab|wall"],"min":3,"strong":"Balanced discussion.","weak":"Give two advantages (mobility, no cables, easy to add) and two disadvantages (security, interference, speed)."},{"criterion":"Intranet vs extranet","marks":3,"look":["intranet","extranet","parent|outsider|external"],"min":3,"strong":"Correct terms and distinction.","weak":"Staff = intranet; parents = extranet with limited access."},{"criterion":"Evaluate server risk","marks":3,"look":["single point|everyone|every (student|user|one)|all (users|students)|whole","backup server|redundan|second server|mirror","\\bUPS\\b|uninterrupt|generator|off.?site|cloud backup|backup"],"min":3,"strong":"Risk explained and two justified measures.","weak":"Explain the impact, then recommend e.g. a backup server and a UPS, saying how each reduces the risk."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Two or more computers or devices linked by cable or wireless so they can communicate and share resources.</p><p><b>(b)</b> A LAN covers a small area such as one building or campus and is usually owned by one organisation, whereas a WAN covers a large area such as several countries and often uses leased links (undersea cables, satellites). The school has a LAN.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Students must log in anywhere and see their own files, which needs central accounts and file storage on a server. The server also gives central security and nightly backups. Peer-to-peer would be insecure and unmanageable with 70 computers.</p><p><b>(d)</b> Advantages: laptops can be used anywhere in the library without cables, and new devices are easy and cheap to add. Disadvantages: radio signals can be intercepted, so strong encryption (WPA2/WPA3) is needed; and Wi-Fi can be slower and affected by interference or thick walls. Overall a WLAN suits the library because mobility matters more there than maximum speed, provided it is secured.</p><p><b>(e)</b> The staff part is an intranet: private to members of the school. The parents' part is an extranet: selected outsiders log in and see only their own child's report.</p><p><b>(f)</b> The server is a single point of failure: if it fails, every student loses log-ins and files at once, which could disrupt SBA deadlines, so the risk is serious even if failures are rare. I recommend (1) a UPS so short Lucayan Power & Water power cuts do not crash the server, and (2) regular off-site or cloud backups (or a backup server) so work can be restored quickly after hardware failure or a hurricane.</p>","key":"<p>(a) 1 linked/connected devices; 1 share resources or communicate. (b) 1 LAN covers a small area/one site (or is owned by one organisation); 1 WAN covers a large area using leased or public links, given as a contrast; 1 the school has a LAN. (c) 1 mark each for up to three linked reasons: log in anywhere with central accounts and files; central security/access control; central backup; manageable for many users (peer-to-peer unmanageable with 70 computers). (d) 1 mark each for two advantages (mobility/no cables; devices easy and cheap to add) and 1 mark each for two disadvantages (signals can be intercepted, so encryption is needed; interference/thick walls; slower or less reliable). (e) 1 intranet for staff; 1 extranet for parents; 1 distinction about who can access each (members vs selected outsiders with log-ins). (f) 1 risk evaluated (a server failure stops every user at once); 1 mark each for two measures, each justified by how it reduces the risk (UPS or generator; backup/redundant server; off-site or cloud backups).</p>","teacherTip":"In (b), the distinction mark needs LAN and WAN compared on the same feature (area or ownership of links); two separate definitions earn 1 at most, plus the mark for saying the school has a LAN. In (d), students often give two advantages but only one real disadvantage, because 'it can be slow' and 'it has a weak signal' are the same point. In (f), each measure must say how it reduces the risk; 'buy a better server' scores 0."}}]},{"id":"S2U2","title":"Network components and transmission media","objectives":["Explain the functions of a network interface card, hub, switch, router, modem and wireless access point (SO 2.2)","Describe repeaters and bridges (enrichment) (SO 2.2)","Describe network software (network operating system, management software, servers) and explain protocols such as Ethernet and TCP/IP (SO 2.2)","Compare twisted pair, coaxial and fibre-optic cable (SO 2.2)","Compare wireless media: Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, infrared, microwave and satellite (SO 2.2)","Explain bandwidth and calculate simple transfer times (SO 2.2)","Distinguish simplex, half-duplex and full-duplex transmission with examples (SO 2.2)"],"videos":[{"title":"Hub vs switch vs router","q":"hub vs switch vs router explained simply"},{"title":"What does a modem do?","q":"modem vs router difference explained"},{"title":"Twisted pair, coaxial and fibre-optic cables","q":"twisted pair coaxial fiber optic cable comparison"},{"title":"Simplex, half-duplex and full-duplex","q":"simplex half duplex full duplex explained examples"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Meet the network hardware","html":"<p>In Unit 1 you learned the <i>types</i> of networks. Now we open the box: <b>Specific Objective 2.2</b> asks you to explain the <b>functions of the basic components</b> of a network and the <b>transmission media</b> that carry data.</p>\n<p>Every network, from your home Wi-Fi in Gros Islet to the bank's WAN, needs three things:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Devices</b> that send and receive data: computers, phones, printers, servers.</li>\n<li><b>Connecting hardware</b> that moves data to the right place: network cards, switches, routers, modems, access points.</li>\n<li><b>Transmission media</b>: the cables or wireless signals the data travels through.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 440 150\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Path from a laptop's network card through a wireless access point, switch, router and modem to the ISP and the Internet\">\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"4\" y=\"50\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\" rx=\"5\"/><rect x=\"82\" y=\"50\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\" rx=\"5\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\"/>\n<rect x=\"160\" y=\"50\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\" rx=\"5\"/><rect x=\"238\" y=\"50\" width=\"58\" height=\"40\" rx=\"5\" stroke=\"#F5C542\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<rect x=\"316\" y=\"50\" width=\"52\" height=\"40\" rx=\"5\"/><ellipse cx=\"408\" cy=\"70\" rx=\"30\" ry=\"26\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\"/>\n<path d=\"M62,70 q5,-8 10,0 t10,0\" /><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"70\"/><line x1=\"218\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"238\" y2=\"70\"/><line x1=\"296\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"316\" y2=\"70\"/><line x1=\"368\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"378\" y2=\"70\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 2\"/>\n</g>\n<g font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"33\" y=\"67\">Laptop</text><text x=\"33\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"9\">(NIC)</text>\n<text x=\"111\" y=\"67\">Wireless</text><text x=\"111\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"9\">access point</text>\n<text x=\"189\" y=\"74\">Switch</text><text x=\"267\" y=\"74\">Router</text><text x=\"342\" y=\"74\">Modem</text>\n<text x=\"408\" y=\"67\" font-size=\"9\">ISP and</text><text x=\"408\" y=\"79\" font-size=\"9\">Internet</text>\n<text x=\"72\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"9\">radio</text><text x=\"150\" y=\"40\" font-size=\"9\">cable</text>\n<text x=\"111\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"9\">connects wireless</text><text x=\"111\" y=\"123\" font-size=\"9\">devices to the LAN</text>\n<text x=\"189\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"9\">links devices</text><text x=\"189\" y=\"123\" font-size=\"9\">inside the LAN</text>\n<text x=\"267\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"9\">links the LAN to</text><text x=\"267\" y=\"123\" font-size=\"9\">other networks</text>\n<text x=\"342\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"9\">converts signals</text><text x=\"342\" y=\"123\" font-size=\"9\">for the line</text>\n</g></svg><figcaption>Your data's journey out of the house or school. In many homes, one box from the ISP combines the access point, switch, router and modem.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>When you send a TikTok from your laptop at home, the data leaves through your laptop's <b>network interface card</b>, travels by Wi-Fi to a <b>wireless access point</b>, passes through a <b>switch</b> and a <b>router</b>, is converted by a <b>modem</b> (or fibre terminal) for the line to your <b>Internet Service Provider</b>, and then crosses the Internet. That all happens in a fraction of a second.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> CXC questions usually ask for the <b>function</b> of a device: what job it does. \"A router connects networks\" earns more than \"a router is a box with lights\".</div>","plain":"<p>A network needs devices (like laptops), connecting boxes (like a router) and something for data to travel through (cables or Wi-Fi). Think of a postal service: houses are the devices, sorting offices are the switches and routers, and the roads are the cables and wireless links.</p>","deeper":"<p>In most homes and small businesses the ISP supplies <b>one box</b> that is actually four devices in one case: a <b>modem</b> (or optical network terminal for fibre), a <b>router</b>, a small <b>switch</b> (the yellow or numbered ports at the back) and a <b>wireless access point</b> (the antennas). People call the whole thing \"the router\", but in an exam you must name and explain each <b>function</b> separately. Big organisations such as a hotel at Anse Chastanet or the government buildings in Castries use separate, more powerful devices for each job, so they can be upgraded and managed independently.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Network cards and wireless access points","html":"<h4>Network Interface Card (NIC)</h4>\n<p>A <b>NIC</b> (network interface card, also called a network adapter) is the hardware that <b>allows a device to connect to a network</b>. It takes the data given to it by the CPU and <b>translates it into a form that can travel over the cable or radio link</b>, sends it towards its destination, and translates incoming signals back into data the computer can use. Older NICs were expansion cards plugged into the motherboard or a USB port; today most are a chip built into the motherboard.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <b>wired NIC</b> has an Ethernet port where an <b>RJ45</b> connector clicks in.</li>\n<li>A <b>wireless NIC</b> has a small antenna for Wi-Fi. Phones and laptops have them built in.</li>\n<li>Every NIC has a unique hardware address called a <b>MAC address</b> (Media Access Control), written in hexadecimal, for example <code>3C:52:82:1A:9F:04</code>. Switches use MAC addresses to deliver data to the right device.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Wireless Access Point (WAP)</h4>\n<p>A <b>wireless access point</b> is a device that <b>lets wireless devices connect to a wired network</b>. It sends and receives radio signals (Wi-Fi) and passes the data on to the cabled LAN.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A school places access points in each block so that tablets can reach Google Classroom from every classroom.</li>\n<li>The GAIA terminal uses many access points so travellers keep their signal as they walk to the gate.</li>\n<li>An access point broadcasts a network name (<b>SSID</b>) and should use encryption such as <b>WPA2 or WPA3</b> with a strong password.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> A WAP does not give you Internet by itself. It connects wireless devices to the <b>LAN</b>; the router and modem connect the LAN to the Internet.</div>\n<p><button type=\"button\" class=\"btn ghost\" data-gx=\"opengame\" data-page=\"games/winsim.html?level=1\" data-id=\"winsim\">🪟 Try it now: run ipconfig /all in the Windows Desktop Lab and find your NIC's physical (MAC) address</button></p>","plain":"<p>A <b>NIC</b> is the part inside your computer or phone that lets it join a network, by cable or Wi-Fi. Each one has its own ID number called a MAC address, like a serial number. A <b>wireless access point</b> is the box on the wall or ceiling that lets Wi-Fi devices join the wired network.</p>","deeper":"<p>A device can have <b>several NICs</b>: a laptop often has one Wi-Fi adapter, one Bluetooth adapter and an Ethernet port, each with its own MAC address. MAC addresses are meant to be permanent, but modern phones show a <b>random MAC</b> to each Wi-Fi network for privacy, so shops and malls cannot track you as you walk around. Do not confuse the <b>MAC address</b> (hardware, used inside a LAN by switches) with the <b>IP address</b> (logical, used by routers to move data between networks, Unit 4). Exam trap: \"Mac\" the Apple computer has nothing to do with a MAC address.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Who does what?","bloom":"Remember","intro":"<p>Match each network component to its main function.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Network interface card (NIC)","r":"Lets a device connect to a network"},{"l":"Wireless access point","r":"Lets Wi-Fi devices join a wired LAN"},{"l":"Switch","r":"Sends data only to the device it is addressed to inside a LAN"},{"l":"Router","r":"Connects different networks and forwards data between them"},{"l":"Modem","r":"Converts digital signals to analogue for the line and back again"}],"win":"Matched! Now you know each device by its job.","tutor":[{"h":"Think 'job description'","p":"<p>Each device has one main job. Ask: does it connect a device, connect wireless devices, connect devices inside the LAN, or connect networks?</p>"},{"h":"Modem = MOdulator-DEModulator","p":"<p>It changes the form of the signal for the line.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Who does what? (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"NIC","r":"Lets a computer join a network"},{"l":"Router","r":"Connects your network to the Internet"},{"l":"Wireless access point","r":"Gives Wi-Fi to devices"}]},"up":{"title":"Who does what? (Challenge)","bloom":"Understand","pairs":[{"l":"NIC","r":"Has a unique MAC address burned in"},{"l":"Hub","r":"Copies incoming data to every port"},{"l":"Switch","r":"Learns MAC addresses and forwards to one port"},{"l":"Router","r":"Uses IP addresses to choose a path between networks"},{"l":"Modem","r":"Modulates and demodulates signals for telephone or cable lines"},{"l":"Repeater","r":"Regenerates a weak signal to extend its range"},{"l":"Bridge","r":"Joins two LAN segments and filters traffic between them"}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"Hubs and switches","html":"<p>In a star network, every cable goes to a central box. That box is either a <b>hub</b> or a <b>switch</b>, and the difference is a favourite exam question.</p>\n<figure class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 420 170\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Left: a hub sends a message from PC A to every port. Right: a switch sends it only to PC C.\">\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"75\" y=\"65\" width=\"50\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\"/><rect x=\"295\" y=\"65\" width=\"50\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" stroke=\"#F5C542\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"10\" width=\"40\" height=\"24\" rx=\"3\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\"/><rect x=\"150\" y=\"10\" width=\"40\" height=\"24\" rx=\"3\"/><rect x=\"10\" y=\"120\" width=\"40\" height=\"24\" rx=\"3\"/><rect x=\"150\" y=\"120\" width=\"40\" height=\"24\" rx=\"3\"/>\n<rect x=\"230\" y=\"10\" width=\"40\" height=\"24\" rx=\"3\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\"/><rect x=\"370\" y=\"10\" width=\"40\" height=\"24\" rx=\"3\"/><rect x=\"230\" y=\"120\" width=\"40\" height=\"24\" rx=\"3\"/><rect x=\"370\" y=\"120\" width=\"40\" height=\"24\" rx=\"3\"/>\n<line x1=\"50\" y1=\"28\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"65\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"28\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"65\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><line x1=\"50\" y1=\"126\" x2=\"80\" y2=\"91\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"126\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"91\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n<line x1=\"270\" y1=\"28\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"65\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/><line x1=\"370\" y1=\"28\" x2=\"340\" y2=\"65\"/><line x1=\"270\" y1=\"126\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"91\"/><line x1=\"370\" y1=\"126\" x2=\"340\" y2=\"91\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"/>\n</g>\n<g font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"100\" y=\"82\">HUB</text><text x=\"320\" y=\"82\">SWITCH</text>\n<text x=\"30\" y=\"26\">PC A</text><text x=\"170\" y=\"26\">PC B</text><text x=\"30\" y=\"136\">PC D</text><text x=\"170\" y=\"136\">PC C</text>\n<text x=\"250\" y=\"26\">PC A</text><text x=\"390\" y=\"26\">PC B</text><text x=\"250\" y=\"136\">PC D</text><text x=\"390\" y=\"136\">PC C</text>\n<text x=\"100\" y=\"164\">A → C: copied to EVERY port</text><text x=\"320\" y=\"164\">A → C: sent only to C (MAC address)</text>\n</g></svg><figcaption>A hub shouts the message to everyone; a switch reads the destination MAC address and delivers it only where it is needed.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Hub</h4>\n<ul><li>A simple device with many ports that connects devices in a LAN.</li>\n<li>When data arrives, it <b>sends a copy to every port</b>. It cannot read addresses, so every device receives the data and must check whether it is meant for them.</li>\n<li>This wastes bandwidth, causes <b>collisions</b> as the network grows, and is less secure, since every device sees all traffic.</li></ul>\n<h4>Switch</h4>\n<ul><li>Also connects devices in a LAN, but it is <b>intelligent</b>.</li>\n<li>It uses <b>packet switching</b>: it receives each packet (frame) and forwards it to the destination device. (A switch is sometimes called a switching hub or MAC bridge.)</li>\n<li>It reads the <b>destination MAC address</b> of each piece of data and sends it <b>only to the port</b> where that device is connected.</li>\n<li>Result: less unnecessary traffic, better speed, fewer collisions and better privacy.</li></ul>\n<table><tr><th></th><th>Hub</th><th>Switch</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sends data to</td><td>All ports</td><td>Only the destination port</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reads MAC addresses?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Performance in a busy lab</td><td>Slows down</td><td>Stays fast</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Today</td><td>Mostly obsolete</td><td>Standard in every LAN</td></tr></table>\n<p>So when the Palmetto Point Secondary lab has 30 PCs, the technician installs a <b>switch</b>, never a hub.</p>","plain":"<p>A <b>hub</b> is like a teacher who reads every note out loud to the whole class, even if it is meant for one student. A <b>switch</b> is like a teacher who walks over and hands the note only to the right student. Switches are faster and more private, so they have replaced hubs.</p>","deeper":"<p>Switches learn by listening. Each time a device sends something, the switch notes which port that MAC address came from and stores it in a <b>MAC address table</b>. Next time data is addressed to that MAC, it goes straight to the right port. If the switch has not learned an address yet, it does what a hub does once (sends to all ports) and then remembers the reply. This explains a Grade One answer: a switch <b>reduces collisions and improves security</b> because each conversation stays on its own port. A <b>managed switch</b> can even split one physical LAN into separate virtual LANs (VLANs), for example keeping the school office separate from the student lab.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Routers, modems, repeaters and bridges","html":"<h4>Router</h4>\n<p>A <b>router</b> <b>connects two or more different networks</b> and <b>forwards data packets between them</b>, choosing the best path using <b>IP addresses</b>. Your home router connects your home LAN to your ISP's network and so to the Internet. Big routers at ISPs and in undersea cable stations direct Internet traffic between islands and continents.</p>\n<p>A packet may be passed from router to router across many networks (an <b>internetwork</b>, such as the Internet) until it reaches its destination. Inside a home or office, the router also <b>assigns a local IP address to each device</b> on the network, so it knows where to deliver returning data.</p>\n<p>Home routers usually also give each device an IP address automatically, share one public Internet address between all devices, and include a basic <b>firewall</b>.</p>\n<h4>Modem</h4>\n<p><b>Modem</b> stands for <b>MOdulator–DEModulator</b>. It <b>converts digital signals from a computer into analogue signals</b> that can travel along telephone or cable-TV lines (modulation), and converts incoming analogue signals <b>back into digital</b> (demodulation).</p>\n<ul><li>There are two common types: a <b>DSL (Digital Subscriber Line) modem</b> uses the telephone line, and a <b>cable modem</b> uses the coaxial cable-TV line. The sending modem <b>modulates</b>; the receiving modem <b>demodulates</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Wireless modems</b> (for example in a 4G/5G router or a mobile broadband dongle) convert digital data into radio signals and back.</li>\n<li>Homes on <b>fibre</b> use an optical network terminal (ONT) instead, which converts light signals to electrical ones. People still often call it \"the modem\".</li></ul>\n<h4>Enrichment: repeater and bridge</h4>\n<ul><li>A <b>repeater</b> <b>regenerates (boosts) a weakening signal</b> so it can travel further. A Wi-Fi extender in a large concrete villa is a wireless repeater; long undersea cables have repeaters along the sea bed.</li>\n<li>A <b>bridge</b> <b>joins two LAN segments</b> (for example two floors of an office) and only passes data across when it is meant for the other side.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> Switch = inside one network (MAC addresses). Router = between networks (IP addresses). Modem = changes the signal type for the line.</div>","plain":"<p>A <b>router</b> is like the traffic police at a roundabout: it sends data down the right road to reach another network, like the Internet. A <b>modem</b> is a translator: it changes your computer's digital signals into a form that can travel down the phone or cable line, and back again. A <b>repeater</b> just makes a weak signal strong again.</p>","deeper":"<p>Why do routers use IP addresses and not MAC addresses? MAC addresses are like a person's name: unique, but they don't tell you <i>where</i> someone lives. IP addresses are like a postal address: they show which <b>network</b> a device is on, so routers can send data in the right direction across the world, one \"hop\" at a time. You can watch the hops: the <code>tracert</code> command in Windows lists every router between you and a website. On an island like ours, the first few hops are local, then the data jumps through an undersea cable landing station. Link: IP addresses and DNS are explained in Unit 4.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Which device solves it?","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Each person has a problem. Choose the device that fixes it.</p>","items":[{"text":"Ms Hanley's new desktop has no way to plug into the office network cable.","options":["Network interface card","Modem","Router"],"answer":0,"fb":"A NIC gives the device a network port."},{"text":"Tablets in the library cannot join the school's cabled LAN.","options":["Repeater","Wireless access point","Hub"],"answer":1,"fb":"A WAP connects wireless devices to the wired LAN."},{"text":"The school lab must connect 30 PCs and keep traffic efficient.","options":["Switch","Hub","Modem"],"answer":0,"fb":"A switch connects LAN devices and forwards only to the right port."},{"text":"A small office LAN needs to connect to the ISP's network to reach the Internet.","options":["Router","Switch","NIC"],"answer":0,"fb":"Connecting different networks is a router's job."},{"text":"The computer's digital signals must travel over an old telephone line.","options":["Bridge","Modem","Switch"],"answer":1,"fb":"Modulation/demodulation is the modem's job."},{"text":"Wi-Fi is too weak in the back bedroom of a large concrete house in Cayon.","options":["NIC","Modem","Repeater (Wi-Fi extender)"],"answer":2,"fb":"A repeater boosts and re-sends the signal."}],"win":"Great troubleshooting: right device, right job.","tutor":[{"h":"Find the verb","p":"<p>Join a device? NIC. Join wireless devices? WAP. Link devices in the LAN? Switch. Link networks? Router. Convert the signal? Modem. Boost? Repeater.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which device solves it? (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Pick the device.</p>","items":[{"text":"Connect your home network to the Internet.","options":["Router","Mouse"],"answer":0,"fb":"Routers connect networks."},{"text":"Let a laptop join Wi-Fi in a café.","options":["Printer","Wireless access point"],"answer":1,"fb":"The WAP provides Wi-Fi."},{"text":"Connect 20 PCs in one lab.","options":["Modem","Switch"],"answer":1,"fb":"A switch links devices in a LAN."}]},"up":{"title":"Which device solves it? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Each case has a subtle clue. Choose the BEST device.</p>","items":[{"text":"A lab still uses an old hub and slows down badly when 30 students upload work at once. What should replace it?","options":["A modem","A switch","A second hub"],"answer":1,"fb":"A switch stops broadcasting to every port and cuts collisions."},{"text":"An office has two LAN segments on different floors and wants to join them while keeping local traffic on each floor.","options":["Modem","Hub","Bridge"],"answer":2,"fb":"A bridge joins segments and filters traffic."},{"text":"A home on fibre needs a device to convert light signals to electrical ones before the router.","options":["Hub","ONT (optical network terminal)","Repeater"],"answer":1,"fb":"Fibre uses an ONT instead of a DSL/cable modem."},{"text":"A cable run between two school blocks is 160 m long and the signal is unreliable at the far end.","options":["Modem","Repeater (or a switch half-way)","Second NIC"],"answer":1,"fb":"Twisted pair is limited to about 100 m per run; regenerate the signal."},{"text":"Which device decides the path data takes across the Internet using IP addresses?","options":["Switch","Access point","Router"],"answer":2,"fb":"Routers route between networks using IP addresses."},{"text":"Which device uses MAC addresses to forward frames only to the correct port?","options":["Modem","Switch","Hub"],"answer":1,"fb":"Switches learn and use MAC addresses."}]}},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Hub or switch?","bloom":"Understand","intro":"<p>Your teacher wrote statements about the two central devices on cards. Sort them.</p>","main":"Does this describe a hub or a switch?","bins":["Hub","Switch"],"items":[{"t":"Cannot tell one device from another.","bin":0,"fb":"A hub is 'dumb'."},{"t":"Sends data only to the port of the intended device.","bin":1,"fb":"Targeted delivery is the switch's advantage."},{"t":"Sends incoming data out of every port.","bin":0,"fb":"Broadcasting to all is the hub's behaviour."},{"t":"Every computer sees every message, which is a privacy risk.","bin":0,"fb":"Hubs copy everything to everyone."},{"t":"Keeps a table of which device is on which port.","bin":1,"fb":"The MAC address table belongs to the switch."},{"t":"The standard central device in a modern school lab.","bin":1,"fb":"Switches replaced hubs."},{"t":"Reads the destination MAC address.","bin":1,"fb":"Only a switch reads addresses."},{"t":"Causes more collisions as devices are added.","bin":0,"fb":"Hubs share the bandwidth and collide."}],"win":"Sorted. Hub = shout to everyone; switch = deliver to one.","tutor":[{"h":"Dumb or smart?","p":"<p>A hub cannot read addresses; a switch can.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Hub or switch? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Sends data to every computer.","bin":0,"fb":"Hub."},{"t":"Sends data only to the right computer.","bin":1,"fb":"Switch."},{"t":"Is mostly obsolete today.","bin":0,"fb":"Hub."},{"t":"Is used in modern labs.","bin":1,"fb":"Switch."}]},"up":{"title":"Hub or switch? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","bins":["Hub","Switch","Both"],"items":[{"t":"Broadcasts every frame, so bandwidth is shared by all.","bin":0,"fb":"Hub."},{"t":"Can split the LAN into virtual LANs (when managed).","bin":1,"fb":"VLANs need a managed switch."},{"t":"Has several ports and connects devices in a star LAN.","bin":2,"fb":"Both do this."},{"t":"Uses MAC addresses to make forwarding decisions.","bin":1,"fb":"Switch."},{"t":"Lets two pairs of devices communicate at the same time without colliding.","bin":1,"fb":"Switches keep conversations separate."},{"t":"Is a single point of failure in a star topology.","bin":2,"fb":"Whichever is in the centre, its failure stops the star."},{"t":"Works at the level of electrical signals only, with no addressing.","bin":0,"fb":"Hub."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"Network software, servers and protocols","html":"<p>Cables and boxes are not enough. A network also needs <b>software</b> and agreed <b>rules</b> before devices can work together.</p>\n<h4>Network software</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Network operating system (NOS)</b>: an operating system that supports network tasks such as logging in users, sharing files and sharing printers. Today these features are built into ordinary operating systems like Windows, and server versions add tools for managing hundreds of users.</li>\n<li><b>Network management software</b>: utility software used by the network administrator to <b>set up and manage users and devices</b>, <b>monitor</b> how the network is being used, <b>diagnose and fix problems</b>, and <b>distribute software</b> and updates to every computer at once.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Servers</h4>\n<p>A <b>server</b> combines hardware and software to provide a service to the network: a <b>file server</b> stores and retrieves users' files, a <b>print server</b> manages print jobs for shared printers, and other servers handle log-ins, e-mail or web pages (Unit 1).</p>\n<h4>Protocols</h4>\n<p>A <b>protocol</b> is an <b>agreed set of rules</b> that controls how data is formatted, sent and received. If two devices follow the same protocols, they can communicate, even if different companies made them. A laptop made in China can talk to a router made in the USA because both follow the same rules.</p>\n<table><tr><th>Protocol</th><th>What its rules cover</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Ethernet</b></td><td>How devices in a wired LAN are connected by cable and send data over it</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Wi-Fi</b> (IEEE 802.11)</td><td>How devices communicate over a wireless LAN</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>TCP/IP</b> (Transmission Control Protocol / Internet Protocol)</td><td>How data is broken into packets, addressed with IP addresses, sent across networks and reassembled correctly: the foundation of the Internet</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>HTTP / HTTPS</b></td><td>How web pages are requested and sent (Unit 4)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>FTP</b></td><td>How files are uploaded and downloaded (Unit 4)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>VoIP</b></td><td>How voice calls travel over the Internet (Unit 4)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Telnet</b></td><td>An early protocol for controlling a remote computer by typed commands; it sends everything unencrypted, so it has been replaced by secure alternatives</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> A router is a <b>device</b>, not a protocol. If asked for an example of a protocol, give Ethernet, TCP/IP, HTTP or FTP.</div>","plain":"<p>Networks need software as well as hardware. The network operating system lets people log in and share files and printers; management software helps the technician control and fix the network. <b>Protocols</b> are the agreed rules every device follows so they can understand each other, like everyone agreeing to drive on the right side of the road.</p>","deeper":"<p>Protocols are organised in <b>layers</b>, each doing one job and relying on the layer below. When you load a web page at school: <b>HTTP</b> describes the request, <b>TCP</b> splits it into numbered packets and checks they all arrive, <b>IP</b> addresses each packet so routers can forward it, and <b>Ethernet</b> or <b>Wi-Fi</b> carries it across the local link. Because of layering, the same HTTP request works whether you are on cable, Wi-Fi or 5G. This also explains why replacing an insecure protocol (Telnet, plain FTP, HTTP) with a secure one (SSH, SFTP, HTTPS) does not require new cables: only the rules at one layer change.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Protocol or not?","bloom":"Understand","intro":"<p>Match each protocol to what its rules control.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Ethernet","r":"Connecting devices by cable in a wired LAN"},{"l":"TCP/IP","r":"Splitting data into packets, addressing and delivering them across networks"},{"l":"HTTP","r":"Requesting and sending web pages"},{"l":"FTP","r":"Uploading and downloading files"},{"l":"VoIP","r":"Carrying voice calls over the Internet"}],"win":"Matched. Protocols are rules; devices follow them.","tutor":[{"h":"Protocol = rules","p":"<p>Ask what activity the rules control: cabling, packets, web pages, files or calls.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Protocol or not? (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"HTTP","r":"Sending web pages"},{"l":"FTP","r":"Moving files"},{"l":"Ethernet","r":"Wired LAN connections"}]},"up":{"title":"Protocol or not? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Match each statement to the correct item.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"An agreed set of rules for communication","r":"Protocol"},{"l":"A device that forwards packets between networks","r":"Router (a device, not a protocol)"},{"l":"Utility software to monitor users and distribute updates","r":"Network management software"},{"l":"Old remote-login protocol that sends passwords unencrypted","r":"Telnet"},{"l":"Foundation protocol suite of the Internet","r":"TCP/IP"},{"l":"Server that manages print jobs for shared printers","r":"Print server"}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"Wired media: twisted pair, coaxial and fibre","html":"<p>A <b>transmission medium</b> is the <b>pathway that carries information from sender to receiver</b>, using electrical or electromagnetic signals. It is also called a <b>communication channel</b>. Media come in two forms: <b>wired</b> (network cables or public telephone lines) and <b>wireless</b> (radio waves or light rays). <b>Guided (wired)</b> media keep the signal inside a cable.</p>\n<figure class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 420 160\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Cross-sections of twisted pair, coaxial and fibre-optic cables\">\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<circle cx=\"70\" cy=\"70\" r=\"46\"/>\n<circle cx=\"52\" cy=\"52\" r=\"8\" stroke=\"#F5C542\"/><circle cx=\"64\" cy=\"52\" r=\"8\" stroke=\"#F5C542\"/><circle cx=\"82\" cy=\"58\" r=\"8\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\"/><circle cx=\"90\" cy=\"70\" r=\"8\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\"/>\n<circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"82\" r=\"8\"/><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"92\" r=\"8\"/><circle cx=\"80\" cy=\"88\" r=\"8\" stroke=\"#F5C542\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\"/><circle cx=\"92\" cy=\"96\" r=\"8\" stroke=\"#F5C542\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\"/>\n<circle cx=\"210\" cy=\"70\" r=\"46\"/><circle cx=\"210\" cy=\"70\" r=\"36\" stroke-dasharray=\"3 2\"/><circle cx=\"210\" cy=\"70\" r=\"26\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\"/><circle cx=\"210\" cy=\"70\" r=\"6\" stroke=\"#F5C542\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n<circle cx=\"350\" cy=\"70\" r=\"46\"/><circle cx=\"350\" cy=\"70\" r=\"30\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\"/><circle cx=\"350\" cy=\"70\" r=\"12\" stroke=\"#F5C542\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n</g>\n<g font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"70\" y=\"134\" font-weight=\"700\">Twisted pair</text><text x=\"70\" y=\"147\" font-size=\"9\">4 twisted pairs of copper</text>\n<text x=\"210\" y=\"134\" font-weight=\"700\">Coaxial</text><text x=\"210\" y=\"147\" font-size=\"9\">copper core, insulation, braided shield</text>\n<text x=\"350\" y=\"134\" font-weight=\"700\">Fibre-optic</text><text x=\"350\" y=\"147\" font-size=\"9\">glass core carries light</text>\n<text x=\"350\" y=\"74\" font-size=\"9\">core</text><text x=\"350\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"9\">cladding</text>\n</g></svg><figcaption>Guided (wired) media. Copper carries electrical signals; fibre carries pulses of light through a glass core.</figcaption></figure>\n<table><tr><th>Medium</th><th>How it works</th><th>Strengths</th><th>Weaknesses</th><th>Typical use</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Twisted pair</b> (UTP/STP)</td><td>Pairs of insulated copper wires twisted together to reduce interference; RJ45 plugs</td><td>Cheap, flexible, easy to install</td><td>About 100 m per run; affected by electrical interference</td><td>School and office LANs (Ethernet), telephone lines</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Coaxial</b></td><td>A central copper core, insulation, a braided metal shield and an outer jacket</td><td>Better shielded than twisted pair; carries signals further</td><td>Thicker, stiffer and more costly than twisted pair</td><td>Cable TV and cable Internet, CCTV</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Fibre-optic</b></td><td>Pulses of <b>light</b> travel through very thin strands of glass or plastic</td><td>Highest bandwidth; very long distances with little signal loss; immune to electrical interference and lightning; hard to tap</td><td>Most expensive; fragile; needs specialist skills to join (splice)</td><td>Internet backbones, links between buildings, fibre-to-the-home, undersea cables</td></tr></table>\n<p><b>Twisted pair</b> is also the cable of the traditional telephone network; a higher grade is used for LAN wiring because it is cheaper than coaxial cable. <b>Coaxial</b> carries cable television and broadband Internet. <b>Fibre-optic</b> cable is a bundle of glass fibres, each carrying light; telephone companies now use it widely.</p>\n<table><tr><th>Advantages of fibre-optic over twisted pair and coaxial</th><th>Disadvantages of fibre-optic</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1. Carries significantly more signals than wire cables<br>2. Faster data transmission<br>3. Less affected by noise from other devices, such as a photocopier<br>4. Better security for signals during transmission<br>5. Smaller size: much thinner and lighter</td><td>1. Costs more than twisted pair and coaxial<br>2. Difficult to install and modify</td></tr></table>\n<h4>Caribbean link</h4>\n<p>Our islands are connected to the rest of the world mainly by <b>submarine fibre-optic cables</b> lying on the sea bed. They carry far more data than satellites can, with much less delay. That is why a damaged undersea cable can slow the Internet across a whole island.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> If a question mentions <b>long distance</b>, <b>very high bandwidth</b>, <b>lightning</b> or <b>electrical interference</b> (for example near heavy machinery or a power station), the answer is almost always <b>fibre-optic</b>.</div>","plain":"<p>There are three main kinds of network cable. <b>Twisted pair</b> is the everyday cable in school labs: cheap but short range. <b>Coaxial</b> is the round TV cable: better protected. <b>Fibre-optic</b> sends light through glass: super fast and long distance, but expensive.</p>","deeper":"<p>Why twist the wires? Each wire picks up electrical noise from nearby cables and motors. Twisting a pair means both wires pick up almost exactly the same noise, which the receiver cancels out. <b>STP</b> (shielded twisted pair) adds foil for extra protection in noisy places like a factory. Why is fibre immune? Light is not affected by electromagnetic fields, and glass does not conduct electricity, so a lightning strike on one building cannot travel down a fibre link to the next. Trap: fibre is not \"faster\" because light is faster than electricity in a wire (both travel at large fractions of the speed of light); it is better because it carries <b>far more data</b> (bandwidth) over <b>far longer distances</b> with less loss.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Wireless media: through the air","html":"<p><b>Unguided (wireless)</b> media send signals through the air using radio waves, microwaves or infrared light.</p>\n<table><tr><th>Medium</th><th>Range and needs</th><th>Examples</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Radio waves</b></td><td>Electromagnetic waves sent through the air without wires; travel at the speed of light</td><td>Televisions, radios, mobile phones</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Broadcast radio</b></td><td>Over-the-air radio for direct reception by the general public</td><td>FM radio stations</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Cellular radio</b></td><td>A network of transmitters, each serving a small area (cell); the phone switches frequencies automatically as it moves between cells</td><td>Mobile phone networks</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Wi-Fi</b> (radio)</td><td>Tens of metres from an access point; passes through walls but weakens</td><td>School and café WLANs, home Wi-Fi</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Bluetooth</b> (short-range radio)</td><td>About 10 m; low power; devices must be paired</td><td>Earbuds, smartwatches, car hands-free, PANs</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Infrared</b></td><td>A few metres; needs a clear <b>line of sight</b>; cannot pass through walls</td><td>TV and air-conditioner remotes</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Microwave</b></td><td>A beam of radio waves at microwave frequencies; up to about 50 km between dishes on towers or hilltops; shorter wavelength than ordinary radio, so it cannot get round or through obstacles: needs <b>line of sight</b>; heavy rain can weaken it</td><td>Links between cell towers; links between islands or across mountains</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Satellite</b></td><td>A ground station beams a signal up to a satellite (often in a stationary, geostationary orbit), which sends it back down to a receiving station, possibly in another country, like a giant mirror in space. Covers huge areas, including the sea; the trip to space and back causes <b>delay (latency)</b>; costly</td><td>Ships, remote areas, emergency communications after hurricanes, satellite TV</td></tr></table>\n<h4>Advantages and disadvantages of wireless media</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>+</b> No cables to lay: ideal for mobile users, across water or rough terrain, and for quick set-up after a disaster.</li>\n<li><b>–</b> Signals can be intercepted, so encryption is needed.</li>\n<li><b>–</b> Affected by interference, obstacles, distance and weather.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>After Hurricane Irma in 2017, many cables and towers were damaged. <b>Satellite</b> phones and satellite Internet terminals were vital for emergency teams in the first days, because they need no local infrastructure.</p>","plain":"<p>Wireless media send data through the air. <b>Wi-Fi</b> covers a building, <b>Bluetooth</b> covers a few metres, <b>infrared</b> needs a straight line like a TV remote, <b>microwave</b> beams between towers on hills, and <b>satellite</b> covers huge areas from space but has a delay.</p>","deeper":"<p>Traditional <b>geostationary</b> satellites orbit about 36,000 km above the equator, so a signal's round trip up and down takes roughly half a second: fine for TV, annoying in a video call. Newer <b>low-Earth-orbit</b> satellite services fly only a few hundred to about 1,200 km up, cutting the delay to something close to cable, which is why they are being used by ships, remote communities and relief teams. Exam trap: <b>infrared</b> and <b>microwave</b> both need line of sight, but at very different ranges (a living room vs kilometres between hilltops). Link: Unit 3 explores Bluetooth, Wi-Fi, mobile data and GPS from a user's point of view.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Wired or wireless?","bloom":"Understand","intro":"<p>Sort each transmission medium or feature.</p>","main":"Is this guided (wired) or unguided (wireless)?","bins":["Guided (wired)","Unguided (wireless)"],"items":[{"t":"Microwave dish on a hilltop tower","bin":1,"fb":"Line-of-sight microwave."},{"t":"Satellite link","bin":1,"fb":"Signals through space."},{"t":"Infrared remote control","bin":1,"fb":"Infrared light through the air."},{"t":"Coaxial cable","bin":0,"fb":"Copper cable: guided."},{"t":"Undersea cable between Jamaica and Puerto Rico","bin":0,"fb":"A submarine fibre cable is guided."},{"t":"Bluetooth","bin":1,"fb":"Short-range radio."},{"t":"Fibre-optic cable","bin":0,"fb":"Light inside glass: guided."},{"t":"Twisted pair with RJ45 plugs","bin":0,"fb":"Copper pairs: guided."}],"win":"Correct. Guided media keep signals inside a cable; unguided media send them through air or space.","tutor":[{"h":"Inside a cable?","p":"<p>If the signal is trapped in copper or glass, it is guided. If it travels through air or space, it is unguided.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Wired or wireless? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Fibre-optic cable","bin":0,"fb":"Cable."},{"t":"Wi-Fi","bin":1,"fb":"Radio."},{"t":"Twisted pair cable","bin":0,"fb":"Cable."},{"t":"Bluetooth","bin":1,"fb":"Radio."}]},"up":{"title":"Wired or wireless? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","main":"Which medium does this describe?","bins":["Twisted pair","Coaxial","Fibre-optic","Microwave","Satellite"],"items":[{"t":"Weakened by heavy rain between two hilltops.","bin":3,"fb":"Rain fade affects microwave links."},{"t":"Covers ships far out at sea, but with noticeable delay.","bin":4,"fb":"Satellite."},{"t":"Cheapest for an office LAN, limited to about 100 m per run.","bin":0,"fb":"Twisted pair."},{"t":"Immune to lightning and electrical interference.","bin":2,"fb":"Glass does not conduct electricity."},{"t":"Carries the most data between continents along the sea bed.","bin":2,"fb":"Submarine fibre."},{"t":"Braided metal shield around a copper core; used for cable TV.","bin":1,"fb":"Coaxial."},{"t":"Needs line of sight between dishes tens of kilometres apart.","bin":3,"fb":"Microwave."}]}},{"type":"mcq","title":"Linking two buildings","bloom":"Apply","q":"<p>The Sint Maarten Water &amp; Light company needs a network link between its office and its power station 2 km away. The link runs past high-voltage equipment, and the area gets frequent lightning in the rainy season. The company needs very high bandwidth for live monitoring. Which transmission medium is MOST suitable?</p>","options":[{"t":"Unshielded twisted pair","fb":"UTP is limited to about 100 m per run and is affected by electrical interference."},{"t":"Bluetooth","fb":"Bluetooth reaches only about 10 m."},{"t":"Fibre-optic cable","fb":"Correct. Fibre handles long distances and high bandwidth, and is immune to electrical interference and lightning.","ok":true},{"t":"Infrared","fb":"Infrared needs line of sight over a few metres; useless at 2 km."}],"tutor":[{"h":"List the needs","p":"<p>Distance: 2 km. Environment: high voltage and lightning. Bandwidth: very high. Which medium meets all three?</p>"},{"h":"Light vs electricity","p":"<p>A medium that uses light instead of electricity is not disturbed by electrical fields.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Linking two buildings (Foundation)","q":"<p>A company needs a very fast cable link between two buildings 2 km apart, near high-voltage power equipment. Which cable should it use?</p>","options":[{"t":"Bluetooth","fb":"Bluetooth is wireless and only about 10 m."},{"t":"Twisted pair","fb":"Too short-range and affected by interference."},{"t":"Fibre-optic","fb":"Yes: long distance, fast, not affected by electrical interference.","ok":true}]},"up":{"title":"Linking two buildings (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"<p>The Water &amp; Light company is choosing between fibre-optic cable and a point-to-point microwave link for the 2 km connection. Budget is tight, but the link must keep working during heavy tropical rain and must be very hard to eavesdrop on. Which evaluation is BEST?</p>","options":[{"t":"Microwave, because wireless signals are impossible to intercept.","fb":"Radio beams can be intercepted; they rely on encryption."},{"t":"Fibre, because fibre needs no installation work.","fb":"Fibre needs trenching or poles and specialist splicing: that is its main cost."},{"t":"Fibre, despite the higher installation cost, because it is unaffected by rain fade and is much harder to tap than a radio beam.","fb":"Correct. You weighed cost against the two stated must-haves.","ok":true},{"t":"Microwave, because heavy rain improves microwave signals.","fb":"Heavy rain weakens (fades) microwave signals."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"Wired or wireless network? Pros and cons","html":"<p>When choosing between a wired and a wireless network, learn these advantages and limitations.</p>\n<table><tr><th></th><th>Wired network technology</th><th>Wireless network technology</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Advantages</b></td><td>1. <b>Fastest transfer speeds</b>: Gigabit Ethernet gives up to 1 Gbps (10 Gbps is now available too).<br>2. Physical, fixed connections are <b>not prone to interference</b> or to changes in available bandwidth caused by walls.</td><td>1. <b>Cheaper</b> to set up: no cables between computers, and lower long-term costs because there is less equipment to maintain.<br>2. <b>Mobile and versatile</b>: easy to set up and take down, ideal for temporary worksites, homes or rented space (such as a Carnival booth or a pop-up clinic after a hurricane).</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Limitations</b></td><td>1. Needs more resources (cabling, switches/hubs, network cards), so <b>initial and long-term costs are higher</b>.<br>2. A hassle to add new devices: cables and network cards are needed, and wires must be run from the switch to each computer.</td><td>1. <b>Limited, shared bandwidth</b>. Older wireless links could not support video teleconferencing; today Wi-Fi 6 and 5G handle video calls, but bandwidth is still shared between users and can fluctuate.<br>2. Greater chance of <b>jamming and interference</b> from external factors such as fog, heavy rain, dust or obstacles, and from other networks.</td></tr></table>\n<p>Wireless channels are not as fast or as reliable as cables, but they are very <b>convenient</b>. They are used when it is inconvenient, impractical or impossible to install cables.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> If a scenario says users <b>move around</b> or the site is <b>temporary</b>, lean wireless. If it stresses <b>maximum speed and reliability</b>, lean wired.</div>","plain":"<p>Wired networks are the fastest and most reliable, but cost more and are a hassle to extend because every new computer needs a cable. Wireless networks are cheaper, easy to set up and let people move around, but they share bandwidth and suffer more interference.</p>","deeper":"<p>Most real networks are <b>hybrids</b>. A hotel at Magazine Beach might cable its servers, front desk and security cameras (speed, reliability, security) while giving guests Wi-Fi everywhere (mobility). A Grade One answer recommends a mix and justifies each part. Also note how the 'limited bandwidth' point has changed over time: early Wi-Fi offered a few megabits per second shared by everyone, which made video calls impossible; modern Wi-Fi can exceed a gigabit in ideal conditions. The principle still holds, though: wireless bandwidth is shared, so 30 students streaming at once will still slow each other down.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Wired or wireless: which is it?","bloom":"Understand","intro":"<p>Sort each statement from your notes.</p>","main":"Is this an advantage or limitation of wired or wireless networks?","bins":["Wired","Wireless"],"items":[{"t":"Not affected by walls changing the available bandwidth.","bin":0,"fb":"Wired advantage."},{"t":"Bandwidth is shared and can fluctuate.","bin":1,"fb":"Wireless limitation."},{"t":"More affected by interference from weather, obstacles and other networks.","bin":1,"fb":"Wireless limitation."},{"t":"Fastest transfer speeds, e.g. Gigabit Ethernet.","bin":0,"fb":"Wired advantage."},{"t":"Users can move around freely.","bin":1,"fb":"Wireless advantage: mobility."},{"t":"Easy to set up and dismantle for a temporary site.","bin":1,"fb":"Wireless advantage."},{"t":"Adding a computer means running a new cable from the switch.","bin":0,"fb":"Wired limitation."},{"t":"Higher initial and long-term costs for cabling and switches.","bin":0,"fb":"Wired limitation."}],"win":"Sorted. You can now weigh wired against wireless for any scenario.","tutor":[{"h":"Cable or air?","p":"<p>Statements about cables, switches and speed belong to wired; mobility, interference and quick set-up belong to wireless.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Wired or wireless: which is it? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Fastest speeds.","bin":0,"fb":"Wired."},{"t":"You can move around.","bin":1,"fb":"Wireless."},{"t":"Needs a cable for every computer.","bin":0,"fb":"Wired."},{"t":"Affected by interference.","bin":1,"fb":"Wireless."}]},"up":{"title":"Wired or wireless: which is it? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","bins":["Wired advantage","Wired limitation","Wireless advantage","Wireless limitation"],"items":[{"t":"Thirty users streaming slow each other down.","bin":3,"fb":"Shared bandwidth."},{"t":"Switches, cables and NICs raise the initial cost.","bin":1,"fb":"Cost."},{"t":"A pop-up relief clinic is online within an hour.","bin":2,"fb":"Quick, versatile set-up."},{"t":"Lower maintenance because there is less equipment.","bin":2,"fb":"Lower long-term cost."},{"t":"Signals are unaffected by thick concrete walls.","bin":0,"fb":"No wall interference."},{"t":"Moving the lab to another room means re-running every cable.","bin":1,"fb":"Hard to change."},{"t":"Gigabit Ethernet delivers up to 1 Gbps to each desk.","bin":0,"fb":"Speed."},{"t":"Heavy fog and rain weaken a link.","bin":3,"fb":"Interference."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"Bandwidth and data transfer rate","html":"<p><b>Bandwidth</b> is the <b>amount of data that can be transmitted in a fixed amount of time</b>, usually measured in <b>bits per second (bps)</b>. Think of it as the width of a highway: more lanes, more cars per minute.</p>\n<table><tr><th>Unit</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n<tr><td>kbps</td><td>thousand bits per second</td><td>Old dial-up: 56 kbps</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mbps</td><td>million bits per second</td><td>A home plan of 50 Mbps</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gbps</td><td>billion bits per second</td><td>A fibre backbone link of 10 Gbps</td></tr></table>\n<h4>Bits vs bytes: the classic trap</h4>\n<p>Speeds are quoted in <b>bits</b> (small b: Mbps). File sizes are in <b>bytes</b> (capital B: MB). One byte = 8 bits.</p>\n<p><b>Worked example.</b> Jada downloads a 600 MB video for her SBA on a 40 Mbps connection. How long will it take (ideally)?</p>\n<ul><li>Convert the file to bits: 600 MB × 8 = <b>4,800 Mb</b>.</li>\n<li>Divide by the speed: 4,800 ÷ 40 = <b>120 seconds</b> = 2 minutes.</li></ul>\n<p>Real downloads are a little slower because of other users, Wi-Fi interference and overheads.</p>\n<h4>Why bandwidth matters</h4>\n<ul><li>Video calls on Teams, streaming Carnival live and online gaming need high bandwidth.</li>\n<li>When 30 students stream at once on the same school connection, they <b>share</b> the bandwidth, so everyone slows down.</li>\n<li><b>Fibre</b> has the highest bandwidth of all media; Bluetooth has very low bandwidth.</li></ul>\n<h4>Broadband, narrowband and voiceband</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Broadband</b>: high data rates (traditionally \"up to several million bps\"; today hundreds of Mbps or more) over channels such as microwave links, coaxial and fibre cables and 4G/5G. Carries data-heavy content such as video.</li>\n<li><b>Narrowband</b>: very low speeds, about 40 to 300 bps, e.g. an old telegraph line; enough for low-speed terminals and short text.</li>\n<li><b>Voice band</b>: medium speeds of about 100 to 9,600 bps over an ordinary telephone line, as with old dial-up modems.</li></ul>\n<p>Bandwidth is quoted in <b>bits per second (bps)</b> or, in older texts, <b>characters per second (cps)</b>. The word can also mean the <b>range of radio frequencies</b> a medium can handle.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> Show your working in calculation questions: convert, divide, then state the unit. Marks are given for method.</div>","plain":"<p>Bandwidth is how much data a connection can carry each second, like how many lanes a road has. It is measured in bits per second (Mbps). Remember: files are measured in bytes and 1 byte = 8 bits, so multiply the file size by 8 before dividing by the speed.</p>","deeper":"<b>Bandwidth</b> is the maximum capacity; <b>throughput</b> is what you actually get. A gamer also cares about <b>latency</b> (delay, measured in milliseconds, often called \"ping\"): a satellite link can have huge bandwidth but high latency, which makes fast online games lag. Upload and download bandwidth are often <b>different</b>: a 100/20 plan gives 100 Mbps down but only 20 Mbps up, which matters when a content creator in Chaguanas uploads a 2 GB video (2,000 MB × 8 = 16,000 Mb ÷ 20 = 800 s, about 13 minutes) even though the same file would download in under 3 minutes."},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Bandwidth calculator","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Use: time (s) = file size in MB × 8 ÷ speed in Mbps. Choose the correct answer.</p>","items":[{"text":"How many bits are in 1 byte?","options":["4","10","8"],"answer":2,"fb":"1 byte = 8 bits."},{"text":"A 100 MB file on a 20 Mbps connection takes how long?","options":["5 s","160 s","40 s"],"answer":2,"fb":"100 × 8 = 800 Mb; 800 ÷ 20 = 40 s."},{"text":"A 250 MB game update on a 50 Mbps connection takes how long?","options":["40 s","5 s","50 s"],"answer":0,"fb":"250 × 8 = 2,000; 2,000 ÷ 50 = 40 s."},{"text":"A plan is 80 Mbps. What is that in megabytes per second?","options":["80 MB/s","640 MB/s","10 MB/s"],"answer":2,"fb":"80 ÷ 8 = 10 MB/s."},{"text":"Which medium has the highest bandwidth?","options":["Fibre-optic","Infrared","Bluetooth"],"answer":0,"fb":"Fibre carries the most data."},{"text":"Thirty students stream videos on one school connection at the same time. What happens?","options":["They share the bandwidth, so each is slower","Each gets the full bandwidth","Bandwidth increases automatically"],"answer":0,"fb":"Bandwidth is shared between users."}],"win":"Calculations nailed. Multiply by 8, divide by the speed.","tutor":[{"h":"Bytes to bits","p":"<p>MB × 8 = Mb.</p>"},{"h":"Then divide","p":"<p>Mb ÷ Mbps = seconds.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Bandwidth calculator (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Simple bits and bytes.</p>","items":[{"text":"1 byte = ? bits","options":["2","8"],"answer":1,"fb":"8 bits."},{"text":"Speed is usually measured in","options":["bits per second","metres"],"answer":0,"fb":"bps, e.g. Mbps."},{"text":"Which is faster?","options":["10 Mbps","100 Mbps"],"answer":1,"fb":"Bigger number, more data per second."}]},"up":{"title":"Bandwidth calculator (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Multi-step problems. Show the working in your head.</p>","items":[{"text":"A 1.5 GB file (1,500 MB) downloads on a 60 Mbps line. Time?","options":["200 s","1,500 s","25 s"],"answer":0,"fb":"1,500 × 8 = 12,000 Mb; 12,000 ÷ 60 = 200 s."},{"text":"A 100/20 Mbps plan. How much longer does it take to UPLOAD a 500 MB video than to download it?","options":["Same time","160 s longer","40 s longer"],"answer":1,"fb":"Down: 4,000 ÷ 100 = 40 s. Up: 4,000 ÷ 20 = 200 s. Difference 160 s."},{"text":"A 300 MB file took 60 s. What was the actual throughput?","options":["40 Mbps","5 Mbps","300 Mbps"],"answer":0,"fb":"300 × 8 = 2,400 Mb ÷ 60 s = 40 Mbps."},{"text":"A satellite link has high bandwidth but a gamer still lags. Why?","options":["Too many bytes","Low bandwidth","High latency (delay)"],"answer":2,"fb":"Latency, not bandwidth, causes lag."},{"text":"A 50 Mbps line is shared equally by 10 active users. Each gets about","options":["500 Mbps","50 Mbps","5 Mbps"],"answer":2,"fb":"50 ÷ 10 = 5 Mbps."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"Simplex, half-duplex and full-duplex","html":"<p><b>Transmission mode</b> describes the <b>direction</b> in which data can flow between two devices.</p>\n<figure class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 420 160\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Simplex: one arrow one way. Half-duplex: arrows both ways but taking turns. Full-duplex: two arrows both ways at the same time.\">\n<defs><marker id=\"ah2\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z\" fill=\"#F5C542\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"60\" height=\"30\" rx=\"4\"/><rect x=\"190\" y=\"12\" width=\"60\" height=\"30\" rx=\"4\"/>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"62\" width=\"60\" height=\"30\" rx=\"4\"/><rect x=\"190\" y=\"62\" width=\"60\" height=\"30\" rx=\"4\"/>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"112\" width=\"60\" height=\"30\" rx=\"4\"/><rect x=\"190\" y=\"112\" width=\"60\" height=\"30\" rx=\"4\"/>\n<line x1=\"72\" y1=\"27\" x2=\"186\" y2=\"27\" stroke=\"#F5C542\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#ah2)\"/>\n<line x1=\"72\" y1=\"72\" x2=\"186\" y2=\"72\" stroke=\"#F5C542\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#ah2)\"/><line x1=\"186\" y1=\"84\" x2=\"72\" y2=\"84\" stroke=\"#F5C542\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 4\" marker-end=\"url(#ah2)\"/>\n<line x1=\"72\" y1=\"122\" x2=\"186\" y2=\"122\" stroke=\"#F5C542\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#ah2)\"/><line x1=\"186\" y1=\"134\" x2=\"72\" y2=\"134\" stroke=\"#F5C542\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#ah2)\"/>\n</g>\n<g font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"262\" y=\"24\" font-weight=\"700\">SIMPLEX</text><text x=\"262\" y=\"37\" font-size=\"9\">one way only (radio broadcast)</text>\n<text x=\"262\" y=\"74\" font-weight=\"700\">HALF-DUPLEX</text><text x=\"262\" y=\"87\" font-size=\"9\">both ways, one at a time (\"over!\")</text>\n<text x=\"262\" y=\"124\" font-weight=\"700\">FULL-DUPLEX</text><text x=\"262\" y=\"137\" font-size=\"9\">both ways at the same time (phone call)</text>\n</g></svg><figcaption>Transmission modes describe the direction of data flow between two devices.</figcaption></figure>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Simplex</b>: data flows in <b>one direction only</b>. The receiver can never reply on the same channel.<br>Examples: a radio station broadcasting to car radios; TV broadcasts; a GPS satellite sending signals to your phone; a keyboard sending keystrokes to a computer.</li>\n<li><b>Half-duplex</b>: data flows in <b>both directions, but only one direction at a time</b>. Devices take turns.<br>Examples: walkie-talkies and two-way radios used by Carnival security or the coast guard (\"Over!\"); a hub-based network where only one device can send at once.</li>\n<li><b>Full-duplex</b>: data flows in <b>both directions at the same time</b>.<br>Examples: a telephone or WhatsApp call where both people can talk and hear at once; video calls on Teams; modern switched Ethernet.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Both directions?</th><th>At the same time?</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Simplex</td><td>No</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td>Half-duplex</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Full-duplex</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr></table>\n<p>Many CSEC notes call full-duplex simply <b>duplex</b>: the three modes are then <b>simplex</b>, <b>half-duplex</b> and <b>duplex</b>. If you see \"duplex\" alone in a question, it means both directions <b>at the same time</b>.</p>\n<h4>Point-to-point vs broadcast</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Point-to-point communication</b>: data is sent directly from one device to another along a connecting channel; other users cannot receive it, e.g. a phone call between two people.</li>\n<li><b>Broadcast communication</b>: data is sent from one device to all the others in the network, or from one central point to anyone able to receive it, e.g. a radio or TV station.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> Give an example with each definition. \"Half-duplex: both ways but not at the same time, e.g. a walkie-talkie\" gets both marks.</div>","plain":"<b>Simplex</b> is a one-way street, like a radio station: you can listen but not talk back. <b>Half-duplex</b> is a narrow bridge: traffic can go both ways but only one way at a time, like walkie-talkies. <b>Full-duplex</b> is a two-lane highway: both ways at once, like a phone call.","deeper":"<p>Why does it matter? <b>Half-duplex</b> wastes time while devices wait their turn and can cause <b>collisions</b> if two speak at once, which is exactly why old hub networks slowed down. A switch gives each device its own full-duplex link, so it can send and receive simultaneously, effectively doubling capacity. Careful with examples: a modern printer is <i>not</i> a good simplex example, because it sends status messages (\"paper jam\", \"low ink\") back to the computer. Broadcast radio and TV are the safest simplex examples.</p>"},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Mode and example","bloom":"Understand","intro":"<p>Match each communication situation to its transmission mode.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"A radio station broadcasts soca hits to car radios","r":"Simplex"},{"l":"Two security officers at Carnival use walkie-talkies, saying 'Over' after each message","r":"Half-duplex"},{"l":"A video call between a student in Port of Spain and her aunt in Toronto","r":"Full-duplex"}],"win":"Correct: one way, taking turns, both at once.","tutor":[{"h":"Can the receiver reply?","p":"<p>No = simplex.</p>"},{"h":"Can both talk at once?","p":"<p>Yes = full-duplex; no, they take turns = half-duplex.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Mode and example (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"TV broadcast","r":"Simplex"},{"l":"Walkie-talkie","r":"Half-duplex"},{"l":"Phone call","r":"Full-duplex"}]},"up":{"title":"Mode and example (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Match each description to the term or consequence.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"GPS satellite signal to a fishing boat's receiver","r":"Simplex: the receiver never transmits back"},{"l":"Old hub-based network where only one PC may send at a time","r":"Half-duplex: collisions when two send at once"},{"l":"Switched Ethernet link between a PC and a switch","r":"Full-duplex: sends and receives simultaneously"},{"l":"Coast guard marine radio channel","r":"Half-duplex: push-to-talk, one at a time"},{"l":"Live-streamed Carnival parade watched by thousands","r":"Simplex for viewers: one-way stream of content"}]}},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Spot the full-duplex","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Your classmate lists communication links. Only some allow both sides to send AT THE SAME TIME.</p>","q":"Tap EVERY full-duplex example.","lines":["1. A WhatsApp voice call between two friends","2. A walkie-talkie conversation","3. A Teams video lesson where the teacher and student can talk over each other","4. A TV broadcast of a cricket match at Kensington Oval","5. A landline telephone call","6. A radio DJ broadcasting from Roseau"],"correct":[0,2,4],"win":"Yes. Phone calls and video calls are full-duplex.","miss":"Walkie-talkies take turns (half-duplex); broadcasts are one way (simplex).","tutor":[{"h":"Can both speak at once?","p":"<p>If people can interrupt each other and still be heard, it is full-duplex.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the full-duplex (Foundation)","q":"<p>Tap ALL the full-duplex examples (there are two).</p>","lines":["1. A phone call","2. A radio broadcast","3. A video call"],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Correct: calls let both people talk at once.","miss":"A radio broadcast is one way only."},"up":{"title":"Spot the full-duplex (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","q":"<p>Tap EVERY statement that is correct about transmission modes.</p>","lines":["1. Half-duplex allows data in both directions, but not simultaneously.","2. A printer that reports 'paper jam' to the PC is a perfect simplex example.","3. Full-duplex can roughly double the usable capacity of a link compared with half-duplex.","4. A switch can give each connected device a full-duplex link.","5. Simplex links let the receiver reply on the same channel.","6. Collisions are a problem when two half-duplex devices transmit together."],"correct":[0,2,3,5],"win":"Precise thinking. You know the definitions and their consequences.","miss":"Check: a printer talks back, and simplex never lets the receiver reply."}},{"type":"learn","title":"Designing a small network","html":"<p>Exams often give you a scenario and ask you to <b>recommend</b> components and media. Use a simple checklist.</p>\n<h4>The Castle Bruce Community Centre</h4>\n<p>It wants a lab of 12 desktops, a printer, Wi-Fi for visitors' phones and a link to the Internet.</p>\n<table><tr><th>Need</th><th>Component / medium</th><th>Why</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Each desktop must connect</td><td>NIC (built in) + Cat6 twisted-pair cable</td><td>Cheap, fast, runs are under 100 m</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Connect the 12 desktops and printer</td><td>A 16- or 24-port <b>switch</b></td><td>Sends data only to the right device; spare ports for growth</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Visitors' phones</td><td><b>Wireless access point</b> with WPA3 and a separate guest network</td><td>Mobile devices; keeps visitors away from the lab PCs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Connect the LAN to the Internet</td><td><b>Router</b> + modem/ONT from the ISP</td><td>Joins the LAN to the ISP's network</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Protect equipment in storms</td><td>UPS and surge protector</td><td>Waitukubuli Power & Water outages and lightning surges</td></tr></table>\n<h4>Wired or wireless? Decide with four questions</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Do users move?</b> Yes → wireless.</li>\n<li><b>Is speed and reliability critical?</b> Yes → wired (and fibre for long or noisy runs).</li>\n<li><b>How sensitive is the data?</b> Very → wired, or wireless with strong encryption on a separate network.</li>\n<li><b>Can cables be installed?</b> Historic or concrete buildings, open areas or water → wireless.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> For each item you recommend, give a <b>reason linked to the scenario</b>. \"A switch, because it connects the 12 PCs and sends data only to the intended device\" earns marks; \"a switch\" alone often earns half.</div>","plain":"<p>To design a small network: give each computer a NIC and a cable, plug them all into a switch, add a wireless access point for phones, and use a router and modem to reach the Internet. Use cables where speed matters and Wi-Fi where people move around.</p>","deeper":"<p>Grade One answers think about <b>growth, resilience and security</b>, not just connectivity. Growth: buy a switch with spare ports. Resilience: in hurricane season, keep the router and switch on a <b>UPS</b>, store backups off-site or in the cloud, and consider a <b>mobile-data backup link</b> if the main line fails. Security: separate the guest Wi-Fi from the lab, change default router passwords, and keep firmware updated. These ideas link forward to Section 3 (Countermeasures) and are exactly what a strong 'Evaluate' or 'Justify' answer includes.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"The journey of a message","bloom":"Understand","intro":"<p>Kai in Marsh Harbour sends a photo from his laptop over Wi-Fi to a friend's server overseas. Put the steps in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"The laptop's wireless NIC turns the photo's data into radio signals."},{"t":"The wireless access point receives the signals and passes the data onto the home LAN."},{"t":"The router sees that the destination is on another network and forwards the data towards the ISP."},{"t":"The modem (or ONT) converts the signal for the line to the ISP."},{"t":"The ISP sends the data across the Internet, often through undersea fibre cables."},{"t":"The destination server receives the photo."}],"win":"Spot on: NIC, access point, router, modem, ISP, destination.","tutor":[{"h":"Start at the device","p":"<p>The first piece of hardware is inside Kai's laptop.</p>"},{"h":"Leave the house last","p":"<p>The modem/ONT is the last box before the ISP's line.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"The journey of a message (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put these four steps in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"The laptop's network card sends the data."},{"t":"The Wi-Fi access point receives it."},{"t":"The router sends it out towards the Internet."},{"t":"The data reaches the website's server."}]},"up":{"title":"The journey of a message (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Now a wired PC at school sends a file to a server in another country. Order every step, including the media.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"The PC's NIC places the data on a twisted-pair cable."},{"t":"The switch reads the destination MAC address of the router and forwards the data only to the router's port."},{"t":"The router checks the destination IP address and forwards the packets towards the ISP."},{"t":"The ONT converts electrical signals into light for the fibre to the ISP."},{"t":"The ISP's routers pass the packets across submarine fibre cables to other networks."},{"t":"Routers near the destination deliver the packets to the server's network."},{"t":"The server's NIC receives the data and the file is reassembled."}]}},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Wire the community centre","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>You are helping set up a new computer lab at the <b>Castle Bruce Community Centre</b>. A donor has given 12 desktops. Make the right calls.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The volunteer has found an old <b>hub</b> in a cupboard and wants to use it to connect the 12 PCs. What do you advise?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Buy a switch instead: it sends data only to the right PC, so the lab stays fast.","to":"n2","fb":"Right. Switches avoid the collisions and wasted traffic of hubs."},{"t":"Use the hub: hubs and switches are exactly the same.","to":"bad1","fb":"They are not. A hub broadcasts to every port."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The lab PCs are 15 to 40 m from the switch. Which cable?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Cat6 twisted pair: cheap, fast and well within the 100 m limit.","to":"n3","fb":"Good, sensible choice."},{"t":"Fibre-optic to every desk.","to":"bad2","fb":"Overkill: expensive and needs specialist installation for short runs."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Visitors want Wi-Fi for their phones. What do you set up?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A wireless access point with a separate, WPA3-protected guest network.","to":"n4","fb":"Yes. Mobility for visitors, separation for security."},{"t":"Give visitors the lab's admin password and plug them into the switch.","to":"bad3","fb":"That exposes the lab and phones have no Ethernet port anyway."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>The centre is in a lightning-prone, hilly area and power cuts are common. How do you protect the network?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Nothing: network equipment cannot be damaged by power problems.","to":"bad4","fb":"Surges can destroy equipment and outages drop the connection."},{"t":"Put the router, switch and modem on a UPS with surge protection.","to":"good","fb":"Smart. Equipment stays up through short outages and is protected from surges."}]},"good":{"text":"<b>Lab launched!</b> A switch, Cat6 cabling, a secure guest WLAN and a UPS: fast, safe and storm-ready.","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<b>Slow lab.</b> A hub copies everything to everyone, causing collisions and poor speed. Use a switch.","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<b>Budget blown.</b> Fibre is for long distances, very high bandwidth or noisy environments, not 40 m desk runs.","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<b>Security risk.</b> Visitors need a separate, encrypted wireless network, never admin access.","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<b>Fried router.</b> A lightning surge took out the equipment. UPS and surge protection were needed.","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Device first","p":"<p>Hub vs switch: which delivers only to the right port?</p>"},{"h":"Then media and safety","p":"<p>Short runs: twisted pair. Mobile users: WAP. Storms: UPS.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Wire the community centre (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Help set up a small lab at the Castle Bruce Community Centre.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Which device should connect the 12 PCs together?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A printer","to":"bad1","fb":"A printer does not connect computers."},{"t":"A switch","to":"n2","fb":"Yes."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Visitors want to use Wi-Fi on their phones. What do you add?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A wireless access point","to":"good","fb":"Correct."},{"t":"A longer cable","to":"bad2","fb":"Phones need wireless."}]},"good":{"text":"<b>Done!</b> A switch for the PCs and an access point for phones.","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<b>Not quite.</b> A switch links the computers.","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<b>No ports!</b> Phones connect by Wi-Fi through an access point.","end":"bad"}}},"up":{"title":"Wire the community centre (Challenge)","intro":"<p>The centre is expanding to a second building 400 m away across a road, and wants to stream workshops live.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>How do you link the two buildings 400 m apart?</p>","choices":[{"t":"One long Cat6 cable.","to":"bad1","fb":"Twisted pair is limited to about 100 m per run."},{"t":"Fibre-optic cable (or a point-to-point wireless bridge if trenching is impossible).","to":"n2","fb":"Correct: twisted pair cannot run 400 m."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Live-streaming a workshop needs a steady 10 Mbps upload. The plan is 100/5 Mbps. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Nothing: 100 Mbps is plenty.","to":"bad2","fb":"That is download; the upload is only 5 Mbps."},{"t":"Upgrade to a plan with more upload bandwidth, e.g. 100/20 or fibre.","to":"n3","fb":"Yes: upload bandwidth is the bottleneck."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Staff laptops and visitor phones will share the Wi-Fi. How do you protect staff data?</p>","choices":[{"t":"One open network for everyone.","to":"bad3","fb":"Open networks invite eavesdropping."},{"t":"Separate staff and guest networks (VLANs/SSIDs) with WPA3 and strong passwords.","to":"n4","fb":"Segmentation plus encryption."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>After a hurricane, the fixed line may be down for weeks. What resilience do you plan?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Wait for the line to be repaired.","to":"bad4","fb":"The centre may be needed as a relief hub."},{"t":"A backup mobile-data or satellite link and off-site/cloud backups.","to":"good","fb":"A second path keeps the centre online."}]},"good":{"text":"<b>Resilient design.</b> Fibre between buildings, enough upload, segmented secure Wi-Fi and a backup link.","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<b>Signal loss.</b> 400 m is four times the twisted-pair limit.","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<b>Stream keeps freezing.</b> Upload, not download, limits live streaming.","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<b>Data exposed.</b> Visitors could intercept staff traffic.","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<b>Offline when needed most.</b> Plan a second route.","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"mcq","title":"After the hurricane","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"<p>A Category 5 hurricane has damaged undersea cable equipment, mobile towers and power lines on an island. The emergency operations centre in the capital needs to contact relief agencies overseas TODAY. Which communication medium is MOST suitable in the first days?</p>","options":[{"t":"Twisted pair, because it is the cheapest","fb":"Cheap does not help if the lines are down; it also cannot cross oceans."},{"t":"Satellite, because it needs no local cables or towers to reach other countries","fb":"Correct. Satellite works independently of damaged local infrastructure, despite cost and delay.","ok":true},{"t":"Fibre-optic cable, because it has the highest bandwidth","fb":"Fibre is best normally, but the damaged cables cannot be used until repaired."},{"t":"Bluetooth, because it works without power","fb":"Bluetooth needs power and reaches only about 10 m."}],"tutor":[{"h":"What still works?","p":"<p>If local cables and towers are damaged, which medium does not rely on them?</p>"},{"h":"Best vs available","p":"<p>Sometimes the best medium on paper is not available. Choose what works today.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"After the hurricane (Foundation)","q":"<p>After a hurricane, cables and cell towers on an island are broken. Rescue teams need to call other countries. Which medium can they use?</p>","options":[{"t":"Satellite","fb":"Yes: it does not need local cables or towers.","ok":true},{"t":"Infrared","fb":"Infrared reaches only a few metres."},{"t":"Twisted pair cable","fb":"The cables are broken."}]},"up":{"title":"After the hurricane (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"<p>Two weeks after the hurricane, the government must decide how to restore Internet service across the island long-term. Options: rely on satellite terminals permanently, or repair the submarine fibre link and keep satellite as backup. Which evaluation is BEST?</p>","options":[{"t":"Use Bluetooth meshes across the island instead of either.","fb":"Bluetooth cannot provide island-wide or international capacity."},{"t":"Repair fibre as the main link for its far higher capacity and lower latency, and keep satellite as a backup route for future emergencies.","fb":"Correct. Fibre for capacity, satellite for resilience: a redundant design.","ok":true},{"t":"Use satellite only, because it has lower latency than fibre.","fb":"Satellite, especially geostationary, has higher latency than fibre."},{"t":"Use fibre only and remove all satellite equipment, because cables cannot be damaged again.","fb":"Cables can be damaged again; keeping a backup is wise."}]}},{"type":"write","title":"Structured question: the Layou real-estate office","bloom":"Analyse","genre":"Structured question: network components and media (13 marks)","stimulus":"<p><b>Bayside Realty</b> is opening an office in Layou with 10 desktop computers, a shared printer and a meeting room where clients use their phones and tablets. The office must connect to the Internet through an ISP. A second office in the same complex, 250 m away, must also be linked. Agents often hold video calls with overseas buyers.</p>","prompt":"<p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>State</b> the function of EACH of the following: (i) network interface card, (ii) switch, (iii) router. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> why a switch is preferred over a hub for the office. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Identify</b> the device needed so clients' phones and tablets can join the network, and <b>state</b> ONE security measure for it. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Recommend</b> a transmission medium for the 250 m link between the offices and <b>justify</b> your choice. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>A video call is described as full-duplex. <b>Explain</b> what this means and <b>contrast</b> it with half-duplex. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":100,"max":400,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> (i) A NIC ... (ii) A switch ... (iii) A router ...<br><b>(b)</b> A switch is preferred because it ... whereas a hub ...<br><b>(c)</b> A ... is needed. One security measure is ...<br><b>(d)</b> I recommend ... because ...<br><b>(e)</b> Full-duplex means ... whereas half-duplex ...</p>","checklist":["I gave a function (what it DOES) for each of NIC, switch and router.","I explained hub vs switch using 'sends to all' vs 'sends only to the destination'.","I named a wireless access point and a real security measure (WPA2/WPA3, strong password, guest network).","I chose a medium for 250 m that actually works at that distance and justified it.","I defined full-duplex and contrasted it with half-duplex, with an example."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Functions of NIC, switch, router","marks":3,"look":["connect.{0,40}network|join.{0,20}network|network adapter","switch.{0,80}(destination|intended|specific|correct|right|mac)","router.{0,80}(network|internet|forward|route|path|ip)"],"min":3,"strong":"Each function correct.","weak":"NIC lets a device connect to a network; switch forwards data only to the intended device in a LAN; router connects networks and forwards data between them."},{"criterion":"Switch over hub","marks":2,"look":["hub.{0,60}(all|every)|(all|every).{0,30}port","switch.{0,80}(only|destination|intended|mac)|collision|faster|efficien|secur"],"min":2,"strong":"Clear contrast.","weak":"A hub sends data to every port; a switch sends only to the destination, reducing collisions and improving security."},{"criterion":"Wireless device and security","marks":2,"look":["access point|\\bWAP\\b|wireless router","wpa|encrypt|password|guest network|ssid|mac filter"],"min":2,"strong":"Device and measure named.","weak":"Name a wireless access point and give WPA2/WPA3 encryption or a strong password."},{"criterion":"Medium for 250 m link","marks":3,"look":["fib(re|er)","distance|far|250|long|100 ?m","bandwidth|interference|fast|secur|lightning|speed"],"min":3,"strong":"Fibre justified on distance and quality.","weak":"Recommend fibre-optic: twisted pair only reaches about 100 m; fibre gives high bandwidth over long distance and resists interference. (A point-to-point wireless/microwave link with justification is also acceptable.)"},{"criterion":"Full vs half-duplex","marks":3,"look":["both direction","same time|simultaneous","one (direction )?at a time|take turns|not at the same time|walkie"],"min":3,"strong":"Definitions and contrast.","weak":"Full-duplex: both directions at the same time; half-duplex: both directions but only one at a time (e.g. walkie-talkie)."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> (i) A network interface card allows a computer or device to connect to a network and send and receive data over it. (ii) A switch connects the devices within the LAN and forwards data only to the device it is addressed to, using MAC addresses. (iii) A router connects the office LAN to other networks such as the ISP's network and the Internet, and forwards data packets between networks using IP addresses.</p><p><b>(b)</b> A hub sends every piece of data it receives out of all its ports, so all ten computers receive traffic not meant for them, causing collisions and slowing the network, and anyone could see other people's data. A switch sends data only to the port of the intended computer, so the network is faster and more secure.</p><p><b>(c)</b> A wireless access point (WAP) is needed. It should use WPA2/WPA3 encryption with a strong password, ideally on a separate guest network so clients cannot reach the office computers.</p><p><b>(d)</b> I recommend fibre-optic cable. Twisted pair can only carry signals reliably for about 100 m, but fibre carries data over long distances with very little signal loss and very high bandwidth, so both offices can share files and video calls. It is also immune to electrical interference and lightning, which matters in a stormy climate.</p><p><b>(e)</b> Full-duplex means data can travel in both directions at the same time, so the agent and the buyer can talk and hear each other simultaneously. Half-duplex also allows both directions, but only one direction at a time, so users must take turns, as with a walkie-talkie.</p>","key":"<p>(a) 1 mark each for a correct function (not a description of appearance). (b) 1 hub sends to all ports; 1 switch sends only to destination/fewer collisions/more secure. (c) 1 WAP; 1 valid security measure. (d) 1 fibre (or justified point-to-point wireless); 2 justifications from distance beyond 100 m, bandwidth, interference/lightning immunity, security. (e) 1 full-duplex both directions simultaneously; 1 half-duplex one at a time; 1 example or explicit contrast.</p>","teacherTip":"Students often say a router 'gives Wi-Fi'. Insist on the function: connecting different networks. In (d), reject Cat6 for 250 m unless repeaters/switches along the run are explicitly described.","easy":{"title":"Structured question: the Layou real-estate office (Foundation)","genre":"Structured question: network components and media (8 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>State</b> what a router does. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li><b>State</b> what a switch does. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>Which device lets clients' phones join the network by Wi-Fi? <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> the best cable for the 250 m link and give ONE reason. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> TWO wired and ONE wireless transmission medium. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol>","frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> A router connects ...<br><b>(b)</b> A switch connects ... and sends data ...<br><b>(c)</b> A wireless ...<br><b>(d)</b> Fibre-optic, because ...<br><b>(e)</b> Wired: ... and ... Wireless: ...</p>","min":30,"max":200,"checklist":["Router = connects networks.","Switch = connects devices in a LAN, sends to the right one.","Wireless access point named.","Medium with a reason.","Two wired and one wireless media."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Router","marks":1,"look":["network|internet"],"min":1,"strong":"Correct.","weak":"A router connects the office network to the Internet (other networks)."},{"criterion":"Switch","marks":1,"look":["switch|connect|device|right|correct|intended"],"min":1,"strong":"Correct.","weak":"A switch links the office computers and sends data to the right one."},{"criterion":"Wireless device","marks":1,"look":["access point|\\bWAP\\b|wireless router"],"min":1,"strong":"Correct.","weak":"A wireless access point."},{"criterion":"Medium and reason","marks":2,"look":["fib(re|er)","distance|far|long|fast|bandwidth|interference"],"min":2,"strong":"Fibre with a reason.","weak":"Fibre-optic, because it works over long distances at high speed."},{"criterion":"Media named","marks":3,"look":["twisted|coax|fib(re|er)","twisted|coax|fib(re|er)","wi.?fi|bluetooth|infrared|microwave|satellite"],"min":3,"strong":"Three media named.","weak":"Wired: twisted pair, coaxial, fibre. Wireless: Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, satellite..."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> A router connects the office network to other networks such as the Internet.</p><p><b>(b)</b> A switch connects the office computers in a LAN and sends data only to the computer it is meant for.</p><p><b>(c)</b> A wireless access point.</p><p><b>(d)</b> Fibre-optic cable, because it can carry data quickly over long distances.</p><p><b>(e)</b> Wired: twisted pair and fibre-optic. Wireless: Wi-Fi.</p>","key":"<p>(a) 1 connects the office network to other networks such as the Internet. (b) 1 connects the computers in the LAN and sends data only to the device it is meant for. (c) 1 wireless access point (accept wireless router). (d) 1 fibre-optic cable; 1 reason: works over long distances (beyond 100 m)/high speed or bandwidth/no electrical interference. (e) 1 mark each for two wired media (twisted pair, coaxial, fibre-optic) and 1 mark for one wireless medium (Wi-Fi/radio, Bluetooth, infrared, microwave, satellite).</p>","teacherTip":"Students often say a router 'gives Wi-Fi'. For (a), insist on its function: it connects the office network to other networks such as the Internet. In (d), twisted pair (Cat5e/Cat6) scores 0 for the 250 m link, because it is limited to about 100 m. In (e), ask for the medium itself (twisted pair, coaxial, fibre-optic) rather than 'network cable'."},"up":{"title":"Structured question: the Layou real-estate office (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Structured question: network components, media and design (19 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>State</b> the function of EACH of: NIC, switch, router and modem. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> why a switch is preferred over a hub. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Compare</b> fibre-optic cable and a point-to-point microwave link for the 250 m connection, then <b>recommend</b> one with justification. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>An agent downloads a 900 MB property video on a 60 Mbps connection. <b>Calculate</b> the ideal download time, showing your working. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> full-duplex and half-duplex, with an example of each. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>The office is in a hurricane zone. <b>Evaluate</b> its dependence on a single Internet link and <b>propose</b> TWO improvements. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":170,"max":560,"frame":"<p><b>(c)</b> Fibre ... whereas microwave ... I recommend ... because ...<br><b>(d)</b> 900 × 8 = ... ÷ 60 = ...<br><b>(f)</b> Relying on one link means ... I propose ... and ...</p>","checklist":["Four functions, each stating what the device DOES.","A true comparison of fibre vs microwave (cost, weather, security, installation).","Calculation shown: ×8 then ÷ speed, with units.","Both modes with examples.","Evaluation of single-link risk with two realistic improvements."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Four functions","marks":4,"look":["connect.{0,40}network|network adapter","switch.{0,80}(destination|intended|specific|correct|mac)","router.{0,80}(network|forward|route|ip)","modulat|analog|digital|convert"],"min":4,"strong":"All four functions.","weak":"Give the job of each: NIC, switch, router, modem (modulate/demodulate digital↔analogue)."},{"criterion":"Switch over hub","marks":2,"look":["hub.{0,60}(all|every)","switch.{0,80}(only|destination|intended|mac)|collision"],"min":2,"strong":"Clear contrast.","weak":"Hub broadcasts; switch delivers only to the destination."},{"criterion":"Fibre vs microwave","marks":4,"look":["fib(re|er)","microwave","rain|weather|line of sight|intercept|secur","cost|expens|trench|install"],"min":3,"strong":"Balanced comparison and a recommendation.","weak":"Compare installation cost, weather (rain fade), line of sight and security, then recommend."},{"criterion":"Calculation","marks":2,"look":["7,?200","120"],"min":2,"strong":"Correct working and answer.","weak":"900 × 8 = 7,200 Mb; 7,200 ÷ 60 = 120 s (2 minutes)."},{"criterion":"Duplex modes","marks":3,"look":["same time|simultaneous","one (direction )?at a time|take turns|not at the same time","phone|video call|walkie|radio"],"min":3,"strong":"Both modes with examples.","weak":"Full-duplex: both ways at once (phone call). Half-duplex: one way at a time (walkie-talkie)."},{"criterion":"Evaluate single link","marks":4,"look":["single|one link|only link|point of failure","backup|second|redundan|mobile data|4g|5g|satellite","ups|generator|power|cloud|off.?site"],"min":3,"strong":"Risk evaluated and two justified improvements.","weak":"Explain the impact of one link failing, then propose a backup link (mobile data/satellite) and a UPS or cloud backups, saying how each helps."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> NIC: lets a device connect to and communicate on a network. Switch: connects devices in the LAN and forwards data only to the intended device using MAC addresses. Router: connects the LAN to other networks and forwards packets between them using IP addresses. Modem: converts digital signals to analogue for transmission over a telephone or cable line and back again.</p><p><b>(b)</b> A hub sends data to every port, causing collisions and exposing data; a switch sends only to the destination port, so it is faster and more secure.</p><p><b>(c)</b> Fibre has very high bandwidth, is unaffected by weather and electrical interference and is very hard to tap, but trenching 250 m and splicing is costly. Microwave avoids digging and is quicker to install, but needs clear line of sight, can be weakened by heavy tropical rain and can be intercepted, so needs encryption. I recommend fibre, because the offices share sensitive client data and need reliable video calls in all weather; the higher installation cost is a one-off.</p><p><b>(d)</b> 900 MB × 8 = 7,200 Mb; 7,200 ÷ 60 = 120 seconds (2 minutes).</p><p><b>(e)</b> Full-duplex: data flows both ways at the same time, e.g. a video call. Half-duplex: both ways but only one at a time, e.g. a walkie-talkie.</p><p><b>(f)</b> With one Internet link, a single cable cut or storm takes the office offline, stopping video calls and cloud access when clients need them most, so the risk is high in hurricane season. I propose (1) a backup mobile-data (4G/5G) or satellite link so traffic can switch automatically, and (2) a UPS on the router and switch with cloud backups, so the network survives short power cuts and data is safe off-site.</p>","key":"<p>(a) 1 mark each for the function (not the appearance) of: NIC (lets a device connect to and communicate on the network); switch (forwards data only to the intended device, using MAC addresses); router (connects the LAN to other networks and forwards packets using IP addresses); modem (converts digital signals to analogue and back, i.e. modulates/demodulates). (b) 1 a hub sends data to every port; 1 a switch sends only to the destination port, so fewer collisions/more secure. (c) 1 fibre point (very high bandwidth, unaffected by weather and interference, hard to tap); 1 microwave point (no trenching and quick to install, but needs line of sight, suffers rain fade and can be intercepted); 1 cost/installation comparison; 1 recommendation justified by the offices' needs (either medium if justified). (d) 1 working: 900 × 8 = 7,200 Mb, then ÷ 60; 1 answer: 120 seconds (2 minutes) with units. (e) 1 full-duplex: both directions at the same time; 1 half-duplex: both directions but one at a time; 1 a correct example of each (e.g. video call; walkie-talkie). (f) 1 risk of a single link explained in context (one cable cut or storm takes the office offline); 1 mark each for two realistic improvements (backup 4G/5G or satellite link; UPS on network equipment; cloud/off-site backups); 1 for justifying how the improvements keep the office working.</p>","teacherTip":"In (a), describing what a device looks like or where it plugs in scores 0; each mark needs what the device does, and the modem mark needs the digital-to-analogue conversion. In (c), either medium can earn the recommendation mark if it is justified, but an answer that only lists fibre's strengths without weighing microwave cannot reach 4. In (d), the common slip is forgetting to multiply by 8 (giving 15 seconds); award the method mark only when megabytes are converted to megabits."}}]},{"id":"S2U3","title":"Mobile communication technologies","objectives":["Describe the features of the mobile generations from 1G to 5G (SO 2.3)","Compare mobile data and Wi-Fi, and explain hotspots, tethering and roaming (SO 2.3)","Explain Bluetooth, NFC/contactless payment and GPS and their uses (SO 2.3)","Assess the suitability of mobile networks for education, commerce (m-commerce), journalism (mobile and citizen journalism), emergencies and telemedicine (SO 2.3)","Discuss issues of coverage, cost and security, and suggest ways to reduce them (SO 2.3)"],"videos":[{"title":"1G to 5G explained","q":"1G 2G 3G 4G 5G explained evolution of mobile networks"},{"title":"How GPS works","q":"how does GPS work trilateration explained"},{"title":"How NFC contactless payment works","q":"how NFC contactless payment works explained"},{"title":"Is public Wi-Fi safe?","q":"public wifi dangers evil twin explained"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Why mobile matters","html":"<p>Most people in the Caribbean go online through a <b>phone</b> before they ever own a laptop. That is why <b>Specific Objective 2.3</b> asks you to <b>assess the importance of mobile communication technologies</b> as part of modern networks.</p>\n<p><b>Mobile communication technology</b> means the devices and networks that let people communicate and access information <b>while on the move</b>, without a fixed cable. It includes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Cellular networks</b> (2G, 3G, 4G, 5G) run by mobile operators.</li>\n<li><b>Mobile devices</b>: smartphones, tablets, smartwatches, mobile card terminals.</li>\n<li><b>Short-range technologies</b>: Wi-Fi hotspots, Bluetooth and NFC.</li>\n<li><b>Location technology</b>: GPS.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>A day on the island</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>A taxi driver at the Castries cruise port cruise pier takes bookings on WhatsApp and uses GPS to reach a villa in Laborie.</li>\n<li>A Form 5 student at Sandbox Tree College submits homework to Google Classroom on mobile data from the bus.</li>\n<li>A vendor at Carnival Village takes tap-to-pay card payments on a small mobile terminal linked to her phone.</li>\n<li>After a storm warning, residents receive an emergency alert on every phone in range.</li>\n<li>On the ferry to Martinique, a family streams music, plays a multiplayer game and video-calls cousins in Toronto: mobile networks for <b>leisure</b> and keeping in touch.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> \"Assess the importance\" means weigh <b>benefits and drawbacks</b> and reach a judgement. Have examples ready for business, education, emergencies and health, and also issues such as cost, coverage and security.</div>","plain":"<p>Mobile technology lets you talk, text, learn, pay and find your way while you move around, without plugging into anything. Phones, cell towers, Wi-Fi hotspots, Bluetooth, tap-to-pay and GPS are all part of it. For many people in the Caribbean, the phone IS their computer.</p>","deeper":"<p>Mobile technology is often called a <b>leapfrog</b> technology: some communities skipped expensive fixed telephone lines and went straight to mobile. In small island states, mobile networks are also quicker to rebuild than buried cables after a disaster, which makes them central to resilience planning. A Grade One answer goes beyond \"it is convenient\" and shows <b>who</b> benefits (vendors, students, patients, emergency services), <b>how</b> (payment, learning, alerts, telemedicine) and <b>what limits</b> the benefit (coverage gaps, data cost, security, digital divide).</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"2G to 5G: the generations","html":"<p>Each <b>generation</b> (\"G\") of cellular technology brought new things you could do on the move.</p>\n<figure class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 440 170\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Timeline of mobile generations: 2G in the 1990s for digital voice and SMS, 3G in the 2000s for mobile Internet, 4G in the 2010s for fast broadband and streaming, 5G from about 2019 for very high speed, very low latency and many connected devices\">\n<line x1=\"20\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"60\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"2\"><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"60\" r=\"14\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"165\" cy=\"60\" r=\"14\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/><circle cx=\"270\" cy=\"60\" r=\"14\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\"/><circle cx=\"375\" cy=\"60\" r=\"16\" stroke=\"#F5C542\"/></g>\n<g font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"700\" fill=\"currentColor\" text-anchor=\"middle\"><text x=\"60\" y=\"64\">2G</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"64\">3G</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"64\">4G</text><text x=\"375\" y=\"64\">5G</text></g>\n<g font-size=\"9\" fill=\"currentColor\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"60\" y=\"30\">1990s</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"30\">2000s</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"30\">2010s</text><text x=\"375\" y=\"30\">2019 onward</text>\n<text x=\"60\" y=\"94\">digital voice</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"106\">SMS texts</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"118\">very slow data</text>\n<text x=\"165\" y=\"94\">mobile Internet</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"106\">e-mail, browsing</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"118\">first video calls</text>\n<text x=\"270\" y=\"94\">LTE broadband</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"106\">HD streaming, apps</text><text x=\"270\" y=\"118\">voice over LTE</text>\n<text x=\"375\" y=\"94\">very high speed</text><text x=\"375\" y=\"106\">very low latency</text><text x=\"375\" y=\"118\">huge numbers of IoT</text><text x=\"375\" y=\"130\">devices</text>\n<text x=\"220\" y=\"158\">Each generation: faster data, lower delay, more users per cell</text>\n</g></svg><figcaption>Mobile generations at a glance. Dates are approximate; rollout reached different islands at different times.</figcaption></figure>\n<table><tr><th>Generation</th><th>Main new features</th><th>What it made possible</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>1G</b></td><td><b>Analogue</b> voice calls only (1980s); no texts or data; easy to eavesdrop on</td><td>Making calls on the move for the first time</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>2G</b></td><td>Radio signal <b>digitised</b> (more capacity, better use of frequencies) using the <b>GSM</b> standard (Global System for Mobile Communications); calls <b>digitally encrypted</b>; <b>SMS</b> text messages; basic data added later through GPRS and EDGE</td><td>Clearer, more secure calls; texting</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>3G</b></td><td>Standardised by the <b>3GPP</b> (3rd Generation Partnership Project) as <b>UMTS</b>; later upgrades HSPA and HSPA+ gave higher bit rates; mobile <b>Internet</b> at usable speeds</td><td>E-mail and web browsing on phones, early video calls, the first smartphones</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>4G (LTE)</b></td><td>Defined by the <b>ITU</b> (International Telecommunication Union); delivered as <b>LTE</b> (Long Term Evolution) and LTE Advanced; <b>mobile broadband</b> with much faster speeds and lower latency; everything, even voice (VoLTE), carried over an IP network</td><td>HD video streaming, social media video, ride-hailing, mobile banking apps, video calls everywhere</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>5G</b></td><td><b>Very high speeds</b>, <b>very low latency</b> (delay of a few milliseconds) and support for <b>huge numbers of connected devices</b></td><td>Smart cities and sensors (the Internet of Things), cloud gaming, real-time remote control of machines, fixed wireless broadband</td></tr></table>\n<p>Speeds vary a lot with the operator, the number of users and how far you are from the tower, so learn the <b>features</b> of each generation rather than exact numbers. Over time, labels such as \"3G\" and \"4G\" have also become <b>marketing terms</b> as much as technical standards, so a phone showing \"4G\" may be on an enhanced 3G network.</p>\n<p>Across the generations, the big change is that mobile networks moved from carrying <b>voice only</b> to giving <b>\"anytime, anywhere\"</b> access to data, multimedia and the Internet, and each step also improved <b>security</b> (encryption) compared with the one before.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> A very common question: \"State ONE advantage of 5G over 4G.\" Good answers: faster data speeds, lower latency, or more devices connected per area. Always say what the advantage <i>allows</i>, e.g. \"lower latency, which allows real-time applications such as cloud gaming.\"</div>","plain":"<b>2G</b> = calls and texts. <b>3G</b> = basic mobile Internet. <b>4G</b> = fast mobile broadband for streaming and apps. <b>5G</b> = super fast, almost no delay, and able to connect huge numbers of gadgets and sensors at once.","deeper":"<p>Why does 5G have lower latency and more capacity? It uses <b>wider frequency channels</b>, including higher frequencies that carry more data but travel shorter distances and are blocked more easily by walls and hills. So high-speed 5G often needs <b>many more small cells</b> close together, which costs more to build. On small, hilly islands operators often roll out 5G first in busy areas such as The Valley and the airport. Older generations are also being <b>switched off</b> worldwide (\"sunsetting\") so their frequencies can be reused, which can leave old devices, such as basic alarm systems relying on 2G, without service.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Generations in order","bloom":"Remember","intro":"<p>Put the mobile generations in order from OLDEST to NEWEST.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Analogue voice calls only, no data","tag":"1G"},{"t":"Digital calls and SMS text messages","tag":"2G"},{"t":"Basic mobile Internet and early video calls","tag":"3G"},{"t":"Fast mobile broadband for HD streaming and apps","tag":"4G"},{"t":"Very high speed, very low latency, massive numbers of IoT devices","tag":"5G"}],"win":"Correct: each generation built on the last.","tutor":[{"h":"Count up","p":"<p>The number goes up with time: 2, 3, 4, 5.</p>"},{"h":"Match the feature","p":"<p>Texting came first; massive IoT is newest.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Generations in order (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put these in order, oldest first.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"1G: analogue calls only"},{"t":"2G: calls and texts"},{"t":"3G: mobile Internet"},{"t":"4G: fast streaming"},{"t":"5G: very fast, tiny delay"}]},"up":{"title":"Generations in order (Challenge)","bloom":"Understand","intro":"<p>No generation labels! Order these milestones from earliest to latest.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Mobile calls become digital and SMS texting begins"},{"t":"Phones can browse the web and send e-mail at usable speeds"},{"t":"Mobile broadband makes HD video streaming and ride-hailing apps common"},{"t":"Networks support real-time remote control and thousands of sensors per square kilometre"},{"t":"Older networks are switched off so their frequencies can be reused"}]}},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Generation features","bloom":"Understand","intro":"<p>Match each feature to the generation that introduced it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"2G","r":"Digital voice calls and SMS"},{"l":"3G","r":"Usable mobile Internet browsing"},{"l":"4G","r":"Mobile broadband for HD streaming"},{"l":"5G","r":"Very low latency for real-time control"}],"win":"Matched. You can now explain what each G added.","tutor":[{"h":"One big idea each","p":"<p>2G texts, 3G Internet, 4G streaming, 5G low latency and IoT.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Generation features (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"2G","r":"Texting (SMS)"},{"l":"4G","r":"Streaming videos"},{"l":"5G","r":"Fastest, tiny delay"}]},"up":{"title":"Generation features (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Match each scenario to the generation that it NEEDS (the oldest generation that could do it well).</p>","pairs":[{"l":"A fisherman texts his catch price to a buyer in Tera Kora","r":"2G"},{"l":"A student reads a short e-mail on the bus","r":"3G"},{"l":"A vlogger live-streams Carnival in HD","r":"4G"},{"l":"Hundreds of smart sensors report flooding levels across a city in real time","r":"5G"}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"The smartphone: a computer in your pocket","html":"<p>A <b>smartphone</b> is a mobile phone with a full operating system, a touch screen and the ability to run apps. It is really a small computer with a radio.</p>\n<h4>What is inside</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Processor, RAM and storage</b>, like a laptop.</li>\n<li><b>Mobile operating system</b>: Android or iOS.</li>\n<li><b>Radios</b>: cellular (4G/5G), Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, NFC and a GPS receiver.</li>\n<li><b>Sensors</b>: accelerometer (detects tilt and steps), gyroscope, compass, light sensor, fingerprint or face recognition, microphone and cameras.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Why smartphones changed everything</h4>\n<table><tr><th>Use</th><th>Caribbean example</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Communication</td><td>WhatsApp groups for families split between the Turks and Caicos Islands, Curaçao and New York</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Banking and payments</td><td>Paying a utility bill by app instead of queueing</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Learning</td><td>Watching a Python tutorial and joining Teams classes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Business</td><td>A guavaberry-liqueur maker sells on Instagram and takes orders on WhatsApp Business</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Navigation</td><td>Map apps with GPS for delivery drivers</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Content creation</td><td>Recording and editing TikTok videos of a beach clean-up</td></tr></table>\n<h4>Limitations</h4>\n<ul><li>Small screens and keyboards make long documents (like your SBA!) hard to produce.</li>\n<li>Battery life: bigger, brighter screens and constant connections drain batteries, a real problem during long power cuts.</li>\n<li>Easily lost or stolen, carrying lots of personal data.</li></ul>","plain":"<p>A smartphone is a tiny computer that also makes calls. It has a processor, memory, an operating system (Android or iOS), apps, a camera, and lots of sensors and radios: mobile data, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, NFC and GPS. That is why one device can be your bank, classroom, camera and map.</p>","deeper":"<p>Smartphones show <b>convergence</b>: many separate devices (camera, music player, GPS unit, torch, calculator, payment card, even a scanner) merged into one. That convenience creates a <b>single point of failure</b> for your personal life: lose the phone and you may lose your 2FA codes, bank app and photos at once. That is why backups to the cloud and a strong screen lock matter (Section 3). In exam answers about choosing devices (Section 1), remember the trade-off: smartphones win on portability and connectivity but lose on screen size, input speed and processing power for heavy tasks like video editing or programming.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Mobile data, Wi-Fi and hotspots","html":"<p>Your phone can go online in two main ways.</p>\n<table><tr><th></th><th>Mobile data (cellular)</th><th>Wi-Fi</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Provided by</td><td>A mobile network operator through cell towers</td><td>A local access point (home, school, café)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Coverage</td><td>Wide: almost anywhere with a signal</td><td>Small area: tens of metres</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cost</td><td>Pay per plan or data bundle; can run out</td><td>Often free or part of a fixed home plan</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Speed</td><td>Depends on generation and signal</td><td>Usually fast, depends on the line behind it</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Security</td><td>Encrypted between phone and tower</td><td>Home Wi-Fi with WPA2/WPA3 is secure; <b>open public Wi-Fi is risky</b></td></tr></table>\n<h4>Hotspots</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Wi-Fi (Wireless Fidelity)</b> is a set of wireless standards (a protocol) that uses high-frequency radio signals to connect devices to a WLAN over distances of up to a few hundred feet. A device needs a <b>wireless adapter</b> (wireless NIC) to join.</li>\n<li>A <b>hotspot</b> is a specific location that provides Internet access through a WLAN: a public place where you can use Wi-Fi. A hotspot is created by a <b>modem and a wireless router</b>; its radio signals spread out from the centre and <b>weaken with distance and interference</b>. You find hotspots at airports such as PJIA, the Anguilla ferry terminal in Marigot, bus stops, Internet cafés, supermarkets, shopping malls and cafés on Front Street.</li>\n<li>A <b>personal (mobile) hotspot</b>, also called <b>tethering</b>, is when your phone shares its <b>mobile data</b> with other devices over Wi-Fi. Your phone acts as a tiny router and access point. Great for getting a laptop online to submit an assignment, but it uses up your data and battery fast.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Roaming on a shared island</h4>\n<p><b>Roaming</b> means using another operator's network outside your home network, often with extra charges. On our island, phones near the border or in Marigot may connect to a tower on the other side, so it is wise to check your settings and plan.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> Never say \"Wi-Fi is the Internet\". Wi-Fi is just the <b>wireless link to a local network</b>; that network still needs an ISP connection to reach the Internet.</div>","plain":"<b>Mobile data</b> comes from cell towers and works almost everywhere, but you pay for each bundle. <b>Wi-Fi</b> comes from a box nearby and is usually cheaper or free, but only works close by. A <b>hotspot</b> is a place, or your own phone, sharing an Internet connection by Wi-Fi.","deeper":"<p>Why is open public Wi-Fi risky? On a network with no password, other users can attempt to <b>eavesdrop</b> on unencrypted traffic, and criminals can set up a fake hotspot with a convincing name (\"PJIA_Free_WiFi\") to capture what you type: an <b>evil twin</b> attack. HTTPS (Unit 4) protects the contents of most websites, but you should still avoid banking on open Wi-Fi or use mobile data or a trusted VPN instead. Note also that phones increasingly use <b>Wi-Fi calling</b>, sending ordinary calls over Wi-Fi when the cellular signal is weak indoors, which blurs the line between the two.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Mobile data or Wi-Fi?","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>For each situation, choose the BEST way to get online.</p>","items":[{"text":"You are on the bus from St. George’s to Grenville and need to check your Teams class.","options":["Home Wi-Fi","Mobile data"],"answer":1,"fb":"On the move, only mobile data reaches you."},{"text":"You want to download a 3 GB game update at home and your data bundle is small.","options":["Home Wi-Fi","Mobile data"],"answer":0,"fb":"Big downloads on Wi-Fi save your bundle."},{"text":"At an airport café you want to log in to your bank app. The free Wi-Fi has no password.","options":["Mobile data","Open public Wi-Fi"],"answer":0,"fb":"Avoid open Wi-Fi for banking; mobile data is encrypted to the tower."},{"text":"Your laptop has no Internet, but your phone has a 4G signal and you must submit your SBA tonight.","options":["Personal hotspot (tethering)","Bluetooth earbuds"],"answer":0,"fb":"Tethering shares your phone's data with the laptop."},{"text":"A tourist from the USA wants to avoid roaming charges while sightseeing in St. George’s.","options":["Use hotel and café Wi-Fi or a local SIM/eSIM","Use home-country mobile data all day"],"answer":0,"fb":"Local Wi-Fi or a local SIM avoids roaming fees."},{"text":"You are streaming a 2-hour football match at home and Wi-Fi is available.","options":["Home Wi-Fi","Mobile data"],"answer":0,"fb":"Long HD streams use lots of data; Wi-Fi is cheaper."}],"win":"Good calls. Think coverage, cost and security every time.","tutor":[{"h":"Three questions","p":"<p>Am I moving? How much data? How sensitive is it?</p>"},{"h":"Open Wi-Fi rule","p":"<p>No password + sensitive data = use mobile data instead.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Mobile data or Wi-Fi? (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Choose mobile data or Wi-Fi.</p>","items":[{"text":"Checking messages on a moving bus.","options":["Mobile data","Wi-Fi"],"answer":0,"fb":"Mobile data works on the move."},{"text":"Downloading a huge game at home.","options":["Wi-Fi","Mobile data"],"answer":0,"fb":"Wi-Fi saves your data bundle."},{"text":"Sharing your phone's data with your laptop.","options":["Personal hotspot","Infrared"],"answer":0,"fb":"That is a hotspot."}]},"up":{"title":"Mobile data or Wi-Fi? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Choose the BEST decision and reason.</p>","items":[{"text":"A café hotspot named 'YoungSt_Free_WiFi2' appears beside the café's real network. What should you do?","options":["Ask staff for the real network name; it may be an evil twin","Connect and log in to your bank to test it","Connect: free is free"],"answer":0,"fb":"Fake hotspots imitate real names."},{"text":"A school has only 20 Mbps for 300 students. Students tether laptops from phones during exams prep. Main drawback?","options":["Tethering needs fibre","Uses students' paid data and drains batteries","Tethering is illegal"],"answer":1,"fb":"Hotspotting costs data and battery."},{"text":"A tour operator needs reliable connectivity on a catamaran trip to Carriacou. Best option?","options":["Rely on beach-bar Wi-Fi","Bluetooth","Mobile data with a regional roaming plan or local SIM, plus downloaded offline maps"],"answer":2,"fb":"Wide coverage needs cellular; plan for roaming."},{"text":"Indoors, your 5G signal is weak but home Wi-Fi is strong. How can calls still work?","options":["Infrared","Wi-Fi calling","NFC"],"answer":1,"fb":"Wi-Fi calling routes calls over Wi-Fi."},{"text":"Which is the most secure for online banking?","options":["Any network, because apps are always safe","Open café Wi-Fi","Home Wi-Fi with WPA3 or mobile data, on an HTTPS site"],"answer":2,"fb":"Encrypted link plus HTTPS."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"Bluetooth, NFC and contactless payment","html":"<p>Two short-range wireless technologies are built into almost every smartphone.</p>\n<figure class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 440 190\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Approximate ranges: NFC a few centimetres, Bluetooth about 10 metres, Wi-Fi tens of metres, a cell tower several kilometres, satellites cover whole regions\">\n<g font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"10\" y=\"30\" font-weight=\"700\">NFC</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"64\" font-weight=\"700\">Bluetooth</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"98\" font-weight=\"700\">Wi-Fi</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"132\" font-weight=\"700\">Cell tower</text><text x=\"10\" y=\"166\" font-weight=\"700\">Satellite</text>\n</g>\n<g stroke-width=\"10\" stroke-linecap=\"round\">\n<line x1=\"90\" y1=\"26\" x2=\"96\" y2=\"26\" stroke=\"#F5C542\"/><line x1=\"90\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"60\" stroke=\"#F5C542\"/><line x1=\"90\" y1=\"94\" x2=\"210\" y2=\"94\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\"/><line x1=\"90\" y1=\"128\" x2=\"320\" y2=\"128\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\"/><line x1=\"90\" y1=\"162\" x2=\"420\" y2=\"162\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-opacity=\".6\"/>\n</g>\n<g font-size=\"9\" fill=\"currentColor\">\n<text x=\"106\" y=\"30\">about 4 cm: tap to pay</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"64\">about 10 m: earbuds, car</text><text x=\"220\" y=\"98\">tens of metres: café, school</text><text x=\"330\" y=\"132\">several km</text><text x=\"250\" y=\"182\" text-anchor=\"middle\">whole regions and oceans (bars not to scale)</text>\n</g></svg><figcaption>Short range is not a weakness: NFC's tiny range is exactly what makes tap-to-pay hard to trigger by accident.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Bluetooth</h4>\n<ul><li>An open wireless standard introduced in <b>1994</b> as a wireless replacement for <b>RS-232 serial cables</b>.</li>\n<li>Short-range radio in the unlicensed <b>2.4 GHz</b> band, typically about <b>10 m (33 feet)</b>; the exact range depends on the device <b>class</b>. Early versions transferred about 1 Mbps; newer versions are much faster. Low power.</li>\n<li>Supports services such as file pushing (sending a file), voice transmission and replacing serial cables.</li>\n<li>Devices must be <b>paired</b> first (you confirm a code or tap \"Pair\").</li>\n<li>Uses: wireless earbuds and speakers, smartwatches, car hands-free calling, game controllers, sending a file to a nearby phone. It creates a <b>PAN</b> (Unit 1).</li>\n<li>Tip: turn Bluetooth off or make your device non-discoverable in crowded places to reduce the risk of unwanted connections.</li></ul>\n<h4>NFC: Near Field Communication</h4>\n<ul><li>Very short range: about <b>4 cm</b>. Devices must almost touch.</li>\n<li>No pairing needed: just <b>tap</b>.</li>\n<li>Uses: <b>contactless payment</b> with a card, phone or smartwatch; tapping a hotel key card; reading smart posters and tags.</li></ul>\n<h4>Contactless payment</h4>\n<p>At a Rue Capois store, you hold your phone near the card terminal. Your phone's NFC chip sends payment details, the terminal sends them to the bank over the Internet, and approval comes back in about a second. Phone wallets (such as Apple Pay or Google Wallet) send a <b>token</b> (a substitute number) instead of your real card number, and need your fingerprint, face or PIN, which makes them more secure than handing over a card.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> The <b>very short range</b> of NFC is an advantage: it is hard for a payment to happen by accident or for someone across the room to intercept it.</div>","plain":"<b>Bluetooth</b> connects gadgets that are a few metres apart, like earbuds and your phone, after you pair them. <b>NFC</b> works only when devices almost touch, like tapping your phone or card on a shop's card machine to pay.","deeper":"<p>Contactless payment is quick, hygienic and reduces cash handling for small businesses, which also lowers robbery risk. The risks: a lost <b>card</b> (unlike a phone wallet) can often be tapped for small amounts without a PIN, and fraudsters have tried \"skimming\" by holding a reader very close to people in crowds. Banks set <b>contactless limits</b> and fraud monitoring to manage this. A balanced exam answer to \"Evaluate contactless payment for a Carnival food vendor\" would weigh speed, fewer cash errors and safety against terminal fees, dependence on mobile data at a crowded venue, and customers without cards.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"GPS: where in the world am I?","html":"<p><b>GPS (Global Positioning System)</b> is a satellite-based system that lets a receiver work out its <b>exact location</b> (latitude, longitude and altitude) and the <b>time</b>, anywhere on Earth.</p>\n<figure class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 420 200\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"A phone on Earth receives timing signals from four GPS satellites and calculates its position\">\n<path d=\"M40,200 A190,90 0 0 1 380,200\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"50\" y=\"30\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" rx=\"3\"/><rect x=\"150\" y=\"12\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" rx=\"3\"/><rect x=\"250\" y=\"16\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" rx=\"3\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"40\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" rx=\"3\"/>\n<rect x=\"200\" y=\"128\" width=\"16\" height=\"26\" rx=\"3\" stroke=\"#F5C542\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n</g>\n<g stroke=\"#F5C542\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 4\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"><line x1=\"67\" y1=\"48\" x2=\"202\" y2=\"130\"/><line x1=\"167\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"206\" y2=\"128\"/><line x1=\"267\" y1=\"34\" x2=\"212\" y2=\"128\"/><line x1=\"357\" y1=\"58\" x2=\"216\" y2=\"132\"/></g>\n<g font-size=\"9\" fill=\"currentColor\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"67\" y=\"43\">sat 1</text><text x=\"167\" y=\"25\">sat 2</text><text x=\"267\" y=\"29\">sat 3</text><text x=\"357\" y=\"53\">sat 4</text>\n<text x=\"208\" y=\"172\">your phone (receiver only)</text><text x=\"208\" y=\"186\">works out distance to each satellite from signal timing</text>\n</g></svg><figcaption>GPS receivers only listen: each satellite broadcasts its position and the exact time; signals from at least four satellites give your position.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>How it works (simply)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>About 30 GPS satellites orbit the Earth, each constantly broadcasting its position and a very precise time signal.</li>\n<li>Your phone's GPS <b>receiver</b> picks up signals from several satellites and measures how long each signal took to arrive, which tells it how far away each satellite is.</li>\n<li>Using the distances to <b>at least four satellites</b>, it calculates its position. This method is called <b>trilateration</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>GPS is <b>receive-only</b> (simplex): the satellites never know who is using them. A phone can find its position <b>without mobile data</b>, but it needs data to download map images, unless you saved <b>offline maps</b> beforehand.</p>\n<h4>Uses</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Navigation</b>: drivers, delivery riders and taxis; hikers on Sweeney’s.</li>\n<li><b>Tracking</b>: courier parcels, school buses, fleets of rental cars.</li>\n<li><b>Fishing and boating</b>: marking fishing grounds and finding the way home safely.</li>\n<li><b>Emergency</b>: sharing an exact location with rescuers or the coast guard.</li>\n<li><b>Apps</b>: ride-hailing, food delivery, fitness tracking, geotagged photos.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Privacy concern:</b> apps that track your location can reveal where you live and go to school. Check which apps have location permission.</p>","plain":"<p>GPS uses satellites in space to tell your phone exactly where it is. The phone listens to signals from at least four satellites and works out its position from how long each signal took to arrive. It powers maps, delivery apps and fitness trackers.</p>","deeper":"<p>Why <b>four</b> satellites and not three? Three distances would be enough to fix a point in 3D if your phone had a perfect atomic clock, but it does not. The fourth satellite lets the receiver correct its own clock error. Today phones use <b>GNSS</b>: GPS (USA) plus other systems such as Galileo (Europe), GLONASS (Russia) and BeiDou (China), giving faster and more accurate fixes. <b>Assisted GPS (A-GPS)</b> uses the mobile network to download satellite data quickly, which is why location is faster with a signal. Trap: GPS position does not come from the cell tower, though phones can also estimate location roughly from nearby towers and Wi-Fi networks.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Which mobile technology?","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Each card describes something people do with their phones. Which technology makes it possible?</p>","main":"Which technology is being used?","bins":["Bluetooth","NFC","GPS","Mobile data (cellular)"],"items":[{"t":"Sending a WhatsApp voice note while walking down King Street.","bin":3,"fb":"Mobile data carries the message over the network."},{"t":"Streaming a live cricket match on the bus.","bin":3,"fb":"Wide-area Internet on the move = cellular data."},{"t":"A fishing boat records the location of a good fishing spot.","bin":2,"fb":"GPS records coordinates."},{"t":"A delivery rider's map shows her exact position on the way to Spanish Town.","bin":2,"fb":"Position comes from GPS."},{"t":"Tapping a hotel key card on the room door.","bin":1,"fb":"Very short-range tap: NFC."},{"t":"Paying for johnny cakes by tapping a phone on the card machine.","bin":1,"fb":"Tap = NFC."},{"t":"Listening to music on wireless earbuds.","bin":0,"fb":"Earbuds pair by Bluetooth."},{"t":"Talking hands-free through the car's speakers.","bin":0,"fb":"Car hands-free uses Bluetooth."}],"win":"All sorted. Tap = NFC, pair = Bluetooth, location = GPS, Internet on the move = mobile data.","tutor":[{"h":"Keyword spotting","p":"<i>Tap</i> means NFC. <i>Pair, earbuds, hands-free</i> mean Bluetooth. <i>Where am I</i> means GPS. <i>Internet while moving</i> means cellular."}],"easy":{"title":"Which mobile technology? (Foundation)","bins":["Bluetooth","NFC","GPS"],"items":[{"t":"Map shows where you are.","bin":2,"fb":"GPS."},{"t":"Tap to pay at a shop.","bin":1,"fb":"NFC."},{"t":"Car hands-free calling.","bin":0,"fb":"Bluetooth."},{"t":"Wireless earbuds.","bin":0,"fb":"Bluetooth."}]},"up":{"title":"Which mobile technology? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Careful: some cards need TWO technologies; choose the one that does the job described in bold.</p>","items":[{"t":"A ride-hailing app <b>shows the driver's position</b> to the passenger.","bin":2,"fb":"Position comes from the driver's GPS (shared over data)."},{"t":"The same app <b>sends that position</b> to the passenger's phone across the island.","bin":3,"fb":"Sending it requires mobile data."},{"t":"A smartwatch <b>sends heart-rate readings</b> to the phone in your pocket.","bin":0,"fb":"Watch-to-phone is Bluetooth."},{"t":"A smartwatch is <b>tapped on a terminal</b> to pay for a bus ticket.","bin":1,"fb":"NFC."},{"t":"A hiker's phone with no signal still <b>shows her position</b> on a downloaded offline map.","bin":2,"fb":"GPS works without data."},{"t":"A phone <b>shares its 4G connection</b> with a laptop.","bin":3,"fb":"The shared connection is cellular data (via a Wi-Fi hotspot)."}]}},{"type":"mcq","title":"Tap to pay on Harbour Drive","bloom":"Understand","q":"<p>Tamara buys sunglasses at a store on Harbour Drive. She holds her phone about 2 cm from the card terminal, confirms with her fingerprint, and the payment is approved. Which technology sent the payment details from her phone to the terminal?</p>","options":[{"t":"GPS","fb":"GPS finds location; it does not send payment data."},{"t":"Infrared","fb":"Infrared is used by remote controls, not phone payments."},{"t":"NFC (Near Field Communication)","fb":"Correct. NFC works over a few centimetres and needs no pairing.","ok":true},{"t":"Bluetooth","fb":"Bluetooth needs pairing and works over about 10 m; payments use NFC."}],"tutor":[{"h":"How close?","p":"<p>A couple of centimetres is the clue.</p>"},{"h":"No pairing","p":"<p>Payment taps do not ask you to pair devices.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Tap to pay on Harbour Drive (Foundation)","q":"<p>Tamara pays at a shop in George Town by tapping her phone on the card machine. Which technology does this use?</p>","options":[{"t":"GPS","fb":"GPS is for location."},{"t":"Satellite","fb":"Satellites are not involved in a tap."},{"t":"NFC","fb":"Yes: tap to pay.","ok":true}]},"up":{"title":"Tap to pay on Harbour Drive (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"<p>A Carnival Village food vendor is deciding whether to accept contactless (NFC) payments using a small terminal linked to her phone's mobile data. Which evaluation is MOST complete?</p>","options":[{"t":"It has no disadvantages, because NFC works without any network.","fb":"The terminal must reach the bank over a network."},{"t":"It is unsafe, because NFC can be intercepted from across the whole village.","fb":"NFC's range is about 4 cm."},{"t":"It is only useful for tourists, because locals never use cards.","fb":"An unsupported generalisation; many residents pay by card or phone."},{"t":"Faster queues and less cash to lose or be robbed of, but she pays transaction fees and depends on mobile data in a crowded venue, so she should keep a cash option.","fb":"Correct. Benefits, costs and a risk, plus a sensible conclusion.","ok":true}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"Mobile tech for business and education","html":"<h4>Business</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Mobile banking and payments</b>: customers pay bills and transfer money any time; vendors accept card and phone payments with mobile terminals.</li>\n<li><b>Marketing and sales</b>: small businesses sell through Instagram, TikTok and WhatsApp Business catalogues; a Simpson Bay boat-tour operator takes bookings while at sea.</li>\n<li><b>Working on the move (mobile workforce)</b>: an engineer repairing an air-conditioning system at a customer's villa looks up the manual, orders the part and video-calls a senior colleague from the site; sales reps check stock from the customer's office. Remote staff get the same up-to-date information as in the office, \"anytime, anywhere\".</li>\n<li><b>Location-based services</b>: delivery tracking, taxi dispatch, customer check-ins.</li>\n<li><b>Tourism</b>: cruise passengers find attractions, restaurants and tours from their phones.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Education</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Preparing students for the future</b>: using mobile devices responsibly is part of adult life and of many careers, so learning with them prepares students for the workforce.</li>\n<li><b>Up-to-date learning</b>: instant access to the latest news, information and statistics, instead of out-of-date encyclopaedias.</li>\n<li><b>An alternative to textbooks</b>: digital textbooks are lighter to carry, often cheaper, updated regularly and include videos and interactive features; they keep students organised.</li>\n<li><b>Learning outside the classroom</b>: students research from anywhere on campus, collaborate on projects and use study apps in their downtime. Schools do this through <b>BYOD</b> (Bring Your Own Device) or one-device-per-student (1:1) schemes.</li>\n<li><b>Access anywhere</b>: Google Classroom and Teams apps let students check assignments and join lessons on phones, even without a laptop at home.</li>\n<li><b>Communication</b>: class WhatsApp groups and school apps share notices with parents.</li>\n<li><b>Learning tools</b>: educational videos, dictionary and coding apps, recording interviews for SBA research, photographing notes.</li>\n<li><b>Continuity</b>: after Hurricane Irma damaged schools, mobile devices helped students keep learning while buildings were repaired.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>The flip side</h4>\n<ul><li>Not every student can afford a smartphone and data: the <b>digital divide</b>.</li>\n<li>Distraction from notifications and social media.</li>\n<li>Small screens are poor for long typing tasks.</li></ul>","plain":"<p>For business, phones let owners sell, get paid, advertise and manage staff from anywhere. For school, phones let you check Google Classroom, join classes and talk to teachers outside school hours. The catch: not everyone can afford a phone and data, and phones can be very distracting.</p>","deeper":"<p>Use the <b>stakeholder</b> lens for higher marks. A mobile payment system benefits the <b>vendor</b> (faster sales, less cash), the <b>customer</b> (convenience) and the <b>bank</b> (fees, data), but may disadvantage <b>older customers</b> or those without bank accounts. In education, the same app that helps a student in Dutch Quarter catch up also creates pressure to be \"always on\" for teachers. Showing that one technology affects different groups differently is exactly the kind of judgement \"assess the importance\" questions reward, and it previews Section 3 (Social and Economic Impact).</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"M-commerce: shopping by phone","html":"<p><b>Mobile commerce (m-commerce)</b> is buying and selling goods and services using mobile devices. It is part of <b>e-commerce</b> (trading over the Internet). Customers shop from the couch, on the bus or while waiting in a queue at the supermarket.</p>\n<h4>Why businesses go mobile</h4>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Easy store access</b>: customers no longer have to travel, walk the aisles and queue; the \"store\" is in their pocket. The website or app must be <b>mobile-optimised</b> (easy layout, quick checkout) or shoppers leave.</li>\n<li><b>Attracts new customers</b>: people find shops through mobile searches and mobile adverts; <b>re-targeting</b> ads remind recent visitors of items they looked at.</li>\n<li><b>A new marketing channel</b>: <b>beacons</b> (small Bluetooth sensors on shelves and displays) send deals to nearby phones, and <b>push notifications</b> remind shoppers about items left in their cart or new arrivals.</li>\n<li><b>Helps traditional retail sales</b>: in a Heerenstraat store, a customer can scan a product to read reviews, see a video of it in use and find in-store promotions, which encourages purchases.</li>\n<li><b>A growing market with high return on investment</b>: more people shop by phone every year, so a mobile-friendly shop gains an advantage over competitors, and every satisfied customer spreads the word.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Caribbean example:</b> the Willemstad boutique <i>Island Threads</i> lets cruise visitors browse its Instagram catalogue, pay by phone and collect at the pier before the ship leaves.</p>\n<p><b>Drawbacks</b>: small screens make comparing products harder, customers worry about card security, and the business must pay to build and update a mobile-friendly site or app.</p>","plain":"<p>M-commerce means shopping and selling with your phone. Businesses like it because customers can shop anywhere, new customers find them through mobile searches and ads, they can send deals straight to phones, and it even helps sales in the physical store. But the website must work well on a small screen.</p>","deeper":"<p>Notice how m-commerce links several technologies from this section: <b>mobile data or Wi-Fi</b> for access, <b>NFC</b> for in-store payment, <b>Bluetooth beacons</b> for location-based offers, <b>GPS</b> for delivery tracking and <b>HTTPS</b> to protect card details (Unit 4). An Evaluate answer should also consider <b>privacy</b>: re-targeting and beacons work by tracking what you look at and where you are. A business that explains clearly what it collects, and lets customers opt out, builds trust; one that hides it risks its reputation.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Mobile journalism and citizen journalism","html":"<p><b>Mobile journalism (\"mojo\")</b> is a way of telling news stories in which reporters are trained and equipped to work <b>fully mobile and independently</b>, using a smartphone to <b>shoot, edit and broadcast</b> from one device.</p>\n<h4>Why newsrooms use mobile journalism</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Publish instantly</b>: being first matters in news. A reporter at a Carnival parade or a hurricane press briefing can film, edit and send the story to the newsroom in minutes.</li>\n<li><b>Cost-effective</b>: a mid-range phone with a small tripod and clip-on microphone costs far less than a professional TV camera, so small Caribbean newsrooms can produce video stories.</li>\n<li><b>Portable</b>: the whole kit fits in a backpack and weighs a few kilograms, so stories can be produced anywhere, anytime.</li>\n<li>Large broadcasters even build apps that send video, audio and photos straight into their production systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Citizen journalism</h4>\n<p><b>Citizen journalism</b> is the collecting and reporting of news by <b>members of the public</b>, usually through social media. During a storm, residents' videos of flooding on Queen Street may reach people before any official report.</p>\n<table><tr><th>Benefits</th><th>Drawbacks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Very fast; eyewitness video from places reporters cannot reach; gives ordinary people a voice</td><td>Can be unprofessional, unverified or false; may spread rumours; may ignore privacy; can compete with and damage trust in reputable publishers</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> Mobile journalism is an example of how mobile networks make work possible \"anytime, anywhere\". Always pair a benefit with a risk (e.g. speed vs accuracy).</div>","plain":"<p>Mobile journalism means reporters use just a smartphone to film, edit and send news stories, quickly and cheaply, from anywhere. Citizen journalism is when ordinary people post news on social media themselves: fast, but not always accurate.</p>","deeper":"<p>Mobile journalism depends on <b>upload</b> bandwidth (Unit 2): a reporter sending a 2-minute HD clip from a crowded J'ouvert route may struggle when thousands of phones share the same cells, so newsrooms compress video or use bonded connections that combine several mobile links. Citizen journalism raises the source-evaluation skills you met in Section 1: check <b>authority</b> (who filmed it?), <b>currency</b> (is it today's flood or an old video re-posted?) and <b>accuracy</b> (do other sources confirm it?). Many false hurricane rumours spread on WhatsApp each season; verifying before sharing is part of digital citizenship (Section 3).</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Which field benefits?","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Each card describes a benefit of mobile networks. Choose the field it belongs to.</p>","items":[{"text":"Digital textbooks that are updated regularly and cheaper to carry.","options":["Journalism","Commerce","Education"],"answer":2,"fb":"An alternative to textbooks: education."},{"text":"A Bluetooth beacon sends a 20% voucher to shoppers walking past a display.","options":["Commerce","Journalism","Education"],"answer":0,"fb":"A new marketing channel: m-commerce."},{"text":"A reporter films, edits and sends a story from a press briefing in minutes.","options":["Commerce","Journalism","Education"],"answer":1,"fb":"Instant publishing: mobile journalism."},{"text":"Students research from anywhere on campus during group projects.","options":["Education","Journalism","Commerce"],"answer":0,"fb":"Learning outside the classroom."},{"text":"A push notification reminds a customer about items left in her cart.","options":["Journalism","Education","Commerce"],"answer":2,"fb":"M-commerce marketing."},{"text":"Residents post videos of flooding on social media before official reports.","options":["Journalism","Commerce","Education"],"answer":0,"fb":"Citizen journalism."}],"win":"Well matched. Mobile networks serve education, commerce and the news in different ways.","tutor":[{"h":"Who benefits?","p":"<p>Students and teachers = education. Shops and customers = commerce. Reporters and the public sharing news = journalism.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which field benefits? (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Reading a digital textbook on a tablet.","options":["Education","Commerce"],"answer":0,"fb":"Education."},{"text":"Buying shoes with a shopping app.","options":["Commerce","Education"],"answer":0,"fb":"Commerce."},{"text":"A reporter sends a video story from her phone.","options":["Commerce","Journalism"],"answer":1,"fb":"Journalism."}]},"up":{"title":"Which field benefits? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Choose the BEST evaluation for each case.</p>","items":[{"text":"A school wants BYOD. What is the strongest argument AND the main concern?","options":["Textbooks become free; no concerns","Phones replace teachers; no concerns","Up-to-date learning anywhere; but students without devices or data are left behind"],"answer":2,"fb":"Benefit plus digital-divide concern."},{"text":"A boutique's site is not mobile-optimised. Most likely result?","options":["No effect","More sales","Mobile shoppers leave without buying"],"answer":2,"fb":"Poor mobile experience loses customers."},{"text":"A viral video claims the airport is flooded. What should a news outlet do first?","options":["Ignore all citizen video","Verify the source, date and location before publishing","Publish immediately"],"answer":1,"fb":"Speed must not beat accuracy."},{"text":"Why are beacons and re-targeting a privacy issue?","options":["They need infrared","They track what shoppers view and where they are","They use fibre-optic cable"],"answer":1,"fb":"Tracking behaviour and location."},{"text":"Why is mojo especially useful for small Caribbean newsrooms?","options":["It needs a satellite truck","Low equipment cost and portability","It requires no Internet at all"],"answer":1,"fb":"Cheap, portable kit."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"Mobile tech in emergencies and telemedicine","html":"<h4>Before, during and after a hurricane</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Before</b>: storm warnings and evacuation information reach phones through apps, SMS and <b>emergency alerts</b> sent to every phone in an area.</li>\n<li><b>During</b>: family WhatsApp groups check in; GPS location can be shared with rescuers.</li>\n<li><b>After</b>: relief agencies coordinate deliveries by phone; people report damage with photos; mobile money and payments keep shops running when banks are closed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When towers lose power or are damaged, as happened after Hurricane Irma in 2017, operators bring in <b>generators</b>, <b>temporary mobile towers</b> (cell on wheels) and <b>satellite links</b>. Responders also carry <b>satellite phones</b>, which work even when the local network is completely down.</p>\n<h4>Telemedicine</h4>\n<p><b>Telemedicine</b> is the use of telecommunications to provide <b>health care at a distance</b>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Video consultations</b>: a patient in Sint Maarten speaks to a specialist overseas without the cost of flying.</li>\n<li><b>Remote monitoring</b>: a diabetic patient's glucose readings or a heart patient's blood pressure are sent from a home device via the phone to the clinic, which is alerted to dangerous readings.</li>\n<li><b>Sharing images</b>: X-rays and scans are sent securely to specialists for a second opinion.</li>\n<li><b>E-prescriptions and reminders</b> by app and SMS.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Telemedicine matters for small islands where some specialists do not live locally. Its limits: it needs a <b>reliable connection</b>, patients need a device and some skills, <b>privacy of medical data</b> must be protected, and some conditions need a physical examination.</p>","plain":"<p>In a hurricane, phones get warnings to people, help families check on each other, and let rescuers know exactly where people are. <b>Telemedicine</b> means seeing a doctor or sending health readings over the network, so you can get expert help even if the specialist is in another country.</p>","deeper":"<p>Telemedicine is a good example of a <b>system</b>: a device (glucose meter), a link (Bluetooth to the phone), a network (mobile data to the clinic's server), software (alerts when a reading is dangerous) and people (the nurse who calls back). If any link fails, during a storm for instance, the system fails, so good designs store readings until the connection returns. In an \"Evaluate\" answer, weigh <b>access and cost savings</b> against <b>connectivity, privacy and the limits of remote diagnosis</b>, then conclude: telemedicine <i>supplements</i> local care rather than replacing it.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Help after the hurricane","bloom":"Understand","intro":"<p>A youth group lists ways mobile technology helps people after a hurricane. Some claims are wrong.</p>","q":"Tap EVERY statement that is TRUE.","lines":["1. Emergency alerts can be sent to every phone in an area.","2. Phones work normally even when every cell tower has lost power.","3. Satellite phones can work when the local network is down.","4. GPS can help rescuers find someone's exact location.","5. Mobile networks never need repairs after storms.","6. Temporary towers and generators can restore coverage quickly."],"correct":[0,2,3,5],"win":"Correct. Alerts, satellite phones, GPS sharing and temporary towers all help.","miss":"Remember: phones need working towers (or satellites), and storms do damage networks.","tutor":[{"h":"Infrastructure matters","p":"<p>A normal phone needs a working tower with power. A satellite phone does not need local towers.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Help after the hurricane (Foundation)","q":"<p>Tap ALL the true statements (there are two).</p>","lines":["1. Phones can receive storm warnings.","2. Phones can work with no towers or satellites at all.","3. Satellite phones can work when local towers are down."],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Yes.","miss":"Ordinary phones need a working tower."},"up":{"title":"Help after the hurricane (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"<p>Tap EVERY statement that is a valid, well-reasoned point for a disaster communication plan.</p>","lines":["1. Keep power banks charged before the storm, because towers may be up but homes may lose power.","2. Rely only on social media, because it is always accurate during disasters.","3. Agree a family check-in plan using SMS, which can sometimes get through when data is congested.","4. Download offline maps, because GPS still works without mobile data.","5. Assume telemedicine will replace hospitals during a disaster.","6. Store copies of important documents in the cloud and on a device, because paper may be destroyed."],"correct":[0,2,3,5],"win":"A strong, realistic plan.","miss":"Watch the over-claims: social media is not always accurate, and telemedicine cannot replace hospitals."}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"learn","title":"Issues: coverage, cost and security","html":"<h4>Coverage on small islands</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Terrain</b>: hills and valleys cause <b>dead spots</b>; thick hurricane-proof concrete weakens signals indoors.</li>\n<li><b>Borders and roaming</b>: on a shared island, or near neighbouring islands like Anguilla and St. Barths, phones may connect to a foreign network.</li>\n<li><b>Sea</b>: signal fades quickly offshore, a safety issue for fishers and boaters.</li>\n<li><b>Investment</b>: small populations mean fewer customers to pay for new towers and 5G upgrades, so rollouts can lag behind big countries.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Cost</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Smartphones, data bundles and roaming fees are expensive for many families.</li>\n<li>Data caps run out quickly with video, which affects online learning.</li>\n<li>This creates a <b>digital divide</b> between those who can afford good access and those who cannot.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Security</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Lost or stolen phones</b> expose messages, photos and banking apps.</li>\n<li><b>Open public Wi-Fi</b> and fake hotspots allow eavesdropping.</li>\n<li><b>Malicious apps</b> and fake app links can steal data.</li>\n<li><b>Smishing</b>: phishing by SMS or WhatsApp (\"Your parcel is held, pay the fee here\").</li>\n<li><b>SIM-swap fraud</b>: a criminal persuades an operator to move your number to their SIM to receive your verification codes.</li>\n<li><b>Location tracking</b> by apps can reveal your routines.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Health and social issues</h4>\n<p>Distraction (including texting while driving), sleep loss, cyberbullying and over-dependence are also raised in exam questions, especially in Section 3.</p>","plain":"<p>Mobile technology has problems too. <b>Coverage</b>: hills and the sea cause dead spots, and you may roam near borders. <b>Cost</b>: phones and data are expensive, so not everyone has equal access. <b>Security</b>: phones get lost or hacked, public Wi-Fi can be unsafe, and scam texts try to trick you.</p>","deeper":"<p>Notice how the issues <b>interact</b>. Expensive data pushes students onto free public Wi-Fi, which raises security risk. Poor indoor coverage pushes people to Wi-Fi calling, which depends on the home Internet line and power. When you <b>evaluate</b> a mobile solution, say how a risk could be reduced: for example, \"SIM-swap fraud can be reduced by using an authenticator app instead of SMS codes and setting a SIM PIN with the operator\". Proposing realistic mitigations turns a list of problems into a judgement, and that is what earns the top marks.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Staying safe on mobile","html":"<p>A few habits make a huge difference. Think of them as your phone's \"hurricane shutters\".</p>\n<table><tr><th>Risk</th><th>Countermeasure</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Phone lost or stolen</td><td>Screen lock with PIN, fingerprint or face; turn on Find My Device so you can locate, lock or <b>remotely wipe</b> it</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Data loss</td><td>Automatic <b>cloud backup</b> of photos and contacts</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Malicious apps</td><td>Install only from official app stores; check permissions; delete apps you don't use</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Security holes</td><td>Install system and app <b>updates</b> promptly</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Public Wi-Fi snooping</td><td>Use mobile data or a trusted VPN for sensitive tasks; check for HTTPS; \"forget\" open networks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Unwanted Bluetooth connections</td><td>Turn Bluetooth off or set it to non-discoverable when not needed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Smishing and scams</td><td>Do not tap links in unexpected messages; verify with the company using a number you find yourself</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Account takeover / SIM swap</td><td>Use an <b>authenticator app</b> for two-factor authentication; set a PIN on your SIM and mobile account</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Location tracking</td><td>Give location permission only \"while using the app\"</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> When asked for a security measure, name it <b>and</b> say what it protects against: \"Enable remote wipe, so that if the phone is stolen the thief cannot access the bank app.\"</div>","plain":"<p>Lock your phone, back it up, update it, only install apps from the official store, be careful on free Wi-Fi, switch off Bluetooth when you are not using it, and never tap links in strange messages. These habits protect you if your phone is lost or someone tries to trick you.</p>","deeper":"<p>Security is always a balance with <b>usability</b>. A 6-digit PIN plus biometrics is strong and still quick; a 20-character password typed on every unlock is so annoying that people disable it, which makes them <i>less</i> secure. Organisations handle staff phones with <b>mobile device management (MDM)</b>: they can enforce screen locks, separate work apps from personal ones, and wipe only the work data if a phone is lost. That idea, matching controls to risk and users, comes back in Section 3.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Coverage, cost or security?","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Each card describes a problem with mobile technology on a Caribbean island. Classify the main issue.</p>","main":"Which type of issue is this?","bins":["Coverage","Cost","Security"],"items":[{"t":"A tourist gets a large bill after using data while roaming.","bin":1,"fb":"Roaming charges: cost."},{"t":"A stolen phone with no screen lock exposes a banking app.","bin":2,"fb":"Unprotected device: security."},{"t":"A house in a valley behind Spanish Town has no signal indoors.","bin":0,"fb":"Terrain dead spot: coverage."},{"t":"A fisherman loses signal 5 km offshore.","bin":0,"fb":"Signal range: coverage."},{"t":"A student cannot join online class because her data bundle ran out.","bin":1,"fb":"Paying for data: cost."},{"t":"A fake text claims a parcel is waiting and asks for card details.","bin":2,"fb":"Smishing: security."},{"t":"A family cannot afford a smartphone for each child.","bin":1,"fb":"Device cost: digital divide."},{"t":"A criminal moves a victim's number to a new SIM to receive her codes.","bin":2,"fb":"SIM-swap fraud: security."}],"win":"Well analysed. Most real problems fit one of these three headings.","tutor":[{"h":"Ask what causes it","p":"<p>Signal problems = coverage. Money problems = cost. Someone stealing or tricking = security.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Coverage, cost or security? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"No signal in a valley.","bin":0,"fb":"Coverage."},{"t":"Data bundle too expensive.","bin":1,"fb":"Cost."},{"t":"A scam text asks for your password.","bin":2,"fb":"Security."},{"t":"Phone stolen with no lock.","bin":2,"fb":"Security."}]},"up":{"title":"Coverage, cost or security? (Challenge)","bins":["Coverage","Cost","Security","Social/health"],"items":[{"t":"Higher-frequency 5G signals struggle through thick concrete walls.","bin":0,"fb":"Coverage."},{"t":"Drivers texting on the Airport Road cause accidents.","bin":3,"fb":"Distraction: social/health."},{"t":"Students lose sleep scrolling late at night.","bin":3,"fb":"Health."},{"t":"Roaming near the border adds unexpected charges.","bin":1,"fb":"Cost."},{"t":"An app keeps tracking a student's location in the background.","bin":2,"fb":"Privacy/security."},{"t":"A fake 'Free_Airport_WiFi' hotspot captures passwords.","bin":2,"fb":"Evil twin: security."},{"t":"A small population makes new towers hard to pay for, so 5G arrives late.","bin":1,"fb":"Investment cost."}]}},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Going cashless at the food truck","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<b>Auntie Mavis</b> runs a johnny-cake and saltfish food truck that parks near the La Romana bridge and at Carnival Village. Customers keep asking to pay by card or phone. Help her go mobile wisely.","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Mavis asks: \"What do I need to accept card and phone payments?\"</p>","choices":[{"t":"A mobile card terminal that accepts contactless (NFC), linked to her phone or a SIM.","to":"n2","fb":"Right: NFC for taps, mobile data to reach the bank."},{"t":"A GPS tracker on the truck.","to":"bad1","fb":"GPS gives location; it does not take payments."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Should the terminal connect through her phone's mobile data or the free Wi-Fi from the bar next door?</p>","choices":[{"t":"The bar's open Wi-Fi: it is free.","to":"bad2","fb":"Open Wi-Fi is risky for payments and she loses it when she moves."},{"t":"Her own mobile data: it moves with the truck and is not an open network.","to":"n3","fb":"Good: coverage plus security."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>At Carnival Village, thousands of phones crowd the network and data slows down. What should she plan?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Keep a cash option and choose a terminal that can store or queue transactions briefly.","to":"n4","fb":"A sensible backup for congestion."},{"t":"Turn customers away if card payments are slow.","to":"bad3","fb":"Lost sales: always have a fallback."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Mavis wants customers to find the truck each day. Which is the smartest mobile idea?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Post her location daily on Instagram/WhatsApp with a map pin from GPS.","to":"good","fb":"Location sharing + social media = more customers."},{"t":"Share her bank PIN in the WhatsApp group so customers can pay her directly.","to":"bad4","fb":"Never share a PIN."}]},"good":{"text":"<b>Business is booming!</b> Contactless payments over secure mobile data, a cash backup, and daily GPS location posts. Mavis's queue is shorter and her takings are safer.","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<b>Wrong tool.</b> GPS tells you where you are; payments need an NFC card terminal and a network connection.","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<b>Risky.</b> Public open Wi-Fi can be snooped on and does not move with the truck.","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<b>Lost sales.</b> Network congestion at big events is common; always keep a fallback.","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<b>Security disaster.</b> A PIN must never be shared; anyone could empty her account.","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Match tech to task","p":"<p>Taps = NFC; reaching the bank = mobile data; finding the truck = GPS.</p>"},{"h":"Plan for failure","p":"<p>Busy events overload networks. What is the backup?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Going cashless at the food truck (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Help Auntie Mavis take card payments at her food truck.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Which technology lets customers TAP to pay?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Bluetooth earbuds","to":"bad1","fb":"Earbuds are for music."},{"t":"NFC","to":"n2","fb":"Yes."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>How should the card machine reach the bank?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Her own mobile data","to":"good","fb":"Yes: it moves with the truck."},{"t":"A cable to the bank","to":"bad2","fb":"The truck moves around."}]},"good":{"text":"<b>Great!</b> NFC taps and mobile data.","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<b>Not quite.</b> Tap-to-pay uses NFC.","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<b>Impossible.</b> A moving truck needs a wireless connection.","end":"bad"}}},"up":{"title":"Going cashless at the food truck (Challenge)","intro":"<p>Auntie Mavis now has two trucks and wants to expand. Make expert decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>She wants to see both trucks' sales live on her phone. What setup do you recommend?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Each truck keeps a paper book and texts totals at night.","to":"bad1","fb":"No live view and more errors."},{"t":"A cloud-based point-of-sale app on each truck's tablet, syncing over mobile data.","to":"n2","fb":"Cloud sync gives live totals anywhere."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>A message arrives: \"Your POS account is locked, log in here to fix it\" with a strange link. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Tap the link quickly before sales are lost.","to":"bad2","fb":"Classic smishing trick."},{"t":"Ignore the link and contact the POS company using the number in the official app.","to":"n3","fb":"That was smishing."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>How should she protect the POS account?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Strong unique password plus an authenticator-app second factor, not SMS codes.","to":"n4","fb":"Resists SIM-swap attacks."},{"t":"Use her birthday as the password so staff remember it.","to":"bad3","fb":"Easy to guess and shared."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>During hurricane season, how does she protect the business?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Cloud backups of sales records, charged power banks, and a cash fallback if networks go down.","to":"good","fb":"Resilient."},{"t":"Keep all records only on one tablet in the truck.","to":"bad4","fb":"One flood and everything is gone."}]},"good":{"text":"<b>Expert level.</b> Cloud POS, smishing spotted, strong 2FA and a hurricane plan.","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<b>Too slow.</b> Live cloud sync would show her sales instantly.","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<b>Account stolen.</b> Never log in through links in unexpected messages.","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<b>Weak security.</b> Guessable, shared passwords invite fraud.","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<b>Data lost.</b> Back up off the device.","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"mcq","title":"Telemedicine for Mr Lake","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"<p>Mr Lake, 68, lives in Bartica and has a heart condition. His cardiologist is overseas. The clinic proposes a home blood-pressure monitor that sends readings by Bluetooth to his phone, which forwards them over mobile data to the clinic each day, plus monthly video consultations. Which is the BEST evaluation?</p>","options":[{"t":"It is perfect: telemedicine can replace every hospital visit.","fb":"Some conditions need physical examination and tests."},{"t":"It will not work, because Bluetooth cannot reach overseas.","fb":"Bluetooth only links the monitor to the phone; mobile data carries the readings overseas."},{"t":"It is too risky, because GPS will reveal his medical records.","fb":"GPS gives location only; it does not hold medical records."},{"t":"It saves him costly trips and catches dangerous readings early, but depends on reliable data, his ability to use the phone and secure handling of his health data, so the clinic should train him and keep in-person check-ups.","fb":"Correct. Benefits, limits and a sensible recommendation.","ok":true}],"tutor":[{"h":"Follow the data","p":"<p>Monitor → Bluetooth → phone → mobile data → clinic. Which link does what?</p>"},{"h":"Evaluate = both sides","p":"<p>Give benefits, limits, then a judgement.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Telemedicine for Mr Lake (Foundation)","q":"<p>Mr Lake lives in Guyana. His heart specialist is in another country. His blood-pressure readings are sent from home to the specialist using his phone. What is this an example of?</p>","options":[{"t":"Contactless payment","fb":"That is paying by tap."},{"t":"Telemedicine","fb":"Yes: health care at a distance.","ok":true},{"t":"Online gaming","fb":"Not related."}]},"up":{"title":"Telemedicine for Mr Lake (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","q":"<p>Mr Lake's readings stopped reaching the clinic for three days after a storm, and his phone showed 'No Service'. His monitor still showed new readings. Which analysis is MOST accurate, and what design change would help?</p>","options":[{"t":"Nothing can be done; telemedicine never works after storms.","fb":"Store-and-forward and alerts make it resilient."},{"t":"The mobile data link failed (no service), not Bluetooth; the app should store readings and upload them when service returns, and the clinic should be alerted when readings stop.","fb":"Correct. You located the failed link and proposed store-and-forward plus an alert.","ok":true},{"t":"GPS failed, so readings could not be sent.","fb":"GPS is not used to send readings."},{"t":"The Bluetooth link failed, so the monitor must be replaced.","fb":"The monitor and phone are side by side; the 'No Service' message points to the cellular link."}]}},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"The right tech for the job","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Choose the mobile technology that BEST fits each need.</p>","items":[{"text":"A fishing boat must know its exact position to return to a reef.","options":["Bluetooth","NFC","GPS"],"answer":2,"fb":"Position = GPS."},{"text":"A passenger pays for a bus ride by tapping a smartwatch.","options":["Microwave","GPS","NFC"],"answer":2,"fb":"Tap = NFC."},{"text":"A city wants thousands of flood sensors to report in real time.","options":["Infrared","5G","2G"],"answer":1,"fb":"Massive IoT and low latency = 5G."},{"text":"A grandmother wants to hear calls through her hearing aid wirelessly.","options":["Satellite","Bluetooth","GPS"],"answer":1,"fb":"Short-range audio = Bluetooth."},{"text":"A rescue team needs to call abroad while all local towers are down.","options":["Wi-Fi","NFC","Satellite phone"],"answer":2,"fb":"Satellite needs no local towers."},{"text":"A student needs Internet for a laptop at the beach using her phone's 4G.","options":["Infrared","NFC","Personal hotspot"],"answer":2,"fb":"Tethering shares phone data."}],"win":"Excellent matching of technology to need.","tutor":[{"h":"Think range and purpose","p":"<p>Centimetres: NFC. Metres: Bluetooth. Building: Wi-Fi. Island: cellular. Anywhere: satellite. Position: GPS.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"The right tech for the job (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Find your location on a map.","options":["GPS","NFC"],"answer":0,"fb":"GPS."},{"text":"Tap to pay.","options":["NFC","GPS"],"answer":0,"fb":"NFC."},{"text":"Wireless earbuds.","options":["Bluetooth","Satellite"],"answer":0,"fb":"Bluetooth."}]},"up":{"title":"The right tech for the job (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Each case has a constraint. Choose the BEST option.</p>","items":[{"text":"Hikers on a remote trail with no mobile signal want to see their position on a map.","options":["GPS with offline maps downloaded beforehand","Mobile data maps","NFC tags"],"answer":0,"fb":"GPS works without data; maps must be offline."},{"text":"A surgeon wants to guide a robot remotely with almost no delay.","options":["5G","3G","4G"],"answer":0,"fb":"Very low latency is 5G's key feature."},{"text":"A shop wants a payment method that cannot be triggered from across the room.","options":["Wi-Fi","Bluetooth","NFC"],"answer":2,"fb":"NFC's tiny range is a security feature."},{"text":"A family's alarm system still uses 2G and the operator is switching 2G off. What should they do?","options":["Upgrade to a 4G/5G-capable alarm module","Nothing","Switch to infrared"],"answer":0,"fb":"Sunset networks leave old devices without service."},{"text":"A tourist wants to avoid roaming fees for a week's stay.","options":["Buy a local SIM or eSIM","Use Bluetooth","Use roaming data all week"],"answer":0,"fb":"Local SIM avoids roaming charges."}]}},{"type":"write","title":"Structured question: mobile tech for Island Taxis","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Structured question: mobile communication technologies (14 marks)","stimulus":"<p><b>Island Taxis Co-op</b> has 40 drivers serving the cruise pier, Queen Beatrix Airport airport and hotels. It plans a smartphone app so tourists can book rides, track their driver and pay by card or phone. Drivers will use 4G or 5G phones. Some drivers say phone data is too expensive, and the co-op worries about lost phones and scams.</p>","prompt":"<p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Identify</b> the mobile technology that lets the app show the driver's location, and briefly <b>explain</b> how it finds the location. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> TWO differences between 4G and 5G. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Tourists will pay by tapping a phone on the driver's terminal. <b>Name</b> this technology and <b>state</b> ONE advantage of it. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> TWO ways the app benefits the co-op's business. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Describe</b> ONE issue the co-op may face (coverage, cost or security) and <b>suggest</b> how to reduce it. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":100,"max":420,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> The app uses ... The phone's receiver ...<br><b>(b)</b> First, 5G ... whereas 4G ... Second, ...<br><b>(c)</b> This is ... One advantage is ...<br><b>(d)</b> One benefit is ... This helps the co-op because ... Another benefit ...<br><b>(e)</b> One issue is ... This could be reduced by ...</p>","checklist":["I named GPS and explained satellites + signals + distance/timing.","I gave two real 4G/5G differences (speed, latency, number of devices).","I named NFC and gave a genuine advantage.","I explained (not just listed) two business benefits.","I described one issue AND a practical way to reduce it."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"GPS","marks":3,"look":["\\bGPS\\b|global positioning","satellite","signal|tim|distance|trilaterat|four|4"],"min":3,"strong":"GPS explained.","weak":"GPS: the phone receives timing signals from at least four satellites and calculates its distance from each to find its position."},{"criterion":"4G vs 5G","marks":2,"look":["faster|speed|higher.{0,10}(data|bandwidth)","latency|delay|more devices|iot|capacity"],"min":2,"strong":"Two differences.","weak":"5G has faster speeds and lower latency, and supports more connected devices."},{"criterion":"NFC and advantage","marks":2,"look":["\\bNFC\\b|near field","fast|quick|convenien|secure|token|short range|no cash|hygien"],"min":2,"strong":"Named with advantage.","weak":"NFC; fast, convenient, secure (short range, tokens)."},{"criterion":"Business benefits","marks":4,"look":["book|order","track|locat|eta|wait","pay|cash|card|revenue|record","custom|tourist|satisf|more (rides|business)"],"min":3,"strong":"Two explained benefits.","weak":"Explain e.g. easier bookings → more rides; tracking → less waiting, happier tourists; card payments → less cash, automatic records."},{"criterion":"Issue and mitigation","marks":3,"look":["coverage|signal|dead spot|cost|expensive|data|secur|lost|stolen|scam|smish|phish","reduc|solution|could|should|by (using|giving|providing)|subsid|bundle|lock|remote wipe|2fa|two.?factor|wi.?fi"],"min":2,"strong":"Issue described and mitigated.","weak":"Describe one issue (e.g. data cost) and how to reduce it (e.g. co-op business data plan)."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> GPS (Global Positioning System). The driver's phone has a GPS receiver that picks up timing signals from at least four satellites. From how long each signal takes to arrive it calculates the distance to each satellite and so works out its exact position, which the app sends to the tourist over mobile data.</p><p><b>(b)</b> 5G gives much faster data speeds than 4G. 5G also has much lower latency (delay) and can support many more connected devices in the same area.</p><p><b>(c)</b> NFC (Near Field Communication). It is fast and convenient: the tourist taps and pays in about a second, without carrying local cash, and phone wallets use tokens so the real card number is not shared.</p><p><b>(d)</b> First, tourists can book rides from the cruise pier or hotel in a few taps, so drivers get more bookings, including from visitors who do not know local taxi numbers. Second, card and phone payments are recorded automatically, so drivers carry less cash (safer) and the co-op has accurate records of every fare for accounts and disputes.</p><p><b>(e)</b> Cost: some drivers cannot afford enough data to keep the app running all day, so they may miss bookings. The co-op could negotiate a group business data plan with an operator, or subsidise data for members from booking fees.</p>","key":"<p>(a) 1 GPS; 1 satellites; 1 timing/distance/four satellites. (b) 1 mark each: speed; latency; number of devices. (c) 1 NFC; 1 advantage. (d) 2 marks per benefit: 1 identified, 1 explained in context. (e) 1 issue identified, 1 described, 1 realistic mitigation.</p>","teacherTip":"Reject 'Bluetooth' for (c) and 'Wi-Fi' for (a). In (d), an unexplained list ('booking, tracking') earns only 2 of 4.","easy":{"title":"Structured question: mobile tech for Island Taxis (Foundation)","genre":"Structured question: mobile communication technologies (8 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Name</b> the technology that shows where the taxi is on the map. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> the technology used to tap a phone to pay. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li><b>State</b> ONE advantage of 5G over 4G. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li><b>State</b> TWO ways the app helps tourists. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> ONE problem with the app and ONE way to fix it. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol>","frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> ...<br><b>(b)</b> ...<br><b>(c)</b> 5G is ...<br><b>(d)</b> Tourists can ... They can also ...<br><b>(e)</b> One problem is ... This can be fixed by ...</p>","min":30,"max":220,"checklist":["GPS named.","NFC named.","One 5G advantage.","Two ways it helps tourists.","A problem and a fix."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"GPS","marks":1,"look":["\\bGPS\\b|global positioning"],"min":1,"strong":"Correct.","weak":"GPS."},{"criterion":"NFC","marks":1,"look":["\\bNFC\\b|near field"],"min":1,"strong":"Correct.","weak":"NFC."},{"criterion":"5G advantage","marks":1,"look":["faster|speed|latency|delay|more devices"],"min":1,"strong":"Correct.","weak":"5G is faster / has less delay."},{"criterion":"Helps tourists","marks":2,"look":["book|order","track|see where|locat|pay|card|wait"],"min":2,"strong":"Two ways.","weak":"Book easily; see where the driver is; pay without cash."},{"criterion":"Problem and fix","marks":3,"look":["cost|expensive|data|signal|coverage|lost|stolen|scam","fix|solv|reduc|could|should|by "],"min":2,"strong":"Problem and fix.","weak":"E.g. data is expensive; the co-op could buy a group data plan."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> GPS.</p><p><b>(b)</b> NFC (Near Field Communication).</p><p><b>(c)</b> 5G is faster and has less delay.</p><p><b>(d)</b> Tourists can book a taxi easily from the pier, and they can see where their driver is so they know how long to wait.</p><p><b>(e)</b> One problem is that data is expensive for drivers. This can be fixed by the co-op buying a cheaper group data plan.</p>","key":"<p>(a) 1 GPS. (b) 1 NFC (Near Field Communication). (c) 1 faster speed, lower latency (less delay) or more devices connected. (d) 1 mark each for two ways the app helps tourists: book a taxi easily; see where the driver is/how long to wait; pay by card or phone without cash. (e) 1 problem identified (data cost, poor coverage, lost or stolen phones, scams); 1 for saying who it affects or why it matters; 1 realistic fix that matches the problem.</p>","teacherTip":"Reject 'Wi-Fi' in (a) and 'Bluetooth' in (b): the answers are GPS and NFC. In (d), both ways must help tourists, not drivers. In (e), the fix must match the problem named: a group data plan answers cost, while screen locks and remote wipe answer lost phones."},"up":{"title":"Structured question: mobile tech for Island Taxis (Challenge)","genre":"Structured question: mobile communication technologies, with evaluation (19 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Explain</b> how GPS determines the driver's location, and why the app still needs mobile data. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Distinguish</b> between 4G and 5G, giving ONE way 5G could benefit the co-op in future. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Compare</b> NFC and Bluetooth, and <b>justify</b> why NFC is used for payment. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> TWO business benefits of the app. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Assess</b> the importance of the app to the co-op, considering coverage, cost and security, and reach a conclusion. <i>(5 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":180,"max":600,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> GPS ... but mobile data is needed to ...<br><b>(c)</b> NFC works over ... whereas Bluetooth ... NFC is better for payment because ...<br><b>(e)</b> On one hand ... On the other hand ... Overall, I judge that ... because ...</p>","checklist":["GPS explained, plus why data is still needed (send location, load maps).","4G/5G distinction and a future benefit.","NFC vs Bluetooth on range/pairing and a payment justification.","Two explained business benefits.","A balanced assessment of coverage, cost AND security with a clear conclusion."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"GPS and data","marks":4,"look":["satellite","signal|tim|distance|trilaterat|four|4","data|internet|cellular","send|share|transmit|map|book"],"min":3,"strong":"Full explanation.","weak":"GPS receiver + satellites + timing; data needed to send the location and load maps/bookings."},{"criterion":"4G vs 5G","marks":3,"look":["faster|speed","latency|delay|devices|iot","future|could|self.?driving|real.?time|sensor|fleet"],"min":2,"strong":"Distinction and benefit.","weak":"5G: faster, lower latency, more devices; e.g. real-time fleet sensors."},{"criterion":"NFC vs Bluetooth","marks":3,"look":["\\bNFC\\b","bluetooth","cm|centimet|short|pair|10 ?m|range|accident|intercept"],"min":3,"strong":"Compared and justified.","weak":"NFC ~4 cm, no pairing; Bluetooth ~10 m with pairing; NFC's short range makes accidental or remote payments unlikely."},{"criterion":"Business benefits","marks":4,"look":["book","track|locat|wait","pay|cash|record","more|increase|satisf|efficien"],"min":3,"strong":"Two explained benefits.","weak":"Explain each benefit with its effect on the co-op."},{"criterion":"Assessment","marks":5,"look":["coverage|signal|dead spot","cost|expensive|data","secur|lost|stolen|scam|smish|fraud","overall|conclu|judge|therefore|on balance"],"min":4,"strong":"Balanced judgement.","weak":"Discuss coverage, cost and security with mitigations, then conclude."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> The driver's phone contains a GPS receiver that listens to signals from GPS satellites. Each signal carries the satellite's position and precise time; by measuring how long each signal took to arrive, the phone calculates its distance from at least four satellites and works out its position (trilateration). GPS only tells the phone where it is, so mobile data is still needed to send that location to the co-op's server and the tourist's app, and to load map tiles and booking information.</p><p><b>(b)</b> 5G offers much higher speeds, much lower latency and many more connected devices per area than 4G. In future the co-op could use 5G to monitor every vehicle's sensors in real time (fuel, faults, location), improving maintenance and dispatch.</p><p><b>(c)</b> NFC works only over about 4 cm and needs no pairing, whereas Bluetooth works over about 10 m and requires pairing. NFC is used for payment because its tiny range means a payment cannot be triggered accidentally or intercepted from across the taxi, and a tap is faster than pairing.</p><p><b>(d)</b> Bookings: tourists from cruise ships can book instantly, increasing rides and income at busy times. Payments and records: card/phone payments reduce the cash drivers carry, making them safer, and every fare is recorded automatically, reducing disputes and simplifying accounts.</p><p><b>(e)</b> The app could greatly increase business, because tourists expect to book and pay by phone. However, coverage dead spots in hilly areas may delay location updates, though GPS still works and updates resume when signal returns. Data cost could exclude some drivers, but a group business plan could solve this. Security risks include lost phones and smishing; screen locks, remote wipe and 2FA reduce them. Overall, I judge the app to be very important to the co-op's future, because its benefits to bookings, safety and records outweigh risks that can be managed with sensible measures.</p>","key":"<p>(a) 1 the phone's GPS receiver picks up signals from GPS satellites; 1 it uses the signal timing to work out its distance from at least four satellites and calculates its position (trilateration); 1 mobile data is needed to send the location to the co-op's server and the tourist's app; 1 mobile data is needed to load maps and booking information. (b) 1 mark each for two differences (higher speed; lower latency; more connected devices per area); 1 a realistic future benefit for the co-op (e.g. real-time monitoring of every vehicle's sensors). (c) 1 NFC works over about 4 cm with no pairing; 1 Bluetooth works over about 10 m and needs pairing; 1 justification: the tiny range means payments cannot be triggered accidentally or intercepted from a distance, and a tap is quick. (d) For each of two benefits: 1 identified, 1 effect on the co-op explained (e.g. more bookings and income; less cash carried and every fare recorded). (e) 1 mark each for coverage, cost and security, each discussed with a mitigation; 1 for weighing these against the benefits to the co-op; 1 for a clear, justified conclusion.</p>","teacherTip":"In (a), many students stop at 'GPS uses satellites'; full marks need the timing/distance idea AND the reason mobile data is still needed (GPS only receives; it cannot send the location). In (c), the justification mark needs the security idea: the tiny range means a payment cannot be triggered from across the taxi. In (e), cap answers without a final judgement at 4, and do not credit a factor that is mentioned without any mitigation."}}]},{"id":"S2U4","title":"The Web and web technologies","objectives":["Distinguish between the Internet and the World Wide Web (SO 2.4)","Explain web page, website, web server, web browser, hyperlink and home page (SO 2.4)","Identify the parts of a URL and explain domain names and top-level domains (SO 2.4)","Explain IP addresses, DNS, HTML, HTTP and HTTPS and how they work together (SO 2.4)","Explain ISPs, uploading, downloading, FTP and search engines (SO 2.4)","Describe e-mail, blogs, vlogs, podcasts, wikis, forums, instant messaging, social media, VoIP and cloud computing, with advantages and disadvantages (SO 2.4)"],"videos":[{"title":"Internet vs World Wide Web","q":"difference between internet and world wide web explained"},{"title":"How DNS works","q":"how DNS works explained simply"},{"title":"HTTP vs HTTPS","q":"http vs https explained encryption padlock"},{"title":"What is cloud computing?","q":"what is cloud computing explained for beginners"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"The Internet is not the Web","html":"<p><b>Specific Objective 2.4</b> asks you to explain how key web technology concepts <b>fit together</b>. Start with the most common mix-up in the whole syllabus.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <b>Internet</b> is a <b>global network of interconnected computer networks</b>. It is the <b>infrastructure</b>: the cables (including undersea fibre), routers, ISPs and the rules (protocols such as TCP/IP) that move data between them. <b>No single person, company or government owns or controls the Internet</b>; it works because every connected network agrees to use the same protocols.</li>\n<li>The <b>World Wide Web (WWW)</b> is a <b>collection of linked web pages and other resources</b>, stored on web servers and accessed <b>over the Internet</b> using a web browser and the HTTP/HTTPS protocol.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 420 180\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"The Internet is the infrastructure at the bottom; services such as the World Wide Web, e-mail, VoIP, FTP and online gaming run on top of it\">\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke-width=\"1.6\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"120\" width=\"400\" height=\"50\" rx=\"10\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<rect x=\"14\" y=\"20\" width=\"110\" height=\"80\" rx=\"8\" stroke=\"#F5C542\" stroke-width=\"2.2\"/>\n<rect x=\"134\" y=\"40\" width=\"64\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/><rect x=\"206\" y=\"40\" width=\"64\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<rect x=\"278\" y=\"40\" width=\"64\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/><rect x=\"350\" y=\"40\" width=\"60\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n</g>\n<g font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"210\" y=\"142\" font-weight=\"700\">THE INTERNET</text><text x=\"210\" y=\"157\" font-size=\"9\">global network of networks: cables, routers, ISPs, IP addresses</text>\n<text x=\"69\" y=\"45\" font-weight=\"700\">World Wide Web</text><text x=\"69\" y=\"62\" font-size=\"9\">web pages linked</text><text x=\"69\" y=\"74\" font-size=\"9\">by hyperlinks,</text><text x=\"69\" y=\"86\" font-size=\"9\">sent with HTTP(S)</text>\n<text x=\"166\" y=\"66\">E-mail</text><text x=\"166\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"9\">SMTP</text><text x=\"238\" y=\"66\">VoIP</text><text x=\"238\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"9\">calls</text>\n<text x=\"310\" y=\"66\">FTP</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"9\">file transfer</text><text x=\"380\" y=\"66\">Gaming,</text><text x=\"380\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"9\">streaming</text>\n<text x=\"210\" y=\"14\" font-size=\"9\">services that USE the Internet</text>\n</g></svg><figcaption>The Internet is the road network; the Web is just one kind of traffic on it, alongside e-mail, VoIP, FTP and more.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Another way to say it: the Web is a <b>network of online content formatted in HTML and accessed via HTTP</b>, an information-sharing model <b>built on top of</b> the Internet. It was designed in <b>1991 by Tim Berners-Lee</b> at CERN, which chose to give the invention away free, which is why anyone can publish on the Web today. The early Web was mostly text pages run by organisations with the skill to set up a web server and write HTML; today it includes interactive social media and user-generated content that needs little or no technical skill.</p>\n<p>So the Web is just <b>one service</b> that runs on the Internet. Others include:</p>\n<ul><li><b>E-mail</b> (your Outlook or Gmail messages)</li><li><b>VoIP</b> calls such as WhatsApp or Teams calls</li><li><b>FTP</b> file transfers</li><li>online gaming and many app connections</li></ul>\n<p>When you send a WhatsApp voice note you are using the <b>Internet</b>, but not the <b>Web</b>. When you open The Bridgetown Courier's website in a browser, you are using the <b>Web</b>, which travels over the Internet.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> \"Distinguish between the Internet and the World Wide Web\" (2 marks): \"The Internet is the global network of networks (the hardware and connections), <b>whereas</b> the WWW is a collection of linked web pages accessed over the Internet using browsers and HTTP.\"</div>","plain":"<p>The <b>Internet</b> is like the island's road network: the physical connections that carry everything. The <b>Web</b> is like the buses on those roads: one service that uses them. E-mail, WhatsApp calls and online games use the same roads but are not the Web.</p>","deeper":"<p>History helps the distinction stick. The Internet grew from research networks in the late 1960s and 1970s; the World Wide Web was invented much later, around 1989–1991, by Tim Berners-Lee at CERN, who combined three ideas: <b>HTML</b> (to write pages), <b>HTTP</b> (to send them) and <b>URLs</b> (to address them). The Internet existed for about twenty years before the Web. Exam trap: \"I'm on the Internet\" and \"I'm on the Web\" are used loosely in everyday speech, but in CSEC answers precision earns the marks.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Pages, sites, servers and browsers","html":"<table><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Web page</b></td><td>A single document on the Web, written in HTML, that can contain text, images, video and hyperlinks</td><td>The \"Fixtures\" page of a cricket club's site</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Website</b></td><td>A collection of related files and web pages (text, images, digital media) under one domain name, linked together. A website may also be a forum, webmail service, blog or search engine</td><td>A site, www.coralreefcafe.gy, with Home, Menu and Contact pages</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Home page</b></td><td>The initial or main page of a website, also called the <b>index page</b>, front page or main page; it holds navigation buttons or links to the other pages</td><td>The page that loads when you type just the domain name (often index.html)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Web server</b></td><td>A computer program that <b>dispenses web pages as they are requested</b>, using HTTP; the machine it runs on is also called the web server. It stores websites and is connected to the Internet. Every computer that hosts a website must run web server software</td><td>The server, possibly in a data centre overseas, that hosts the café's site</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Web browser</b></td><td>Application software that <b>requests, retrieves, translates (interprets) HTML/XHTML and displays</b> web pages</td><td>Chrome, Edge, Safari, Firefox</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Hyperlink</b></td><td>An element that links a <b>source</b> to a <b>target</b>: clicking it takes you to another document, another part of the same page, a file or an e-mail address. It can appear as text (often blue or purple and underlined), an image, a URL or a control such as a button. A text link is called <b>anchor text</b></td><td>\"Book a table\" on the café's home page</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Web hosting</b></td><td>A service that rents space on a web server so a website is available on the Internet</td><td>The café pays a hosting company each year</td></tr></table>\n<h4>How they work together</h4>\n<p>You click a <b>hyperlink</b> in your <b>browser</b>. The browser sends a request to the <b>web server</b>, which sends back the <b>web page</b>. The browser reads the HTML and displays the page. That is <b>client–server</b> again (Unit 1): the browser is the client.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> Do not confuse a <b>browser</b> (Chrome) with a <b>search engine</b> (Google Search). The browser is the program that shows pages; a search engine is a website that helps you find pages.</div>\n<p><button type=\"button\" class=\"btn ghost\" data-gx=\"opengame\" data-page=\"games/websim.html?level=1\" data-id=\"websim\">🌐 Try it now: build a two-page site with a hyperlink in the Web Page Builder (Level 1)</button></p>","plain":"<p>A <b>web page</b> is one page; a <b>website</b> is a group of linked pages, like a book is a group of pages. The <b>web server</b> is the computer that stores the website and sends pages out. The <b>browser</b> (Chrome, Safari) is the app on your device that asks for pages and shows them. <b>Hyperlinks</b> are the clickable bits that jump you to another page.</p>","deeper":"<p>The browser is doing a lot more than \"showing\" a page. One web page can trigger <b>dozens of separate requests</b>: the HTML, then each image, font, style sheet and script, sometimes from different servers (for example, a video embedded from a video-sharing site). That is why a page can load partly and why slow sites frustrate users, which links to <b>load time</b> when you evaluate websites in Section 4. In your SBA web page component you will create pages, link them with hyperlinks of four types (to another page, to a bookmark on the same page, to an e-mail address and to a file), and publish them to a web server.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Web vocabulary","bloom":"Remember","intro":"<p>Match each term to its meaning.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Web page","r":"A single HTML document on the Web"},{"l":"Website","r":"A collection of related, linked web pages under one domain"},{"l":"Web server","r":"Stores websites and sends pages when requested"},{"l":"Web browser","r":"Software that requests and displays web pages"},{"l":"Hyperlink","r":"Clickable text or image that takes you to another resource"},{"l":"World Wide Web","r":"All the linked web pages accessed over the Internet"}],"win":"Vocabulary sorted. These definitions earn easy marks in Paper 02.","tutor":[{"h":"Page vs site","p":"<p>One page vs a collection of pages.</p>"},{"h":"Server vs browser","p":"<p>The server stores and sends; the browser asks and displays.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Web vocabulary (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Web page","r":"One page"},{"l":"Website","r":"Many linked pages"},{"l":"Web browser","r":"App that shows pages, like Chrome"},{"l":"Hyperlink","r":"Clickable link"}]},"up":{"title":"Web vocabulary (Challenge)","bloom":"Understand","pairs":[{"l":"Internet","r":"Global network of networks: the infrastructure"},{"l":"World Wide Web","r":"A service of linked pages using HTTP over the Internet"},{"l":"Web hosting","r":"Renting server space so a site is available online"},{"l":"Home page","r":"The main entry page of a website"},{"l":"Browser","r":"Client software that renders HTML"},{"l":"Web server","r":"Responds to HTTP requests with resources"},{"l":"Search engine","r":"A website that indexes pages so users can find them"}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"URLs: reading a web address","html":"<p>A <b>URL (Uniform Resource Locator)</b> is the <b>unique address of a resource</b> (page, image or file) on the Web.</p>\n<figure class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 440 130\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"The URL https://www.islandnews.aw/sports/carnival.html labelled: protocol, subdomain, domain name, top-level domain, path and file name\">\n<text x=\"10\" y=\"50\" font-size=\"15\" font-family=\"monospace\" fill=\"currentColor\">https://www.islandnews.aw/sports/carnival.html</text>\n<g stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\">\n<line x1=\"10\" y1=\"58\" x2=\"50\" y2=\"58\" stroke=\"#F5C542\"/><line x1=\"82\" y1=\"58\" x2=\"108\" y2=\"58\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\"/><line x1=\"118\" y1=\"58\" x2=\"208\" y2=\"58\" stroke=\"#F5C542\"/>\n<line x1=\"210\" y1=\"58\" x2=\"228\" y2=\"58\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\"/><line x1=\"230\" y1=\"58\" x2=\"296\" y2=\"58\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/><line x1=\"298\" y1=\"58\" x2=\"410\" y2=\"58\" stroke=\"#F5C542\"/>\n</g>\n<g font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"30\" y=\"78\">protocol</text><text x=\"95\" y=\"96\">subdomain</text><text x=\"95\" y=\"108\" font-size=\"9\">(host)</text><text x=\"163\" y=\"78\">domain name</text>\n<text x=\"219\" y=\"96\">TLD</text><text x=\"219\" y=\"108\" font-size=\"9\">(.aw)</text><text x=\"263\" y=\"78\">path</text><text x=\"263\" y=\"90\" font-size=\"9\">(folder)</text><text x=\"354\" y=\"78\">file name</text>\n<text x=\"220\" y=\"20\" font-size=\"9\">URL = Uniform Resource Locator: the full address of one resource on the Web</text>\n</g></svg><figcaption>Read a URL left to right: how to fetch it (protocol), which server (domain), where on the server (path) and which file.</figcaption></figure>\n<table><tr><th>Part</th><th>In the example</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Protocol</b></td><td>https://</td><td>The rules used to fetch the resource. HTTPS means secure (encrypted) HTTP.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Subdomain / host</b></td><td>www</td><td>A specific server or section of the domain (often \"www\").</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Domain name</b></td><td>islandnews.aw</td><td>The human-friendly name of the website; it identifies the web server.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Top-level domain (TLD)</b></td><td>.aw</td><td>The last part of the domain: a country code or category.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Path</b></td><td>/sports/</td><td>The folder (directory) on the server.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>File name</b></td><td>carnival.html</td><td>The specific file (web page) requested.</td></tr></table>\n<p>Many textbooks and mark schemes call the whole of <code>www.islandnews.aw</code> the \"domain name\". Either way is accepted, as long as you identify the parts clearly.</p>\n<h4>Spot-the-fake skills</h4>\n<p>Scammers make URLs that <i>look</i> right. In <code>https://islandbank.aw.secure-login.example/</code>, the real domain is <b>secure-login.example</b>, not islandbank.aw, because the domain is read <b>just before the first single slash</b>, from the right.</p>","plain":"<p>A URL is a web address, like a home address for a web page. Read it in order: <b>https</b> (how to get it), <b>www.islandnews.aw</b> (which website), <b>/sports/</b> (which folder) and <b>carnival.html</b> (which page).</p>","deeper":"<p>URLs can carry more: a <b>port</b> (<code>:8080</code>), a <b>query string</b> after <code>?</code> (<code>?q=guavaberry</code>, which sends search words to the server) and a <b>fragment</b> after <code>#</code> (<code>#fixtures</code>, which jumps to a bookmark on the page, one of the four hyperlink types in Section 4). Real-world security skill: the true domain is the part just before the first single <code>/</code> after the protocol, read from the right. <code>islandbank.aw.login-check.example</code> belongs to <b>login-check.example</b>. Phishing links depend on people reading from the left; in Section 3 you will see why checking this matters.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Domain names and top-level domains","html":"<p>A <b>domain name</b> is a unique, human-readable name that identifies a website or server on the Internet, such as <code>coralreefcafe.vc</code>. Organisations <b>register</b> domain names (usually for a yearly fee) through a registrar.</p>\n<h4>Top-level domains (TLDs)</h4>\n<table><tr><th>Generic TLD</th><th>Usually for</th><th>Country-code TLD</th><th>Territory</th></tr>\n<tr><td>.com</td><td>commercial businesses</td><td>.vc</td><td>St. Vincent and the Grenadines</td></tr>\n<tr><td>.org</td><td>organisations, often non-profit</td><td>.jm</td><td>Jamaica</td></tr>\n<tr><td>.net</td><td>network providers (now used widely)</td><td>.tt</td><td>Trinidad and Tobago</td></tr>\n<tr><td>.edu</td><td>educational institutions (mainly US universities)</td><td>.bb</td><td>Barbados</td></tr>\n<tr><td>.gov</td><td>government (mainly US)</td><td>.gy</td><td>Guyana</td></tr>\n<tr><td>.mil</td><td>US military</td><td>.lc</td><td>Saint Lucia</td></tr>\n<tr><td>.info</td><td>information sites</td><td>.ai</td><td>Anguilla</td></tr></table>\n<p>Country-code TLDs can have second levels, such as <code>.edu.jm</code>, <code>.gov.tt</code> or <code>.com.bb</code>, so a Jamaican school might use <code>school.edu.jm</code>.</p>\n<h4>Why the TLD matters</h4>\n<ul><li>It gives a <b>clue</b> about who runs the site: <code>.gov</code> or <code>.gov.tt</code> suggests an official government source, useful when judging reliability (Section 1).</li>\n<li>But it is only a clue: anyone can buy most <code>.com</code>, <code>.org</code> or <code>.info</code> names, so always check the content too.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> If asked to \"identify the top-level domain\" in <code>www.carnival.org.vc</code>, the answer is <b>.vc</b> (the very last part). <code>.org</code> is a second-level domain under .vc.</div>","plain":"<p>A domain name is the name of a website, like <code>coralreefcafe.vc</code>. The last part (the top-level domain) gives a clue about the site: <b>.com</b> for business, <b>.org</b> for organisations, <b>.edu</b> for education, <b>.gov</b> for government, and country codes like <b>.vc</b> for St. Vincent and the Grenadines or <b>.jm</b> for Jamaica.</p>","deeper":"<p>Some country codes have become globally popular for reasons that have nothing to do with the country: <code>.ai</code> (Anguilla) is in heavy demand from technology companies, and <code>.tv</code> (Tuvalu) from video sites. Registration fees for these names have become a significant source of income for those small territories, a real example of how small island states can benefit from the Internet economy. It also shows why a TLD is <b>not proof</b> of where a site is based: a <code>.ai</code> site is very unlikely to be in Anguilla.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Dissect the URL","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Read this URL carefully: <code>https://www.axaferries.ai/schedules/anguilla.html</code> (a site).</p>","q":"Tap EVERY statement that is correct about this URL.","lines":["1. The protocol is https.","2. The top-level domain is .ai.","3. The file name is schedules.","4. The path (folder) is /schedules/.","5. The domain name includes axaferries.","6. The protocol shows the connection is not encrypted."],"correct":[0,1,3,4],"win":"Precise reading! https, .ai, /schedules/, axaferries and anguilla.html.","miss":"Check: the file name is anguilla.html, and https means encrypted.","tutor":[{"h":"Left to right","p":"<p>Protocol, then domain, then path, then file.</p>"},{"h":"The S in HTTPS","p":"<p>S = Secure: encrypted.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Dissect the URL (Foundation)","q":"<p>Tap ALL the correct statements (there are two).</p>","lines":["1. The protocol is https.","2. The file name is www.","3. The top-level domain is .ai."],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Right: https and .ai.","miss":"www is the subdomain, not the file name."},"up":{"title":"Dissect the URL (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Now examine this suspicious link sent in a text message: <code>http://axaferries.ai.ticket-refund.example/claim?id=4471</code></p>","q":"<p>Tap EVERY statement that is correct.</p>","lines":["1. The real (registered) domain is ticket-refund.example.","2. The site belongs to axaferries.ai because that name appears first.","3. The protocol is not encrypted.","4. '?id=4471' is a query string sending data to the server.","5. The TLD is .ai.","6. This link shows several warning signs of phishing."],"correct":[0,2,3,5],"win":"Expert eye. You read the domain from the right and spotted the warning signs.","miss":"Read the domain from the right, just before the first single slash. The TLD here is .example."}},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Name that part","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Look at <code>https://www.cxc-revision.org.jm/it/networks/topologies.html</code> (a site). Identify each part.</p>","items":[{"text":"https","options":["Path","Protocol","Domain name"],"answer":1,"fb":"How the page is fetched."},{"text":".jm","options":["File name","Top-level domain","Protocol"],"answer":1,"fb":"The last part of the domain: Jamaica's country code."},{"text":"/it/networks/","options":["Subdomain","Path","Top-level domain"],"answer":1,"fb":"Folders on the server."},{"text":"topologies.html","options":["Domain name","Protocol","File name"],"answer":2,"fb":"The specific page."},{"text":"cxc-revision.org.jm","options":["File name","Domain name","Path"],"answer":1,"fb":"The registered name of the site."},{"text":"www","options":["Top-level domain","File name","Subdomain (host)"],"answer":2,"fb":"The host part."}],"win":"Every part identified. You can read any URL now.","tutor":[{"h":"Find the slashes","p":"<p>After https:// the first chunk until the next / is the domain. Everything after is path and file.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Name that part (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Look at <code>https://www.school.gy/news.html</code>.</p>","items":[{"text":"https","options":["File name","Protocol"],"answer":1,"fb":"Protocol."},{"text":".gy","options":["Top-level domain","Protocol"],"answer":0,"fb":"TLD."},{"text":"news.html","options":["Domain","File name"],"answer":1,"fb":"File name."}]},"up":{"title":"Name that part (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Answer about different URLs.</p>","items":[{"text":"In <code>https://shop.example.com:8443/cart?item=72#pay</code>, what is <code>?item=72</code>?","options":["Path","Query string","Port"],"answer":1,"fb":"Data sent to the server after ?."},{"text":"In the same URL, what is <code>#pay</code>?","options":["Protocol","Fragment (bookmark on the page)","TLD"],"answer":1,"fb":"A fragment jumps to a section of the page."},{"text":"In <code>http://www.gov.tt/services</code>, the TLD is","options":[".gov","www",".tt"],"answer":2,"fb":"The last part: .tt."},{"text":"Which URL is encrypted?","options":["https://bank.example","http://bank.example","ftp://bank.example"],"answer":0,"fb":"HTTPS."},{"text":"In <code>login.islandbank.gy.verify-now.example</code>, which organisation controls the site?","options":["islandbank.gy","login","verify-now.example"],"answer":2,"fb":"Read from the right: verify-now.example."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"IP addresses and DNS","html":"<h4>IP address</h4>\n<p>An <b>IP address (Internet Protocol address)</b> is a <b>unique number that identifies a device on a network</b> so data can be sent to it. Routers use IP addresses to deliver data across the Internet.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>IPv4</b>: four numbers from 0 to 255 separated by dots, e.g. <code>192.0.2.45</code>. That allows only about 4.3 billion addresses, which have run out.</li>\n<li><b>IPv6</b>: much longer, written in hexadecimal, e.g. <code>2001:db8::45</code>, allowing a practically unlimited number of addresses.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>DNS: the Internet's contact list</h4>\n<p>People remember names like <code>islandnews.vg</code>; computers need numbers. The <b>Domain Name System (DNS)</b> <b>translates domain names into IP addresses</b>.</p>\n<figure class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 440 190\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"DNS lookup: the browser asks a DNS server for the IP address of a domain, receives it, then contacts the web server at that address\">\n<defs><marker id=\"ah4\" viewBox=\"0 0 10 10\" refX=\"9\" refY=\"5\" markerWidth=\"6\" markerHeight=\"6\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z\" fill=\"#F5C542\"/></marker></defs>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"70\" width=\"90\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\"/><rect x=\"330\" y=\"10\" width=\"100\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\"/><rect x=\"330\" y=\"130\" width=\"100\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\" stroke=\"#F5C542\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n</g>\n<g stroke=\"#F5C542\" stroke-width=\"1.8\" fill=\"none\">\n<line x1=\"100\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"326\" y2=\"30\" marker-end=\"url(#ah4)\"/><line x1=\"326\" y1=\"48\" x2=\"104\" y2=\"94\" marker-end=\"url(#ah4)\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 3\"/>\n<line x1=\"100\" y1=\"108\" x2=\"326\" y2=\"150\" marker-end=\"url(#ah4)\"/><line x1=\"326\" y1=\"168\" x2=\"104\" y2=\"118\" marker-end=\"url(#ah4)\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 3\"/>\n</g>\n<g font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"55\" y=\"92\" font-weight=\"700\">Your browser</text><text x=\"55\" y=\"106\" font-size=\"9\">islandnews.vg?</text>\n<text x=\"380\" y=\"32\" font-weight=\"700\">DNS server</text><text x=\"380\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"9\">name → number</text>\n<text x=\"380\" y=\"152\" font-weight=\"700\">Web server</text><text x=\"380\" y=\"166\" font-size=\"9\">192.0.2.45</text>\n<text x=\"210\" y=\"44\" font-size=\"9\">1. \"What is the IP of islandnews.vg?\"</text><text x=\"210\" y=\"82\" font-size=\"9\">2. \"It is 192.0.2.45\"</text>\n<text x=\"210\" y=\"122\" font-size=\"9\">3. HTTPS request for the page</text><text x=\"214\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"9\">4. Server sends the HTML page</text>\n</g></svg><figcaption>DNS is the Internet's contact list: you remember the name, the network needs the number.</figcaption></figure>\n<ol>\n<li>You type <code>www.islandnews.vg</code> into the browser.</li>\n<li>Your device asks a <b>DNS server</b> (usually your ISP's): \"What is the IP address of www.islandnews.vg?\"</li>\n<li>The DNS server replies with the IP address (finding it from other DNS servers if it doesn't know).</li>\n<li>Your browser contacts the web server at that IP address and requests the page.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>It is just like your phone's contacts: you tap \"Mommy\" and the phone dials the number stored for that name.</p>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> State the function of DNS in one sentence: \"DNS translates human-readable domain names into the numerical IP addresses that computers use to locate servers.\"</div>","plain":"<p>Every device on the Internet has a number called an <b>IP address</b>, like a phone number. But numbers are hard to remember, so we use names like <code>islandnews.vg</code>. <b>DNS</b> is the giant contact list that looks up the name and gives back the number, so your browser can find the right server.</p>","deeper":"<p>DNS is <b>hierarchical</b>: root servers know where the TLD servers are (.vg, .com), TLD servers know where each domain's own name servers are, and those give the final answer. Answers are <b>cached</b> (stored for a while) by your device and ISP so repeat visits are fast. Try it: <code>ping www.example.com</code> in Command Prompt shows the IP address DNS returned. If DNS fails, websites seem \"down\" even though the Internet connection works; typing the IP address directly would still reach the server. Criminals can poison DNS caches or change a router's DNS settings to send you to a fake site with the real name in the address bar: this is <b>pharming</b>, which you will meet in Section 3.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"What happens when you press Enter?","bloom":"Understand","intro":"<p>Jaden types <code>www.tciferries.tc</code> into his browser and presses Enter. Put the steps in the correct order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"The browser takes the domain name Jaden typed."},{"t":"The device asks a DNS server for the IP address of the domain."},{"t":"The DNS server replies with the web server's IP address."},{"t":"The browser sends an HTTPS request to the web server at that IP address."},{"t":"The web server sends back the HTML page and its files."},{"t":"The browser renders (displays) the page."}],"win":"Perfect sequence: name → DNS → IP → request → response → display.","tutor":[{"h":"Name before number","p":"<p>The browser can't contact the server until it knows the IP address.</p>"},{"h":"Display last","p":"<p>Rendering happens only after the files arrive.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"What happens when you press Enter? (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put these four steps in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Type the web address."},{"t":"DNS finds the IP address."},{"t":"The web server sends the page."},{"t":"The browser shows the page."}]},"up":{"title":"What happens when you press Enter? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>A detailed version. Order every step.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"The browser reads the URL and identifies the protocol (https) and domain."},{"t":"The device checks its own DNS cache; it has no record for this domain."},{"t":"The ISP's DNS server is asked, and it queries other DNS servers in the hierarchy if needed."},{"t":"The IP address is returned and cached for next time."},{"t":"The browser and server set up an encrypted TLS connection (the padlock)."},{"t":"The browser sends an HTTP GET request for /index.html."},{"t":"The server responds with the HTML; the browser requests the images and style sheets it references."},{"t":"The browser renders the complete page."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"HTML, HTTP and HTTPS","html":"<h4>HTML</h4>\n<p><b>HTML (HyperText Markup Language)</b> is the <b>language used to create web pages</b>. It uses <b>tags</b> in angle brackets to mark up the structure of the content, which the browser then displays.</p>\n<pre>&lt;h1&gt;Coral Reef Café&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;p&gt;Fresh johnny cakes every morning in Bodden Town.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;img src=\"johnnycakes.jpg\" alt=\"A plate of golden johnny cakes\"&gt;\n&lt;a href=\"menu.html\"&gt;See our menu&lt;/a&gt;</pre>\n<p><code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> is a main heading, <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> a paragraph, <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> an image (with <b>alt text</b> for screen readers) and <code>&lt;a href&gt;</code> a <b>hyperlink</b>. HTML is a <b>markup language</b>: it tells the browser how to structure and display text, images and other multimedia. (Some notes call it a simple programming language, but strictly it describes content rather than giving step-by-step instructions.)</p>\n<p><b>XHTML (Extensible HTML)</b>, introduced in 2000, is a <b>stricter</b> version of HTML with more rules, so that every browser could handle the code in the same way. Today the standard is <b>HTML5</b>, which kept many of XHTML's good habits.</p>\n<p>You do not have to type tags by hand: an <b>authoring tool</b> (authorware) such as a web page builder lets you create hypertext and multimedia pages by arranging objects (text, pictures, sound) without programming expertise.</p>\n<h4>HTTP and HTTPS</h4>\n<p>A <b>protocol</b> is an agreed-upon format (set of rules) for transmitting data between two devices. Three key Internet protocols are HTTP, FTP and TCP.</p>\n<p><b>HTTP (HyperText Transfer Protocol)</b> is the <b>set of rules for transferring files (text, images, sound, video and other multimedia) on the World Wide Web</b> between web servers and browsers. It is a <b>request–response</b> protocol: the browser sends a <b>request</b>; the server sends a <b>response</b>. As soon as you open a browser, you are using HTTP.</p>\n<p><b>TCP (Transmission Control Protocol)</b> makes transmission <b>reliable</b> on LANs, MANs and WANs. Data is split into <b>packets</b>, sent independently across the network, then checked and <b>reassembled</b> in the right order at the destination. HTTP runs on top of TCP connections.</p>\n<p><b>HTTPS (HTTP Secure)</b> is HTTP with <b>encryption</b> (using TLS). Data between the browser and server is scrambled, so anyone intercepting it (for example on public Wi-Fi) sees only gibberish.</p>\n<table><tr><th></th><th>HTTP</th><th>HTTPS</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Encrypted?</td><td>No: data is sent as plain text</td><td>Yes: data is encrypted</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Browser shows</td><td>\"Not secure\" warning</td><td>Padlock icon</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Proves the site's identity?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes: uses a digital certificate for that domain</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Use for</td><td>Should no longer be used for any site</td><td>Everything, and essential for log-ins and payments</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> HTTPS protects data <b>in transit</b>. The padlock does <b>not</b> mean a site is honest: scammers can get certificates too. Check the domain name as well.</div>","plain":"<b>HTML</b> is the code language used to build web pages, using tags like &lt;h1&gt; for headings. <b>HTTP</b> is the set of rules for sending those pages from the server to your browser. <b>HTTPS</b> is the secure version: it scrambles (encrypts) the data, shown by a padlock, so nobody can read your passwords on the way.","deeper":"<p>HTML, HTTP and URLs are the Web's three founding technologies and they depend on each other: a URL says <b>where</b>, HTTP says <b>how to fetch</b>, HTML says <b>what it looks like</b> once it arrives. Modern pages add <b>CSS</b> (style: fonts, colours, layout) and <b>JavaScript</b> (behaviour: menus, games, form checks). Exam trap: HTTPS encrypts the <b>connection</b> and confirms you are connected to the server that owns that domain, but it cannot tell you whether that domain belongs to a trustworthy organisation. A phishing site at a look-alike domain can still show a padlock. Strong answers mention both encryption and checking the domain.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"Paying school fees online","bloom":"Apply","q":"<p>Ms Hodge is paying her son's school fees on the portal of a school in Paramaribo. The payment page address begins <code>http://</code> and the browser shows a 'Not secure' warning. What should she conclude?</p>","options":[{"t":"The page is safe because it belongs to a school.","fb":"Even genuine organisations make mistakes; without HTTPS the data is not encrypted."},{"t":"The connection is not encrypted, so card details could be intercepted; she should not pay on this page and should contact the school.","fb":"Correct. HTTP sends data as plain text; payment pages must use HTTPS.","ok":true},{"t":"HTTP is faster, so it is better for payments.","fb":"Speed is not the issue; security is."},{"t":"The warning means the school's website has been deleted.","fb":"It means the connection is not encrypted, not that the site is gone."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Read the protocol","p":"<p>http:// vs https://: which one is encrypted?</p>"},{"h":"What travels?","p":"<p>Card numbers travel from browser to server. Who could see them?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Paying school fees online (Foundation)","q":"<p>Ms Hodge wants to pay school fees online. Which address shows the page is secure (encrypted)?</p>","options":[{"t":"http://pay.school.example","fb":"HTTP is not encrypted."},{"t":"ftp://pay.school.example","fb":"FTP is for transferring files, not secure payments."},{"t":"https://pay.school.example","fb":"Yes: HTTPS is encrypted.","ok":true}]},"up":{"title":"Paying school fees online (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"<p>Ms Hodge receives an SMS: 'Pay school fees now at https://bayleafhill-fees.example to avoid a late penalty.' The page shows a padlock and the school's logo. Which is the BEST evaluation?</p>","options":[{"t":"It is safe: a padlock proves the site belongs to the school.","fb":"Certificates prove control of a domain, not that the domain is the school's."},{"t":"It is safe because the logo is correct.","fb":"Logos are easy to copy."},{"t":"The padlock only shows the connection is encrypted; the domain does not obviously belong to the school, so she should go to the school's official site herself or call the school before paying.","fb":"Correct. HTTPS ≠ trustworthy. Verify the domain independently.","ok":true},{"t":"It is unsafe because all HTTPS sites are scams.","fb":"Most HTTPS sites are legitimate; HTTPS is good, just not proof of honesty."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"ISPs, uploading, downloading and FTP","html":"<h4>Internet Service Provider (ISP)</h4>\n<p>An <b>ISP</b> is a company that <b>provides individuals and organisations with access to the Internet</b>, usually for a monthly fee. ISPs may also provide e-mail accounts, web hosting, domain registration and technical support. In Trinidad and Tobago, homes and businesses connect through local telecom and cable companies acting as ISPs, and they in turn connect to the world through submarine cables.</p>\n<h4>Download vs upload</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Download</b>: transferring data <b>from</b> a remote computer (server) <b>to</b> your device. Streaming a soca playlist, saving a PDF from Google Classroom, installing an app.</li>\n<li><b>Upload</b>: transferring data <b>from</b> your device <b>to</b> a remote computer. Posting a TikTok, submitting your SBA to Teams, backing up photos to the cloud.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Many home plans give <b>faster download than upload</b> speeds, because most people download more than they upload.</p>\n<h4>FTP: File Transfer Protocol</h4>\n<p><b>FTP</b> is a protocol (set of rules) used to <b>transfer files between computers over a network</b>, especially to <b>upload website files</b> to a web server. A web designer uses an FTP program (such as FileZilla) to log in to the hosting server and upload <code>index.html</code>, the images and the style sheet, so the site goes live.</p>\n<ul><li>You need: the server address, a username and password.</li>\n<li>Plain FTP does not encrypt passwords, so secure versions such as <b>SFTP</b> or <b>FTPS</b> are preferred today.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> In upload/download questions, always say the <b>direction</b>: \"Uploading is sending a file from your computer to a server.\"</div>\n<p><button type=\"button\" class=\"btn ghost\" data-gx=\"opengame\" data-page=\"games/websim.html?level=2\" data-id=\"websim\">🌐 Try it now: publish your site via FTP in the Web Page Builder (Level 2)</button></p>","plain":"<p>An <b>ISP</b> is the company you pay to get online. <b>Downloading</b> is bringing something from the Internet onto your device; <b>uploading</b> is sending something from your device up to the Internet. <b>FTP</b> is a set of rules for moving files, often used to upload a website to its server.</p>","deeper":"<p>Why are plans <b>asymmetric</b>? Most home users mainly download (streaming, browsing), so ISPs give more capacity to the download direction. But today's creators, remote workers and students uploading video assignments need more upload, which is one reason fibre plans with symmetric speeds (e.g. 100/100) are popular. Watch the language trap: <b>streaming</b> a video is still downloading (data comes to you) even though you don't keep a copy. And when you <b>e-mail</b> a file you upload it to the mail server; the recipient then downloads it.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Upload or download?","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Decide which direction the data travels.</p>","main":"Is this uploading or downloading?","bins":["Upload (device → server)","Download (server → device)"],"items":[{"t":"Reading today's The Roseau Courier on its website.","bin":1,"fb":"Pages come down to your browser."},{"t":"A web designer sending index.html to the web server by FTP.","bin":0,"fb":"Publishing a site = uploading."},{"t":"Opening a PDF worksheet from Google Classroom.","bin":1,"fb":"The file comes down to you."},{"t":"Backing up your phone's photos to the cloud.","bin":0,"fb":"Photos go up to cloud servers."},{"t":"Installing a new game from the app store.","bin":1,"fb":"The game comes to your device."},{"t":"Submitting your SBA spreadsheet to Microsoft Teams.","bin":0,"fb":"Sent from you to the server."},{"t":"Posting a Carnival video to TikTok.","bin":0,"fb":"Your video goes up to the server."},{"t":"Streaming a cricket match on your phone.","bin":1,"fb":"Data comes down to you."}],"win":"Direction mastered: up to the server or down to you.","tutor":[{"h":"Follow the data","p":"<p>Where does the file start, and where does it end up?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Upload or download? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Posting a photo on Instagram.","bin":0,"fb":"Upload."},{"t":"Watching a video.","bin":1,"fb":"Download."},{"t":"Sending homework to Teams.","bin":0,"fb":"Upload."},{"t":"Saving a PDF from a website.","bin":1,"fb":"Download."}]},"up":{"title":"Upload or download? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","bins":["Mainly upload","Mainly download","Both, significantly"],"items":[{"t":"A two-way video call on Teams.","bin":2,"fb":"Your video goes up while theirs comes down."},{"t":"Syncing a shared cloud folder that you and classmates edit.","bin":2,"fb":"Changes flow both ways."},{"t":"Playing an online multiplayer game.","bin":2,"fb":"Your moves go up; others' come down."},{"t":"A designer publishing a whole site via SFTP.","bin":0,"fb":"Files go to the server."},{"t":"Watching the live stream of J'ouvert.","bin":1,"fb":"The stream comes to you."},{"t":"Downloading a 2 GB game patch.","bin":1,"fb":"Comes to you."},{"t":"Live-streaming J'ouvert from your phone to followers.","bin":0,"fb":"You send the stream up."}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"Connecting to the Internet","html":"<p>There are two ways to connect to the Internet: <b>wired</b> and <b>wireless</b>. Either way, the connection comes from an ISP.</p>\n<table><tr><th>Wired methods</th><th>Wireless methods</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Dial-up</b>: the oldest method. The modem dials the ISP's number over the telephone line and connects after you enter a username and password. Very slow (voice band), and the phone line is busy while online.</td><td><b>Wi-Fi</b>: high-speed wireless connection to a router or hotspot, found in most devices.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>DSL (Digital Subscriber Line)</b>: high-speed broadband over ordinary telephone lines using a DSL modem; you can use the phone and Internet at the same time.</td><td><b>WiMAX (Worldwide Interoperability for Microwave Access)</b>: a wireless broadband technology designed to deliver commercial-grade voice, video streaming and data over long distances. Today it has largely been replaced by 4G/5G.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Cable</b>: broadband over the coaxial cable-TV line using a cable modem.</td><td><b>Mobile broadband (4G/5G)</b>: through a phone, a hotspot device or a 4G/5G router.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Fibre</b>: today's fastest home connection, using an ONT.</td><td><b>Satellite</b>: for remote places, ships and disaster recovery.</td></tr></table>\n<p><b>Wireless broadband</b> provides high-speed Internet access without cables, so people can get online on the move or where cables are impractical. Devices that use it include smartphones, tablets, laptops with mobile broadband, and 4G/5G home routers.</p>\n<h4>Advantages and disadvantages of the Internet</h4>\n<table><tr><th>Advantages</th><th>Disadvantages</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>E-mail</b>: messages delivered almost instantly anywhere in the world, unlike traditional post.</td><td><b>Personal information</b> such as your name and address can be accessed by others; card details used for online shopping can be stolen, like handing someone a blank cheque.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Access to information</b>: information on almost any topic, found quickly with search engines.</td><td><b>Inappropriate and harmful content</b>, including pornography, is easy to find, a serious risk for children; parental controls and filters help.</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> \"Who owns the Internet?\" Nobody. No single person, company or government owns it.</div>","plain":"<p>You can connect to the Internet by wire (old dial-up, DSL on the phone line, cable, fibre) or wirelessly (Wi-Fi, WiMAX, 4G/5G, satellite). The Internet is great for fast e-mail and finding information, but your personal details can be stolen and there is harmful content online.</p>","deeper":"<p>Why did dial-up disappear? It used the voice band of a telephone line (at most 56 kbps), tied up the phone line and needed a new connection each session. DSL uses higher frequencies on the same copper pair, so voice and data share the line and the connection is 'always on'. The same story repeated in wireless: WiMAX promised wide-area wireless broadband but lost out to LTE (4G), which mobile operators were already deploying. In an exam, choose the connection by the scenario: a remote fishing lodge with no cables → satellite or 4G; a Nassau office needing speed → fibre.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Abbreviations that matter","bloom":"Remember","intro":"<p>Match each abbreviation to its full name.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Wi-Fi","r":"Wireless Fidelity"},{"l":"HTML","r":"HyperText Markup Language"},{"l":"WWW","r":"World Wide Web"},{"l":"HTTP","r":"HyperText Transfer Protocol"},{"l":"WiMAX","r":"Worldwide Interoperability for Microwave Access"},{"l":"DSL","r":"Digital Subscriber Line"}],"win":"Spot on. Examiners often ask what an abbreviation stands for AND its purpose.","tutor":[{"h":"Break it up","p":"<p>HT = HyperText in both HTML and HTTP; ML = Markup Language; TP = Transfer Protocol.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Abbreviations that matter (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"WWW","r":"World Wide Web"},{"l":"HTML","r":"HyperText Markup Language"},{"l":"Wi-Fi","r":"Wireless Fidelity"}]},"up":{"title":"Abbreviations that matter (Challenge)","bloom":"Understand","intro":"<p>Match each abbreviation to its PURPOSE.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"HTML","r":"Language used to create and structure web pages"},{"l":"HTTP","r":"Rules for requesting and sending web pages"},{"l":"TCP","r":"Splits data into packets and reassembles them reliably"},{"l":"FTP","r":"Transfers files, e.g. uploading a website"},{"l":"WiMAX","r":"Long-range wireless broadband"},{"l":"DSL","r":"Broadband over ordinary telephone lines"},{"l":"IRC","r":"Real-time text chat via chat servers"}]}},{"type":"learn","title":"Search engines and searching well","html":"<p>A <b>search engine</b> is a website or service that helps you <b>find information on the Web</b> by searching an index of web pages, e.g. Google, Bing, DuckDuckGo.</p>\n<h4>How a search engine works</h4>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Crawling</b>: programs called <b>crawlers</b> (spiders or bots) visit web pages and follow their links to discover new pages.</li>\n<li><b>Indexing</b>: the words and information on each page are stored in a huge <b>index</b>, like the index at the back of a textbook.</li>\n<li><b>Ranking</b>: when you search, the engine looks up your keywords in the index and <b>ranks</b> the results by relevance, quality and other factors.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Search smarter</h4>\n<table><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Example</th><th>Effect</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Quotation marks</td><td>\"Hurricane Irma\" recovery</td><td>Finds the exact phrase</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Minus sign</td><td>jaguar -car</td><td>Excludes a word (the animal, not the car)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>site:</td><td>CSEC syllabus site:cxc.org</td><td>Searches only one website</td></tr>\n<tr><td>filetype:</td><td>network topologies filetype:pdf</td><td>Finds only that file type</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Specific keywords</td><td>Sint Maarten 5G rollout</td><td>Better than a vague long question</td></tr></table>\n<p>Watch out for <b>sponsored results</b> (adverts) at the top of the page, and remember that ranking first does not make a page accurate. Evaluate sources for authority, currency, accuracy, objectivity and coverage (Section 1).</p>","plain":"<p>A search engine like Google sends little programs around the Web to read pages, stores what it finds in a giant index, and then shows you the best matches for your words. Use quotation marks for exact phrases, a minus sign to leave words out, and remember the first result is not always the most reliable.</p>","deeper":"<p>Ranking factors include how well a page matches your words, how many reputable sites link to it, how recent and fast it is, and your location and language. This explains why a search for \"best beach\" gives different results in Philipsburg and in Port of Spain. It also explains <b>filter bubbles</b>: personalised results can show you more of what you already believe. Exam trap: the search engine does <b>not</b> search the live Web when you press Enter; it searches its <b>index</b>, so very new pages may not appear yet. A web <b>browser</b> displays pages; a <b>search engine</b> finds them.</p>"},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"learn","title":"Communicating on the Internet","html":"<table><tr><th>Technology</th><th>What it is</th><th>Example and key features</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>E-mail</b></td><td>The transmission of messages (notes typed on the keyboard or attached files) over communication networks to an address in the form <code>username@domain</code>; the electronic equivalent of the post office. A message can go to one or many people and stays in the recipient's mailbox until it is opened</td><td><code>orders@coralreefcafe.vc</code>. Can include attachments; CC (copy openly) and BCC (copy invisibly). Asynchronous: the receiver reads it later.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Blog</b></td><td>Short for <b>web log</b>: a discussion or personal website where an author (a <b>blogger</b>) posts informal, diary-style entries in <b>reverse date order</b>, newest first; readers can usually comment. The community of bloggers is the <b>blogosphere</b></td><td>A Vincentian chef's food blog; a school's sports blog</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Wiki</b></td><td>A website that <b>many users can edit</b> collaboratively</td><td>Wikipedia; a class wiki of revision notes. Content may be inaccurate if not checked.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Social media / social networking</b></td><td>Online platforms where users create profiles and <b>share content and interact</b></td><td>Instagram, TikTok, Facebook, X. Fast spread of information, including fake news.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>VoIP</b> (Voice over Internet Protocol)</td><td>Making <b>voice (and video) calls over the Internet</b> instead of the traditional telephone network</td><td>WhatsApp calls, Skype, Teams, Zoom. Needs an Internet connection, a microphone and speakers (add a webcam for video). Calls are usually free, excellent for talking to family abroad; quality depends on bandwidth.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Bulletin board</b></td><td>The electronic version of a noticeboard: users post messages, read others' posts and may upload or download files</td><td>A community board for lost-and-found after a storm</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Telnet</b></td><td>A terminal emulation protocol that lets a user log on to a remote computer and run programs on it; old and insecure (unencrypted)</td><td>Replaced today by SSH</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>E-commerce</b></td><td>Buying and selling goods and services electronically over the Internet</td><td>Ordering guavaberry jam from an online shop</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>E-learning</b></td><td>Studying or taking courses online, or using online resources to support normal classes</td><td>This e-course; Google Classroom</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Instant messaging / chat</b></td><td>Real-time text conversation; messages appear as soon as they are sent. <b>IRC (Internet Relay Chat)</b> is an open protocol for this: users run an IRC client that connects to chat servers (client–server), and messages are shared with everyone in the conversation at the same time</td><td>WhatsApp, Messenger</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Discussion forums / newsgroups</b></td><td>Online boards where members post messages on a topic and others reply; messages stay for later readers</td><td>A forum for Caribbean coders; a help forum for a game</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Vlog</b> (video blog)</td><td>A blog whose entries are videos, shared on video-hosting sites where viewers comment</td><td>A Vincentian creator's weekly vlog about island food spots</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Podcast</b></td><td>A series of audio (or video) files that people stream or download; the name combines \"iPod\" and \"broadcasting\", and new episodes are distributed using <b>web feeds</b></td><td>A school's podcast interviewing Carnival band leaders</td></tr></table>\n<h4>Choosing the right tool</h4>\n<ul><li>A formal job application with a CV attached: <b>e-mail</b>.</li>\n<li>Calling grandma in Curaçao for free: <b>VoIP</b>.</li>\n<li>A class building shared revision notes together: <b>wiki</b>.</li>\n<li>A bakery sharing daily specials and photos: <b>social media</b> or a <b>blog</b>.</li></ul>\n<p><button type=\"button\" class=\"btn ghost\" data-gx=\"opengame\" data-page=\"games/inbox.html\" data-id=\"inbox\">📧 Practise e-mail etiquette in Inbox Hero</button></p>","plain":"<b>E-mail</b> sends messages to an address like name@domain. A <b>blog</b> is an online diary with newest posts first. A <b>wiki</b> is a website anyone allowed can edit, like Wikipedia. <b>Social media</b> lets people share and interact. <b>VoIP</b> means making calls over the Internet, like WhatsApp calls.","deeper":"<p>Notice the difference between <b>synchronous</b> tools (VoIP calls, live chat: everyone at the same time) and <b>asynchronous</b> tools (e-mail, blogs, wikis: respond when convenient). This matters across time zones: a Vincentian business working with a partner in Europe may prefer e-mail for decisions and VoIP only for short meetings. Also, a wiki's strength (anyone can edit) is its weakness for reliability; that is why Wikipedia uses citations and edit histories, and why you should follow its sources rather than citing it directly in your SBA.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Cloud computing","html":"<p><b>Cloud computing</b> means <b>storing data and running software on remote servers accessed over the Internet</b>, instead of on your own computer's hard drive.</p>\n<figure class=\"fig\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 420 160\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Phone, laptop and school PC all reach the same files and apps stored on cloud servers in remote data centres over the Internet\">\n<path d=\"M150,70 a32,32 0 0 1 58,-22 a38,38 0 0 1 70,10 a28,28 0 0 1 4,56 h-128 a24,24 0 0 1 -4,-44z\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#7FB2FF\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"><rect x=\"20\" y=\"20\" width=\"60\" height=\"34\" rx=\"4\"/><rect x=\"20\" y=\"106\" width=\"60\" height=\"34\" rx=\"4\"/><rect x=\"346\" y=\"62\" width=\"60\" height=\"34\" rx=\"4\"/>\n<line x1=\"80\" y1=\"37\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"70\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><line x1=\"80\" y1=\"123\" x2=\"156\" y2=\"108\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/><line x1=\"346\" y1=\"79\" x2=\"290\" y2=\"85\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/></g>\n<g font-size=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"50\" y=\"41\">Phone</text><text x=\"50\" y=\"127\">School PC</text><text x=\"376\" y=\"83\">Laptop</text>\n<text x=\"215\" y=\"78\" font-weight=\"700\">THE CLOUD</text><text x=\"215\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"9\">servers in remote data centres:</text><text x=\"215\" y=\"104\" font-size=\"9\">files, apps, backups</text>\n<text x=\"210\" y=\"154\" font-size=\"9\">Same files from any device, anywhere with Internet access</text>\n</g></svg><figcaption>Cloud computing: your files and software live on someone else's servers, reached over the Internet.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Examples</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Cloud storage</b>: OneDrive, Google Drive, iCloud. Your SBA saved in OneDrive can be opened at school and at home.</li>\n<li><b>Cloud applications</b>: Microsoft 365 online, Google Docs, where several students can edit the same document at once.</li>\n<li><b>Streaming services</b> and web e-mail also run in the cloud.</li></ul>\n<p>Enrichment: cloud services are often grouped as <b>SaaS</b> (Software as a Service, e.g. Google Docs), <b>PaaS</b> (Platform as a Service, for developers) and <b>IaaS</b> (Infrastructure as a Service: renting virtual servers).</p>\n<table><tr><th>Advantages</th><th>Disadvantages</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Access from any device, anywhere with Internet</td><td><b>Needs an Internet connection</b>; slow or no access during outages</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Easy collaboration and sharing</td><td>Data is held by a third party: <b>privacy and security</b> concerns</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Automatic <b>off-site backup</b>: files survive if a laptop is lost, stolen or destroyed in a hurricane</td><td>Ongoing <b>subscription</b> costs; free space is limited</td></tr>\n<tr><td>No need to buy big storage or servers; scale up as needed</td><td>Less control: the provider may change prices or terms, or suffer an outage</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Software updates happen automatically</td><td>Large uploads and downloads use bandwidth and data</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"tip\"><b>Exam tip:</b> For a Caribbean business, link the cloud to <b>disaster recovery</b>: data stored in data centres overseas survives a hurricane that destroys the office, but the business still needs Internet access to reach it.</div>","plain":"<p>Cloud computing means your files and apps live on big computers somewhere else and you reach them over the Internet. Save your SBA to OneDrive and you can open it at school, at home or on your phone, and it is safe even if your laptop breaks. The catch: no Internet, no access.</p>","deeper":"<p>\"The cloud\" is just <b>someone else's computers</b> in large data centres. The key trade-off is <b>control vs convenience</b>. A Caribbean hotel moving its booking system to the cloud gains resilience against local disasters and avoids buying servers, but it now depends on its Internet link (so it should have a backup link, Unit 2) and must trust the provider with guests' personal data, which raises questions about which country's data-protection laws apply. A Grade One evaluation weighs these and often recommends a <b>hybrid</b>: cloud for backup and collaboration, with essential operations able to continue briefly offline.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Which web technology?","bloom":"Understand","intro":"<p>Match each technology to its description.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"E-mail","r":"Messages sent to an address like name@domain"},{"l":"Blog","r":"Posts in reverse date order, newest first"},{"l":"Wiki","r":"Website that many users can edit together"},{"l":"VoIP","r":"Voice calls carried over the Internet"},{"l":"FTP","r":"Protocol for transferring files, e.g. uploading a website"},{"l":"Cloud storage","r":"Files kept on remote servers, reached over the Internet"},{"l":"Social media","r":"Platforms for sharing content and interacting with others"}],"win":"All matched. You know the tools of the online world.","tutor":[{"h":"Look for the signature feature","p":"<p>Newest first = blog. Anyone edits = wiki. Calls = VoIP. Files moved = FTP. Files kept remotely = cloud.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which web technology? (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"E-mail","r":"Messages to name@domain"},{"l":"Wiki","r":"Website people edit together"},{"l":"VoIP","r":"Calls over the Internet"},{"l":"Cloud storage","r":"Files saved online"}]},"up":{"title":"Which web technology? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Match each scenario to the BEST technology.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"A class builds a shared, editable set of revision pages","r":"Wiki"},{"l":"A chef publishes weekly recipes that readers comment on","r":"Blog"},{"l":"A designer publishes a site's files to the host","r":"FTP/SFTP"},{"l":"A family in Antigua and Barbuda talks free to relatives in Toronto","r":"VoIP"},{"l":"A firm keeps off-site copies of records for hurricane season","r":"Cloud backup"},{"l":"A formal application with a CV attached","r":"E-mail"},{"l":"A bakery shares daily special photos with followers","r":"Social media"}]}},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Web technology in action","bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>For each situation, choose the correct term.</p>","items":[{"text":"Converts www.coralreefcafe.kn into 192.0.2.45.","options":["FTP","DNS","HTML"],"answer":1,"fb":"DNS translates names to IP addresses."},{"text":"The language used to write the café's home page.","options":["HTTP","HTML","ISP"],"answer":1,"fb":"HTML creates web pages."},{"text":"The company Mrs Bryson pays each month for home Internet access.","options":["Wiki","ISP","Web browser"],"answer":1,"fb":"ISP provides Internet access."},{"text":"The program Mrs Bryson uses to view web pages.","options":["Search engine","Web server","Web browser"],"answer":2,"fb":"A browser displays pages."},{"text":"The secure protocol shown by a padlock in the address bar.","options":["FTP","HTTPS","HTTP"],"answer":1,"fb":"HTTPS is encrypted."},{"text":"A unique number identifying a device on the Internet.","options":["URL","IP address","Domain name"],"answer":1,"fb":"IP address."},{"text":"Google Docs used by four students to edit their SBA write-up together.","options":["FTP","Blog","Cloud computing"],"answer":2,"fb":"Cloud application."}],"win":"Fluent in web-speak. Each technology has a precise job.","tutor":[{"h":"Ask: what is its job?","p":"<p>Translate names (DNS), write pages (HTML), transfer pages (HTTP/S), transfer files (FTP), provide access (ISP).</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Web technology in action (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Turns a website name into an IP address.","options":["DNS","HTML"],"answer":0,"fb":"DNS."},{"text":"Language for making web pages.","options":["HTML","ISP"],"answer":0,"fb":"HTML."},{"text":"Company that gives you Internet access.","options":["Wiki","ISP"],"answer":1,"fb":"ISP."}]},"up":{"title":"Web technology in action (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Each case tests a common confusion.</p>","items":[{"text":"A student's Internet works (WhatsApp calls are fine) but no websites load by name, though typing an IP address works. Most likely cause?","options":["Broken HTML","DNS failure","No ISP"],"answer":1,"fb":"Names aren't being translated: DNS."},{"text":"Which is NOT part of the World Wide Web, though it uses the Internet?","options":["A news web page","A WhatsApp voice call (VoIP)","A blog page"],"answer":1,"fb":"VoIP is an Internet service, not the Web."},{"text":"A site uses HTTPS. What does this guarantee?","options":["The site has no viruses","The data in transit is encrypted","The site is honest"],"answer":1,"fb":"HTTPS encrypts the connection only."},{"text":"A designer's site is finished on her laptop. What must she do so the world can see it?","options":["Email it to DNS","Upload the files to a web server (e.g. via SFTP)","Download it"],"answer":1,"fb":"Publishing = upload to a host."},{"text":"Why do search results differ between two students searching the same words?","options":["Browsers have different HTML","Personalisation by location and history","The Web changes every second for each person"],"answer":1,"fb":"Search engines personalise results."}]}},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Taking the guavaberry shop online","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<b>Miss Lake's Guavaberry Shop</b> on Harbour Drive sells guavaberry liqueur and jams to cruise visitors. She wants to sell online. You are her web adviser.","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>She asks: \"First, what do I need so people can find my shop at an easy web address?\"</p>","choices":[{"t":"Just buy a faster web browser.","to":"bad1","fb":"A browser views sites; it doesn't publish yours."},{"t":"Register a domain name, e.g. lakesguavaberry.ky, and pay for web hosting.","to":"n2","fb":"Right: a name people can type, and a server to hold the site."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Customers will enter card details to buy online. What must the site use?</p>","choices":[{"t":"HTTP, because it loads faster.","to":"bad2","fb":"HTTP sends data as plain text."},{"t":"HTTPS, so payment data is encrypted in transit.","to":"n3","fb":"Essential for any payment page."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Her nephew built the site on his laptop. How does it go live?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Upload the files to the hosting server using SFTP/FTP.","to":"n4","fb":"Publishing means uploading to the web server."},{"t":"Download the site from the Internet.","to":"bad3","fb":"Downloading brings files to you, not the server."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Hurricane season is coming. Where should she keep her order records and product photos?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Only on the shop's old desktop PC.","to":"bad4","fb":"One flood or theft and everything is lost."},{"t":"In cloud storage with automatic backup, plus a local copy.","to":"good","fb":"Off-site cloud backup survives local damage."}]},"good":{"text":"<b>Shop online and storm-ready!</b> Domain + hosting, HTTPS checkout, SFTP publishing and cloud backup. Orders are coming in from cruise visitors who went home wanting more guavaberry.","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<b>Wrong tool.</b> She needs a domain name and hosting so the site exists on a web server.","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<b>Insecure checkout.</b> Without HTTPS, card details can be intercepted, and browsers will warn customers away.","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<b>Wrong direction.</b> To publish, files are uploaded to the server.","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<b>Data at risk.</b> Keep an off-site (cloud) backup.","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Domain + host","p":"<p>A name to find it and a server to hold it.</p>"},{"h":"Secure, publish, back up","p":"<p>HTTPS for payments, upload to publish, cloud for resilience.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Taking the guavaberry shop online (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Help Miss Lake put her guavaberry shop online.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>What gives the shop an easy web address like lakesguavaberry.ky?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A printer","to":"bad1","fb":"Printers don't make web addresses."},{"t":"A domain name","to":"n2","fb":"Yes."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Which protocol should the payment page use?</p>","choices":[{"t":"HTTPS","to":"good","fb":"Yes: encrypted."},{"t":"HTTP","to":"bad2","fb":"Not encrypted."}]},"good":{"text":"<b>Great!</b> A domain name and a secure HTTPS checkout.","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<b>Not quite.</b> She needs a domain name.","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<b>Unsafe.</b> HTTP is not encrypted.","end":"bad"}}},"up":{"title":"Taking the guavaberry shop online (Challenge)","intro":"<p>Miss Lake's site is live. Now handle the tricky stuff.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Customers say the site loads slowly on phones. Product photos are 8 MB each. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Compress and resize the images for the web, keeping alt text.","to":"n2","fb":"Smaller files = faster downloads on mobile data."},{"t":"Tell customers to buy faster phones.","to":"bad1","fb":"The fix is in your control."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>A customer emails: 'I got a text to pay at lakes-guavaberry-orders.example with a padlock. Is it you?' What do you advise?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Yes, the padlock means it's safe.","to":"bad2","fb":"Scam sites use HTTPS too."},{"t":"No: our only domain is lakesguavaberry.ky. A padlock doesn't prove identity. Report it and warn customers on social media.","to":"n3","fb":"You understood HTTPS limits and look-alike domains."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>She wants to update the site herself without giving out the main server password. Best option?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Give her a separate account with limited rights and use SFTP (encrypted), not plain FTP.","to":"n4","fb":"Least privilege and encryption."},{"t":"Share the admin password over WhatsApp and use plain FTP.","to":"bad3","fb":"Plain FTP sends passwords unencrypted."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>The hosting company is overseas. During a hurricane, the island loses Internet for two days. What happens to the online shop?</p>","choices":[{"t":"The site stays online for the world (it's hosted overseas), but Miss Lake can't process orders until her connection returns, so she should plan a backup mobile/satellite link and an 'expect delays' notice.","to":"good","fb":"You separated where the site lives from where she works."},{"t":"The website disappears worldwide.","to":"bad4","fb":"The server is overseas; it's her access that is cut, not the site."}]},"good":{"text":"<b>Web expert!</b> Optimised images, a scam warning, least-privilege SFTP and a realistic continuity plan.","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<b>Customers leave.</b> Large images make pages slow on mobile.","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<b>Customer scammed.</b> Always verify the domain, not just the padlock.","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<b>Credentials exposed.</b> Use limited accounts and encrypted transfer.","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<b>Misunderstanding.</b> Overseas hosting keeps the site up; the local link is the weak point.","end":"bad"}}}},{"type":"mcq","title":"Internet or Web?","bloom":"Analyse","q":"<p>During a study session, Kayla says: \"When I make a WhatsApp video call to my cousin in Antigua and when I read the CXC website, I'm using the World Wide Web both times.\" Which response is MOST accurate?</p>","options":[{"t":"She is right: the Internet and the Web are the same thing.","fb":"They are different: network infrastructure vs a collection of linked pages."},{"t":"Only the video call uses the Internet; websites use HTML instead.","fb":"Websites are written in HTML but are delivered over the Internet."},{"t":"Both use the Internet, but only reading the website uses the Web; the video call is a VoIP service, not a web page.","fb":"Correct. The Web is one service on the Internet; VoIP is another.","ok":true},{"t":"Neither uses the Internet, because WhatsApp uses mobile data.","fb":"Mobile data is a way of connecting to the Internet."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Is it a web page?","p":"<p>If it's a page viewed in a browser via HTTP(S), it's the Web.</p>"},{"h":"Everything rides the Internet","p":"<p>Calls, e-mail and pages all travel over the Internet infrastructure.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Internet or Web? (Foundation)","q":"<p>Kayla makes a WhatsApp video call and later reads a website. Which one uses the World Wide Web?</p>","options":[{"t":"The video call","fb":"That is VoIP, an Internet service, not the Web."},{"t":"Neither","fb":"Reading a website is using the Web."},{"t":"Reading the website","fb":"Yes: web pages are the Web.","ok":true}]},"up":{"title":"Internet or Web? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"<p>A newspaper claims: \"Curaçao lost the World Wide Web for six hours when an undersea cable was damaged.\" Which statement BEST evaluates the headline?</p>","options":[{"t":"Inaccurate wording: the island lost (or had reduced) Internet connectivity, so all Internet services, including the Web, e-mail and VoIP, were affected; the Web itself was not lost worldwide.","fb":"Correct. The cable carries Internet traffic; the Web is just one service affected.","ok":true},{"t":"Inaccurate: undersea cables have nothing to do with the Internet.","fb":"Submarine cables are the main Internet links for islands."},{"t":"Accurate: undersea cables carry only web pages.","fb":"They carry all Internet traffic."},{"t":"Accurate: when the Web goes down, e-mail still works normally.","fb":"E-mail also needs the Internet connection."}]}},{"type":"write","title":"Structured question: Coral Reef Café goes online","bloom":"Analyse","genre":"Structured question: web technologies (15 marks)","stimulus":"<p><b>Coral Reef Café</b> in Orange Walk has a new website at <code>https://www.coralreefcafe.bz/menu/specials.html</code>. Customers can view the menu, order online and pay by card. The owner's nephew built the pages in HTML on his laptop and published them to a hosting company's web server. Staff keep recipes and order records in cloud storage. The café also posts daily specials on social media and makes free VoIP calls to suppliers in Miami.</p>","prompt":"<p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Distinguish</b> between the Internet and the World Wide Web. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>For the URL in the stimulus, <b>identify</b>: (i) the protocol, (ii) the top-level domain, (iii) the file name. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> the role of DNS when a customer types the café's address. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> why the order page must use HTTPS rather than HTTP. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> whether the nephew uploaded or downloaded the pages to publish them, and <b>name</b> a protocol he could use. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Discuss</b> ONE advantage and ONE disadvantage of the café storing its records in the cloud. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":110,"max":450,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> The Internet is ... whereas the World Wide Web is ...<br><b>(b)</b> (i) ... (ii) ... (iii) ...<br><b>(c)</b> DNS translates ... so that the browser ...<br><b>(d)</b> HTTPS encrypts ... so that ...<br><b>(e)</b> He ... the pages using ...<br><b>(f)</b> One advantage is ... because ... However, one disadvantage is ... which means ...</p>","checklist":["I used 'whereas' to contrast Internet (network infrastructure) with the Web (linked pages).","I identified https, .bz and specials.html exactly.","I explained DNS converts domain names to IP addresses.","I linked HTTPS to encryption of card details.","I said 'uploaded' and named FTP/SFTP.","I explained (not just listed) one advantage AND one disadvantage of the cloud, in the café's context."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Internet vs Web","marks":2,"look":["network.{0,30}network|infrastructure|global network|interconnected","web page|pages|hyperlink|http|browser|collection"],"min":2,"strong":"Clear distinction.","weak":"Internet = global network of networks (infrastructure); WWW = collection of linked web pages accessed over it."},{"criterion":"URL parts","marks":3,"look":["https","\\.?bz\\b","specials\\.html"],"min":3,"strong":"All three parts correct.","weak":"Protocol https; TLD .bz; file name specials.html."},{"criterion":"DNS","marks":2,"look":["\\bDNS\\b|domain name system","ip address|ip|number"],"min":2,"strong":"Role explained.","weak":"DNS translates the domain name into the server's IP address so the browser can find it."},{"criterion":"HTTPS","marks":2,"look":["encrypt|scrambl|secure","card|payment|password|intercept|personal|data"],"min":2,"strong":"Linked to encryption of payment data.","weak":"HTTPS encrypts data in transit, so card details cannot be read if intercepted."},{"criterion":"Upload and protocol","marks":2,"look":["upload","\\bs?ftps?\\b|file transfer protocol"],"min":2,"strong":"Correct direction and protocol.","weak":"Uploaded, using FTP (or SFTP)."},{"criterion":"Cloud discussion","marks":4,"look":["access.{0,40}(anywhere|any device|any ?where)|backup|back.?up|hurricane|collaborat|share","internet.{0,40}(connection|down|outage|needed|depend)|privacy|third.?party|subscription|cost|secur","because|so that|which means|this means","however|but|on the other hand|whereas"],"min":3,"strong":"Balanced, explained points.","weak":"Advantage (e.g. off-site backup survives a hurricane) and disadvantage (e.g. no access without Internet), each explained for the café."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> The Internet is the global network of interconnected computer networks: the cables, routers and connections that carry data. The World Wide Web is a collection of linked web pages and resources stored on web servers and accessed over the Internet using browsers and HTTP/HTTPS.</p><p><b>(b)</b> (i) https (ii) .bz (iii) specials.html</p><p><b>(c)</b> When a customer types www.coralreefcafe.bz, the browser asks a DNS server to translate that domain name into the IP address of the café's web server. The browser then uses the IP address to contact the server and request the page.</p><p><b>(d)</b> Customers enter card details on the order page. HTTPS encrypts the data travelling between the browser and the server, so if anyone intercepts it (for example on public Wi-Fi) they cannot read the card numbers. HTTP sends data as plain text, and browsers warn users it is 'Not secure'.</p><p><b>(e)</b> He uploaded the pages (sent them from his laptop to the web server), using FTP or, more securely, SFTP.</p><p><b>(f)</b> One advantage is that records in the cloud are backed up off-site, so if the café's computer is damaged or stolen, for example in a hurricane, the recipes and orders are not lost, and staff can reach them from any device. However, one disadvantage is that staff need a working Internet connection to access the records, so during an outage they could not see online orders, which means the café should keep a backup connection or offline copies.</p>","key":"<p>(a) 1 Internet as network of networks/infrastructure; 1 Web as linked pages accessed over the Internet. (b) 1 each: https; .bz; specials.html. (c) 1 translates domain name; 1 into IP address used to locate server. (d) 1 encryption; 1 protects card/personal data from interception. (e) 1 upload; 1 FTP/SFTP/FTPS. (f) 2 advantage (1 identified, 1 explained in context); 2 disadvantage (1 identified, 1 explained).</p>","teacherTip":"Accept www.coralreefcafe.bz or coralreefcafe.bz as the domain if asked, but for (b)(ii) only .bz is the TLD. In (f), a bare list ('access anywhere; needs Internet') earns 2 of 4.","easy":{"title":"Structured question: Coral Reef Café goes online (Foundation)","genre":"Structured question: web technologies (9 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>What is the World Wide Web? <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>From the URL, <b>write down</b>: (i) the protocol, (ii) the top-level domain, (iii) the file name. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>What does DNS do? <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>Why is HTTPS used for paying? <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li>Did the nephew upload or download the pages to publish them? <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li><b>State</b> ONE advantage of storing records in the cloud. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> ONE web technology the café uses to talk to suppliers. <i>(1 mark)</i></li></ol>","frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> The World Wide Web is ...<br><b>(b)</b> (i) ... (ii) ... (iii) ...<br><b>(c)</b> DNS changes ... into ...<br><b>(d)</b> HTTPS ...<br><b>(e)</b> He ...<br><b>(f)</b> One advantage is ...<br><b>(g)</b> ...</p>","min":30,"max":230,"checklist":["Web = linked web pages.","https, .bz, specials.html.","DNS: names to IP addresses.","HTTPS encrypts.","Upload.","One cloud advantage.","VoIP."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Web","marks":1,"look":["page|hyperlink|link"],"min":1,"strong":"Correct.","weak":"A collection of linked web pages on the Internet."},{"criterion":"URL parts","marks":3,"look":["https","\\.?bz\\b","specials\\.html"],"min":3,"strong":"All correct.","weak":"https, .bz, specials.html."},{"criterion":"DNS","marks":1,"look":["ip"],"min":1,"strong":"Correct.","weak":"Changes domain names into IP addresses."},{"criterion":"HTTPS","marks":1,"look":["encrypt|secure|scrambl"],"min":1,"strong":"Correct.","weak":"It encrypts the card details."},{"criterion":"Upload","marks":1,"look":["upload"],"min":1,"strong":"Correct.","weak":"Uploaded."},{"criterion":"Cloud advantage","marks":1,"look":["anywhere|any device|backup|back.?up|safe|share"],"min":1,"strong":"Correct.","weak":"Access anywhere / safe backup."},{"criterion":"VoIP","marks":1,"look":["voip|voice over|e-?mail"],"min":1,"strong":"Correct.","weak":"VoIP (or e-mail)."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> A collection of linked web pages that you view over the Internet with a browser.</p><p><b>(b)</b> (i) https (ii) .bz (iii) specials.html</p><p><b>(c)</b> DNS changes domain names into IP addresses.</p><p><b>(d)</b> HTTPS encrypts the card details so others cannot read them.</p><p><b>(e)</b> He uploaded them.</p><p><b>(f)</b> Staff can reach the records from any device, and they are backed up safely.</p><p><b>(g)</b> VoIP.</p>","key":"<p>(a) 1 a collection of linked web pages viewed over the Internet (with a browser). (b) 1 mark each: (i) https; (ii) .bz; (iii) specials.html. (c) 1 changes domain names into IP addresses. (d) 1 it encrypts the card details so others cannot read them. (e) 1 uploaded. (f) 1 any valid advantage: records reached from any device or place; safe off-site backup; easy sharing between staff. (g) 1 VoIP (accept e-mail).</p>","teacherTip":"In (b)(ii), only .bz is the top-level domain; 'coralreefcafe.bz' is the domain name and scores 0 for this part. In (a), 'the Internet' is not a definition of the Web: look for linked web pages. In (e), students often write 'downloaded' because the pages were on the nephew's laptop; publishing means sending the files up to the web server, so the answer is uploaded."},"up":{"title":"Structured question: Coral Reef Café goes online (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Structured question: web technologies with evaluation (20 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer all parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Distinguish</b> between the Internet and the World Wide Web, using TWO services from the stimulus as examples. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Label every part of the URL (protocol, subdomain, domain name, TLD, path, file name). <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> the sequence of events, including DNS and HTTP(S), from the moment a customer presses Enter until the page appears. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>A customer receives a text linking to <code>coralreefcafe.bz.orders-pay.example</code> with a padlock. <b>Explain</b> why the padlock does not prove the site is genuine, and how to tell. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> why SFTP is preferred to plain FTP for publishing. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Evaluate</b> the café's reliance on cloud storage and VoIP during hurricane season and <b>recommend</b> TWO measures. <i>(5 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":200,"max":650,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> ... whereas ... For example, the VoIP calls use ... but not ...<br><b>(c)</b> First ... then DNS ... next ... finally ...<br><b>(d)</b> A padlock only shows ... The real domain is ...<br><b>(f)</b> On one hand ... On the other hand ... I recommend ... because ...</p>","checklist":["Internet vs Web with two stimulus services (e.g. website = Web; VoIP = Internet but not Web).","All six URL parts labelled.","Ordered sequence with DNS, IP, request, response, render.","Padlock = encryption only; read the domain from the right.","SFTP encrypts credentials and files.","Balanced evaluation of cloud + VoIP with two justified measures."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Internet vs Web with examples","marks":3,"look":["network.{0,30}network|infrastructure|interconnected","web page|pages|hyperlink|browser","voip|e-?mail|social"],"min":3,"strong":"Distinction with examples.","weak":"Infrastructure vs linked pages; website = Web, VoIP = Internet service not Web."},{"criterion":"URL labelled","marks":3,"look":["https","www","coralreefcafe","\\.bz","/menu/?|menu","specials\\.html"],"min":5,"strong":"All parts labelled.","weak":"https / www / coralreefcafe(.bz) / .bz / /menu/ / specials.html."},{"criterion":"Sequence","marks":4,"look":["\\bDNS\\b","ip","request|get","html|render|display"],"min":4,"strong":"Full ordered sequence.","weak":"DNS lookup → IP → HTTPS request → server sends HTML → browser renders."},{"criterion":"Padlock and domain","marks":3,"look":["encrypt","domain|orders-pay|right|real","not prove|doesn.t prove|does not prove|anyone can|scam|fake|phish"],"min":3,"strong":"Clear explanation.","weak":"Padlock = encrypted connection only; the real domain is orders-pay.example (read from the right); scammers can get certificates."},{"criterion":"SFTP","marks":2,"look":["sftp|secure","encrypt|plain text|password|intercept"],"min":2,"strong":"Reason given.","weak":"SFTP encrypts login details and files; plain FTP sends them as plain text."},{"criterion":"Evaluation","marks":5,"look":["internet.{0,40}(outage|down|connection|depend|need)|cable|offline","backup|back.?up|off.?site|survive|hurricane","recommend|should|measure","because|so that|which","overall|on balance|therefore|conclu"],"min":4,"strong":"Balanced judgement with justified measures.","weak":"Weigh resilience of off-site data against dependence on connectivity; recommend e.g. backup mobile/satellite link and offline copies/phone fallback, each justified."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> The Internet is the global network of interconnected networks, whereas the WWW is a collection of linked web pages accessed over the Internet using HTTP(S) and browsers. The café's website is part of the Web. Its VoIP calls to Miami use the Internet but are not part of the Web, because no web pages are involved.</p><p><b>(b)</b> https = protocol; www = subdomain/host; coralreefcafe.bz = domain name; .bz = top-level domain; /menu/ = path (folder); specials.html = file name.</p><p><b>(c)</b> The browser reads the URL; it asks a DNS server to translate www.coralreefcafe.bz into the web server's IP address; it then sets up an encrypted HTTPS connection to that IP address and sends a request for /menu/specials.html; the server responds with the HTML file (and the browser requests the images and styles it references); finally the browser renders the page.</p><p><b>(d)</b> The padlock only shows the connection is encrypted and that the site controls the domain it is on; scammers can obtain certificates for their own domains. Reading the domain from the right, just before the first slash, the real domain is orders-pay.example, not coralreefcafe.bz, so it is a phishing site.</p><p><b>(e)</b> Plain FTP sends the username, password and files as plain text, so they could be intercepted; SFTP encrypts them.</p><p><b>(f)</b> Cloud storage is a strength in hurricane season: records are held off-site and survive damage to the café. But both the cloud and VoIP depend on the café's Internet connection; if an undersea cable or local line fails, staff cannot see orders or call suppliers. I recommend (1) a backup Internet link (mobile 4G/5G or satellite), so cloud access and VoIP continue if the main line fails, and (2) keeping offline copies of essential records and suppliers' ordinary phone numbers, so the café can operate during a total outage. Overall, cloud and VoIP are sensible for the café provided these fallbacks are in place.</p>","key":"<p>(a) 1 the Internet is the global network of interconnected networks (the infrastructure); 1 the Web is the collection of linked web pages accessed over the Internet with browsers and HTTP(S); 1 two stimulus services used correctly as examples (the website is part of the Web; VoIP calls or cloud storage use the Internet but are not the Web). (b) Six parts: https = protocol; www = subdomain; coralreefcafe(.bz) = domain name; .bz = TLD; /menu/ = path; specials.html = file name. All six correct = 3; four or five = 2; two or three = 1. (c) 1 DNS server asked to translate the domain name; 1 the IP address is returned and the browser connects to that server (encrypted HTTPS connection); 1 HTTPS request for /menu/specials.html; 1 server sends the HTML and the browser renders the page; marks need the correct order. (d) 1 the padlock only shows the connection is encrypted; 1 scammers can obtain certificates for their own domains; 1 reading from the right, the real domain is orders-pay.example, not coralreefcafe.bz. (e) 1 plain FTP sends the username, password and files as plain text that can be intercepted; 1 SFTP encrypts them. (f) 1 strength of cloud storage (records held off-site survive damage to the café); 1 weakness: cloud and VoIP both depend on the Internet connection; 1 mark each for two measures, each justified (backup 4G/5G or satellite link; offline copies of essential records and ordinary phone numbers; UPS); 1 overall judgement.</p>","teacherTip":"In (b), students often label 'coralreefcafe' as the TLD or leave out the path; use the bands in the key rather than marking all-or-nothing. In (c), order matters: the DNS lookup must come before the HTTPS request, and the browser renders only after the server sends the HTML. In (d), the strongest answers read the domain from the right and name orders-pay.example as the real domain; 'the link looks strange' scores 0."}}]}]});
